window.__DIYDB__=window.__DIYDB__||{data:{}};window.__DIYDB__.data["power-wagon"]=[{"id":"pw-front-bumper-aluminum","title":"Aluminum / Low-Profile Front Bumper","vehicle":"power-wagon","category":"armor","subcategory":"bumpers","type":"part","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":5},"cost_usd":{"low":1100,"high":2600},"tools_required":["socket set","drill","trim tool","torque wrench","engine hoist or helper","shipping scale"],"skills_required":["wrenching"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2005-2009","2010-2013","2014-2018","2019-2024"],"tags":["armor","bumper","winch"],"parts":[{"name":"Aluminum front bumper (Chassis Unlimited Octane class)","vendor":"Chassis Unlimited","price_estimate_usd":1900},{"name":"Low-profile steel front bumper (ADD Stealth Fighter class)","vendor":"Addictive Desert Designs","price_estimate_usd":1650},{"name":"Winch re-mount plate + grade-8 hardware kit","vendor":"various","price_estimate_usd":200},{"name":"Hawse fairlead (synthetic line) or roller fairlead (steel cable)","vendor":"Warn / Factor 55","price_estimate_usd":90}],"citations":[{"title":"Chassis Unlimited \u2014 Ram 2500 Power Wagon bumpers","url":"https://chassisunlimited.com/collections/powerwagon"},{"title":"FN Tuned \u2014 best Ram 2500 Power Wagon mods","url":"https://www.fntuned.com/blogs/apex/best-ram-2500-power-wagon-mods"},{"title":"Ram Trucks \u2014 Power Wagon specifications and equipment","url":"https://www.ramtrucks.com/power-wagon.html"}],"alternatives":["pw-front-bumper","pw-winch-upgrade"],"summary":"The full steel bumper in the companion guide is the right call for maximum protection. This aluminum or low-profile alternative is for the Power Wagon owner who is already fighting a heavy front end and does not want to add another hundred-plus pounds over the front axle. On a truck this size, weight management is its own kind of capability \u2014 and the front axle is where you have the least headroom to spend.","description":"Start with the number that actually constrains you: the Power Wagon's **front GAWR is 6,000 lb**, and a 2019-2024 truck's **GVWR is 8,510 lb** against a curb weight in the 7,000 lb range. A stock truck already carries roughly **4,600-5,000 lb on the front axle** with a driver aboard, because the 6.4L Hemi, the transfer case, the factory winch, and a solid front axle all live ahead of the firewall. That leaves on the order of **1,000-1,400 lb of front-axle headroom** \u2014 and every pound of it is also payload you are spending out of a total that is smaller than most owners assume.\n\nA note on the engine, because it changes the airflow math and people get it wrong: **the Power Wagon has never been offered with a Cummins diesel.** 2005-2009 and 2010-2013 trucks are 5.7L Hemi; 2014 and later are 6.4L Hemi. It is a gasoline truck. That matters here because a gas Hemi has a smaller, less demanding cooling stack than a Cummins, so you have somewhat more freedom in bumper face design than the diesel Ram HD crowd does \u2014 but a 6.4 towing in summer heat still wants air.\n\nNow the bumper weights. Published figures vary by configuration, so weigh yours before and after if you want the exact delta, but the tiers land here:\n\n| Bumper type | Typical bare weight | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| Factory Power Wagon steel bumper (winch tray excluded) | ~110-140 lb | OEM |\n| Full-plate steel aftermarket | ~200-280 lb | Fab Fours Vengeance, Road Armor Stealth |\n| Low-profile steel | ~130-170 lb | ADD Stealth Fighter, Chassis Unlimited Attitude |\n| Aluminum | ~85-120 lb | Chassis Unlimited Octane |\n\nSwapping a factory bumper for full-plate steel is commonly a **+80 to +150 lb** change over the front axle. Going aluminum instead is roughly **break-even to -40 lb** versus stock, and **120-190 lb lighter** than the plate-steel route. That is the difference between needing stiffer front springs to hold ride height and not needing them \u2014 see the [heavy-truck weight reality guide](/db/power-wagon/hd-3-4-ton-weight-reality/) for how quickly a 3/4-ton eats its own payload.\n\nThe Power Wagon ships with a **Warn 12,000 lb winch** integrated behind the front bumper, and preserving it is most of the reason to buy a Power Wagon-specific bumper rather than a generic Ram 2500 unit. Before ordering, confirm three things with the manufacturer for your exact model year:\n\n1. **The winch mount pattern and depth.** The factory winch bolts to a tray that ties into the frame horns. A bumper designed for an aftermarket winch may not accept the factory unit's footprint or its motor housing depth.\n2. **The fairlead type.** 2019+ trucks use **synthetic line**, which wants a **hawse fairlead**; earlier trucks ran **steel cable**, which requires a **roller fairlead**. Running synthetic through a roller chafes the line; running cable through a hawse damages both. Match the fairlead to the line, not to the bumper's default.\n3. **Clutch lever and control access.** The free-spool lever has to be reachable with the bumper installed. Some low-profile designs bury it.\n\nThen check the electronics. On 2019+ trucks the **forward-facing camera and any adaptive cruise radar are grille-mounted**, so a bumper swap usually leaves them alone \u2014 but **front park-assist sensors on higher trims live in the bumper fascia**, and if your truck has them you need a bumper with sensor provisions or you will set a permanent fault and lose the feature. Verify against your build sheet rather than a photo of someone else's truck.\n\nFinally, leave the face open. The lower grille opening feeds the radiator, transmission cooler, and A/C condenser stack. A solid plate face across the bottom on a truck that tows in summer is how you find out what the 6.4's cooling margin actually is.\n\n**Aluminum (6061-class):** the biggest weight savings, typically **$1,700-2,300**. It takes a moderate hit fine and shrugs off brush and salt, but it **deforms rather than springs back** on a hard rock strike, and repairing it requires a shop with a TIG welder and aluminum experience \u2014 which is not every rural welding shop. Powder coat holds well; scratches do not rust.\n\n**Low-profile steel:** roughly **$1,400-1,900**, splitting the difference at **130-170 lb**. It takes repeat abuse, and any competent shop can straighten or re-weld it on a Tuesday. On a salt-belt truck it needs the coating maintained or it will rust from the inside of the tube out.\n\n**Full-plate steel:** **$1,500-2,600** and **200-280 lb**. This is the correct answer if the truck's nose regularly meets rock, stumps, or wildlife at speed. It is the wrong answer if the truck's real job is carrying a slide-in camper and 400 lb of gear to a dispersed campsite.\n\nThe honest decision rule: **buy protection proportional to what actually hits the bumper.** A Power Wagon that runs forest roads, tows, and carries an overland load is far more likely to be hurt by 150 lb of unnecessary nose weight \u2014 sagging ride height, longer stopping distances, more front tire wear, less payload \u2014 than by the impact the plate bumper was designed to survive.\n\nBudget **2-5 hours** and do not do it alone. Practical sequence:\n\n1. **Weigh the truck first** if you want a real number \u2014 a CAT scale front-axle pass before and after costs about $15 and settles the argument.\n2. **Disconnect the battery** before touching winch power leads. The factory winch feeds straight off the battery through heavy unfused cable at the winch end; shorting it against the frame is a fire, not a spark.\n3. **Support the bumper on a jack or hoist** before removing the last frame bolts. A plate-steel bumper coming down on a hand is a broken hand; even aluminum is awkward at 100 lb.\n4. **Transfer the winch on the bench**, not in mid-air, and re-terminate the leads with fresh heat-shrinked lugs if the old ones look green.\n5. **Torque frame bolts to the manufacturer's spec** \u2014 typically **grade 8 hardware in the 110-150 ft-lb range** for the main frame-horn bolts, but follow the supplied sheet, not this number. **Re-torque at 100 miles.** Bumper mounts loosen on the first few heat cycles and rough miles.\n6. **Re-verify recovery point ratings.** The factory bumper's shackle tabs are rated; an aftermarket bumper's tabs are only as good as its frame attachment. Read the [factory recovery points guide](/db/power-wagon/recovery-points-and-front-bumper/) before you rig anything to a new bumper.\n\nCommon mistakes: ordering a Ram 2500 bumper that does not clear the Power Wagon's winch; mismatching the fairlead type; blocking the lower grille; and skipping the front ride-height re-check afterward. If the nose settles more than about half an inch, your springs were already at their limit \u2014 address that with the suspension rather than living with it. The [premium reservoir shock guide](/db/power-wagon/pw-shocks-premium/) covers matched shock-and-spring systems for exactly this case.\n\n| Line item | Typical cost |\n|---|---|\n| Aluminum bumper (Chassis Unlimited Octane class) | $1,700-2,300 |\n| Low-profile steel bumper (ADD Stealth Fighter class) | $1,400-1,900 |\n| Full-plate steel bumper (for comparison) | $1,500-2,600 |\n| Winch re-mount plate + grade-8 hardware | $120-250 |\n| Correct fairlead (hawse or roller) | $60-140 |\n| Sensor relocation kit, if your trim has bumper sensors | $60-150 |\n| Shop install, if you would rather not | $250-450 |\n| **Realistic all-in, aluminum** | **$1,900-2,700** |\n\nBuy from a vendor that publishes a **bare bumper weight and a Power Wagon-specific fitment note by model year**. A listing that says \"Ram 2500/3500 2019+\" without mentioning the factory winch has not been engineered around your truck, whatever the photos suggest.\n\nChoose an aluminum or low-profile bumper to keep a heavy Power Wagon from getting nose-heavier while keeping the factory 12,000 lb Warn winch. Confirm winch pattern, fairlead type, and sensor fitment for your year; keep the lower grille open for the 6.4 Hemi's cooling stack; re-torque at 100 miles; and step up to full plate steel only when impact protection genuinely outranks the 120-190 lb it costs. On this truck, saved pounds up front are real capability."},{"id":"pw-front-bumper","title":"Aftermarket Front Bumper Upgrade for the Power Wagon","vehicle":"power-wagon","category":"armor","subcategory":"bumpers","type":"part","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":4},"cost_usd":{"low":699,"high":1499},"tools_required":["socket set","torque wrench","floor jack for weight support","electrical connectors","wire harness pliers","trim tool"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","electrical connectors"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2014-2018","2019-2024"],"tags":["armor","bumper","winch","protection"],"parts":[{"name":"Ranch Hand Summit Series Front Bumper for Ram 2500","vendor":"Ranch Hand","price_estimate_usd":899},{"name":"Fab Four Full-Width Front Bumper for Ram 2500","vendor":"Fab Four","price_estimate_usd":1299},{"name":"Go Rhino Traverse Front Bumper for Ram 2500","vendor":"Go Rhino","price_estimate_usd":799},{"name":"ICI Magnum Front Bumper for Ram 2500 Power Wagon","vendor":"Innovative Creations Inc.","price_estimate_usd":699}],"citations":[{"title":"Ranch Hand Ram 2500 Summit Bumper Install","url":"https://www.ranchhand.com/ram-2500-power-wagon-bumpers"},{"title":"Fab Four Ram 2500 Winch Bumper Product Page","url":"https://www.fab-four.com/ram-2500"},{"title":"WARN Industries \u2014 the Ram Power Wagon's factory winch","url":"https://www.warn.com/blogs/articles/the-ram-power-wagons-warn-winch"}],"alternatives":["pw-front-bumper-aluminum","pw-skid-plates","pw-sliders","pw-recovery-points-install"],"summary":"This is the steel full-replacement bumper guide. The Power Wagon already ships with a bumper that does more than most trucks get from the factory \u2014 including rated tow hooks and a frame-mounted 12,000 lb WARN winch \u2014 so an aftermarket steel bumper here buys brush and impact protection, better shackle geometry, and a look. It does not fill a capability gap. If weight is your constraint, read the [aluminum and low-profile bumper guide](/db/power-wagon/pw-front-bumper-aluminum/) instead; that is the same decision made from the other end.","description":"The factory Power Wagon front end is genuinely capable. It includes tow hooks intended for recovery use, and the WARN 12,000 lb winch sits behind the fascia on a dedicated frame-mounted plate rather than hanging off the bumper itself. That distinction matters: on this truck the winch mount is not what you are upgrading. What you are buying is steel where the factory used composite, a push surface for brush, and shackle mounts positioned where a strap can actually reach them.\n\nWhere the upgrade earns its money: welded push-bar protection for driving through vegetation without marking the fascia, heavy steel in place of the factory's lower composite sections that crack on contact, and D-ring or shackle integration that beats reaching under the truck for a factory tow hook. Where it does not: if you stay on forest roads and established trails, the factory bumper handles that assignment and the $900 is better spent on tires or recovery gear.\n\nRanch Hand's Summit ($899) is a proven full-replacement steel unit with an integrated grille guard \u2014 the choice for brush country and deer-strike territory, and the heaviest option in this group. Fab Four's full-width unit ($1,299) suits a trail-focused build where approach angle and shackle placement matter more than a grille guard. Go Rhino Traverse ($799) and ICI Magnum ($699) are clean, lower-priced installs without full guards: the right call if you want the steel presence and the shackle mounts without the guard silhouette or the extra 60 pounds.\n\nTwo questions decide it faster than the spec sheets. First, does the design cut up into the fascia to improve approach angle, or does it hang flat and full-width? Full-width looks right on a truck this size but is the first thing to catch on a steep entry. Second, are the shackle mounts welded into the frame-tie structure or bolted to the outer skin? Only the former is a recovery point. See the [recovery points install guide](/db/power-wagon/pw-recovery-points-install/) for the distinction that matters when a strap is under load.\n\nThis is the step people get wrong, and it is specific to this truck.\n\nThe Power Wagon's WARN unit is built to OE spec and is closely related to the M12000, but it is not a drop-in copy of a retail winch. The drum rotates in the opposite direction from a standard aftermarket unit, and the control box carries a low-voltage interrupt that stops the winch if system voltage falls too far. Trucks from roughly 2017 onward ship with synthetic line and a hawse fairlead; earlier trucks carried about 90 feet of 7/16-inch wire rope with a roller fairlead. A bumper designed around a roller fairlead opening will look wrong and fit worse with a hawse plate, and vice versa \u2014 confirm which line and fairlead your truck has before ordering, not after.\n\nMost aftermarket bumpers for this platform are cut to accept the factory winch's footprint and the factory frame plate. Confirm three things with the vendor in writing: fairlead type and bolt pattern, whether the factory control box relocates or stays, and whether the clutch (free-spool) lever remains reachable with the bumper installed. A bumper that buries the free-spool lever turns a 20-second line pull into a knuckle-scraping exercise every single time.\n\nA steel bumper adds roughly 100-200 pounds ahead of the front axle, and on a coil-sprung solid front axle that weight lands almost entirely on the front coils. Expect the front to settle a half inch to an inch, which quietly undoes part of a leveling kit and changes headlight aim \u2014 re-aim the lights after install, because a nose-down truck with a steel bumper puts the beam in oncoming drivers' eyes.\n\nThe rest of the effects are smaller but real. Front coil rate that was matched to the factory bumper is now slightly under-sprung, so if you are also planning suspension work, tell the vendor the bumper weight and buy the heavier front coil option rather than adding a spacer later. Parking sensor and adaptive cruise behavior can change if the sensor faces are recessed behind steel \u2014 verify function before you rely on it. And the payload arithmetic applies: on a truck rated for roughly 1,400-1,630 pounds of payload, a bumper, winch line upgrade, and skid plates spend a visible chunk of it. The [weight and payload guide](/db/power-wagon/hd-3-4-ton-weight-reality/) has the full accounting.\n\n- ICI Magnum \u2014 $699-$799, roughly 110 lb added. Lowest-cost steel, no grille guard.\n- Go Rhino Traverse \u2014 $799-$949, roughly 120 lb. Clean look with shackle mounts, no guard.\n- Ranch Hand Summit \u2014 $899-$1,099, roughly 180 lb. The pick for brush and animal-strike country.\n- Fab Four full-width \u2014 $1,299-$1,499, roughly 150 lb. Trail-focused geometry with heavy shackle tabs.\n- Aluminum or low-profile \u2014 $1,100-$2,600, roughly 60-90 lb. For weight-critical overland builds.\n\nAdd $150-$400 in shop labor if you are not doing the install, more if the harness needs extending. Budget for touch-up paint on the frame horns \u2014 the factory bumper hides a lot of bare metal that is about to be exposed to weather.","steps":["Disconnect the negative battery terminal. The bumper area carries fog light wiring and, on many trims, front parking sensor and camera connections.","Disconnect and label every factory connector in the bumper \u2014 fog lights, park sensors, camera, any heated washer element.","Remove the factory tow hook bolts and set the hooks aside. Most aftermarket bumpers provide their own recovery point positions.","Unbolt the factory bumper from the frame brackets. It is heavy \u2014 put a floor jack under it or use a helper. Dropping it is how people crack a fascia they were planning to sell.","Note the winch line routing and free-spool clutch position before anything moves, then unbolt the fairlead.","Position the new bumper on the frame. The factory winch plate may need shims or repositioning per the new bumper's instructions; do this before torquing anything.","Hand-thread all mounting hardware, square the bumper to the fenders with a tape measure on both sides, then torque to spec.","Reconnect fog lights and sensors into the new bumper's provisions. Verify every factory harness reaches without stretch \u2014 extend with proper sealed connectors rather than pulling a harness tight.","Reconnect the battery. Test fog lights, park sensors, camera, and a short winch in-and-out under no load. Re-torque the bumper bolts after the first 100 miles."],"pros":["Heavy steel handles brush and minor trail impact without the factory composite sections cracking","Grille guard options provide real push protection in vegetation and animal-strike country","Factory WARN winch transfers to most aftermarket bumpers without modification","Welded shackle tabs give a usable recovery point far more accessible than the factory tow hook position"],"cons":["The factory bumper is genuinely capable \u2014 this is an upgrade, not a repair of a deficiency","100-200 pounds ahead of the axle settles the front end, changes headlight aim, and spends payload","Fog light, park sensor, and camera compatibility must be verified by trim and year before ordering","Fairlead type changed mid-generation (wire rope to synthetic); ordering for the wrong one is a common and avoidable mistake"]},{"id":"pw-rear-bumper-tire-carrier","title":"Rear Bumper with Tire Carrier for Power Wagon (Carli / AEV / Expedition One)","vehicle":"power-wagon","category":"armor","subcategory":"bumpers","type":"part","difficulty":4,"time_hours":{"low":6,"high":14},"cost_usd":{"low":1800,"high":4200},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","metric sockets through 22mm","torque wrench (250 lb-ft)","drill","extra hands for the carrier-side lift"],"skills_required":["heavy lifting","patience aligning the swing-out hinge"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2014-2018","2019-2024","2025+"],"tags":["bumper","tire-carrier","armor","rear-bumper","overland"],"parts":[{"name":"AEV Rear Bumper with Tire Carrier (Power Wagon-specific)","part_number":"AEV-30210010AA","vendor":"AEV","price_estimate_usd":2800},{"name":"Carli Suspension Rear Bumper + Tire Carrier","vendor":"Carli Suspension","price_estimate_usd":3400},{"name":"Expedition One Trail Series Rear Bumper","vendor":"Expedition One","price_estimate_usd":2200},{"name":"Iron Cross HD Rear Bumper (no carrier \u2014 budget option)","vendor":"Iron Cross","price_estimate_usd":1100}],"alternatives":["pw-front-bumper","pw-tires-mt"],"citations":[{"title":"AEV Ram 2500/3500 rear bumper \u2014 fitment and load specs","url":"https://www.aev-conversions.com/"},{"title":"Expedition One Ram HD bumper product line","url":"https://expeditionone.us/"},{"title":"Carli Suspension \u2014 Ram 2500/Power Wagon armor and geometry notes","url":"https://carlisuspension.com/"}],"summary":"Once you're on 37s, the factory rear bumper has nowhere for the spare to live \u2014 you can't fit a 37 under the bed cradle, and you're not driving without a spare. A rear bumper with a swing-out tire carrier solves that. AEV is the proven choice. Carli is the premium tier. Expedition One is the value pick.","description":"The Power Wagon's factory rear bumper is fine for daily use but has two limits for an off-road build:\n\n1. **The factory spare cradle under the bed won't fit a 37\" tire.** Period. The bed-mount kit some shops offer is a workaround but eats most of the bed.\n2. **The factory bumper is plastic-capped steel** \u2014 fine for parking lots, useless if you need to back up to a steep approach without dragging the bumper.\n\nA purpose-built rear bumper with a swing-out tire carrier solves both. The three serious options:\n\n**AEV Rear Bumper with Tire Carrier** ($2,800) \u2014 the reference. Heavy-gauge steel, integrated D-rings, swing-out tire carrier that supports 37s with two jerry can / utility brackets on the carrier face. Mounts to factory bumper bolts plus 4 additional frame bolts. Hi-lift compatible. The proven Power Wagon choice.\n\n**Carli Suspension Rear Bumper** ($3,400) \u2014 premium. Same overall design philosophy as AEV but with proprietary geometry that matches their lift kits. Higher cost reflects build quality and powder coat. The pick if you're already running a Carli lift.\n\n**Expedition One Trail Series** ($2,200) \u2014 value pick. Slightly less rigid swing-out hinge than AEV, but functionally equivalent. Good powder coat. 80% of the AEV's quality at 80% of the price.\n\n**Iron Cross HD (no carrier)** ($1,100) \u2014 if you're willing to keep your spare in the bed, a rear bumper without a tire carrier is half the price. Loses the tire-carrier benefit but gains weight reduction and more straightforward install. Bad choice if you wheel hard (spare in the bed bounces).\n\nInstall: this is a 2-person job. The rear bumper assembly with carrier weighs 180-220 lb. Plan on 6-14 hours. Stage the bumper on a furniture dolly to wheel under the truck, then jack up incrementally. The 4 frame bolts often need drilling \u2014 most kits include the drill bit and template.\n\nHonest framing: a rear bumper with carrier is $2-3k of armor that you mostly only need if you're running 37s. If you're on 35s with a bed-mount spare cradle that works, skip this until you actually go 37s.\n\nBudget a full day and a second set of hands. The assembly with the carrier arm fitted weighs 180-220 lb, and you are working it into position under the rear of a 6,900 lb truck.\n\n1. Disconnect the factory wiring \u2014 license plate light, reverse sensors, and any trailer harness routed through the factory bumper. Label the connectors. The AEV and Carli kits include a relocation bracket for the sensors; the Iron Cross does not.\n2. Remove the factory bumper. It's four to six bolts depending on year, plus the plastic step cap. On 2014-2018 trucks the bolts are often rust-welded \u2014 soak them the night before and use a six-point socket so you don't round the heads.\n3. Stage the new bumper on a furniture dolly or floor jack with a wide board across it. Roll it under, then raise it incrementally rather than trying to hold it at height.\n4. Start every frame bolt by hand before torquing any of them. Most kits add four through-frame bolts beyond the factory locations; the supplied template and drill bit are for those. Drill slow, deburr, and add a dab of paint to the bare metal so the hole doesn't rust.\n5. Torque the frame bolts to the kit spec (typically 110-150 lb-ft) in a cross pattern. Re-check after your first shakedown drive \u2014 a 200 lb bumper settles on its mounts.\n6. Reconnect and test the lights and sensors before you button up the carrier. Hang and adjust the swing-out arm last, then set the latch tension so it doesn't rattle at speed.\n\nFor the front-axle counterpart and matching D-ring strategy, see [[pw-front-bumper]]. The tire size that forces this whole decision is covered in [[pw-tires-mt]].\n\nThe swing-out hinge is the part that fails owners who skip maintenance. It carries a 37\" tire and wheel \u2014 130+ lb cantilevered off one pivot \u2014 over washboard. Grease the spindle at every oil change and check the latch for play; a loose latch lets the arm slam the body mount and crack a tail light or dent the bedside.\n\nWatch your rear payload math. Adding 200 lb of bumper plus a 130 lb tire-and-carrier puts 330+ lb behind the rear axle, which lightens the front and eats into the Power Wagon's already-modest payload rating. If you also run a rooftop tent or full water tank, you can quietly exceed the rear axle's GAWR. Weigh the loaded truck before a long trip.\n\nFinally, confirm reverse-sensor relocation actually works before you trust it \u2014 a tire carrier sits exactly where the factory sensors expect open air, and a carrier that blocks them gives you constant false alarms or, worse, a dead zone you stop trusting.","pros":["Carries 37s \u2014 the only way to keep a real spare with this tire size","AEV/Carli integrated D-rings = recovery points + bumper in one part","Hi-lift jacking surface","Massive rear approach-angle improvement vs. factory plastic bumper"],"cons":["180-220 lb of added weight at the rear axle \u2014 affects ride and payload","$2-3k for the bumper, more if you add jerry can mounts and lights","Install requires drilling frame bolts on most kits","Swing-out hinge needs occasional grease/inspection"]},{"id":"pw-skid-plates","title":"Skid Plate Upgrade for the Power Wagon \u2014 Extending Factory Coverage","vehicle":"power-wagon","category":"armor","type":"kit","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":3},"cost_usd":{"low":399,"high":899},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","socket set","torque wrench"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","under-vehicle access"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["2016-2024"],"tags":["armor","skid-plate","underbody","protection"],"parts":[{"name":"DPR Offroad Full Skid Plate System for Power Wagon","vendor":"DPR Offroad","price_estimate_usd":549},{"name":"AltRider Power Wagon Skid Plate Kit","vendor":"AltRider","price_estimate_usd":699},{"name":"Mopar Partial Skid Plate Upgrade for Power Wagon","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":399}],"citations":[{"title":"DPR Offroad Power Wagon Skid Install Guide","url":"https://www.dproffroad.com/power-wagon-skid-plates"},{"title":"AltRider Power Wagon Skid Plate Product Page","url":"https://www.altrider.com/power-wagon"}],"alternatives":["pw-sliders","pw-front-bumper"],"summary":"The Power Wagon ships with a transfer case skid from the factory \u2014 aftermarket kits extend that coverage forward to the oil pan and transmission, and rearward to the fuel tank, filling the gaps that real trails exploit.","description":"The factory transfer case skid is a meaningful starting point; most Ram trucks in this segment don't include one at all. But the Power Wagon's underbody still has exposed areas that matter in the field: the front differential and oil pan have limited protection, the transmission skid varies by trim year, and the fuel tank is exposed on harder terrain. An aftermarket skid kit extends the protected surface area and, on most kits, adds heavier plate gauge than the factory steel.\n\nCoverage priority for the Power Wagon: the front differential is the highest-risk component \u2014 it hangs low on the solid front axle's centerline and takes direct hits on rock and in ruts. The oil pan comes next. The fuel tank matters most on anything technical where departure angle puts the frame rear close to the ground. DPR Offroad's full system ($549) addresses all three plus the factory transfer case area in a kit designed to overlap coverage continuously. AltRider's kit ($699) uses heavier material and includes reinforcement gussets around the factory skid mounting points \u2014 the choice if you're running the truck on serious rock. The Mopar partial upgrade ($399) is the value option for owners who want factory warranty compatibility and basic coverage extension.\n\nOne practical note: the Power Wagon runs a **coil-sprung solid front axle**, and that axle moves as a unit through its travel \u2014 the front differential and its cover sweep through an arc as the suspension compresses. A front skid has to clear the diff at full stuff; a plate mounted too close will contact the differential cover when the axle stuffs on a big obstacle. There are no CV half-shafts up front on this truck, so the clearance concern is the differential and axle housing, not half-shaft contact. Verify that any kit is cut specifically for the Power Wagon \u2014 its factory winch, front-axle disconnect, and factory transfer-case skid mounts differ from a standard Ram 2500, and a generic 2500 kit may not clear them.\n\nThe plate material changes the truck's behavior as much as the coverage does. **3/16-inch steel** is the durable, slide-over-rock standard and the cheapest per square foot, but it adds the most weight low and forward \u2014 a full steel set can run **60-80 lbs**. **1/4-inch aluminum** (as AltRider and DPR use on some kits) sheds roughly a third of that weight and resists corrosion, at a higher price and with slightly less abrasion life on repeated rock drags. On a truck that already sits near the front axle's weight limit, the aluminum weight savings up front is not trivial \u2014 it is weight removed from the worst place for it. If you mostly guard against occasional strikes and want to preserve payload, aluminum earns its premium; if you drag skids across granite regularly, steel takes the abuse better and is cheaper to gouge.\n\nFull underbody armor changes how you service the truck, and it is worth planning before you bolt it on. Confirm the front skid has a **drain-access port or drops without pulling the whole panel** so an [oil change](/db/power-wagon/pw-oil-change/) and the [full fluid service](/db/power-wagon/pw-fluids-service/) don't turn into an armor-removal job every time. Note which bolts the skids share with the [rock sliders](/db/power-wagon/pw-sliders/) and body mounts so you torque them once and correctly. Coat the mounting hardware with anti-seize on install \u2014 trail mud and road salt seize skid bolts fast, and a corroded skid bolt is the reason a \"two-hour\" service becomes a weekend.","steps":["Raise the vehicle and support at the frame on jack stands \u2014 work under the full length of the truck.","Clean and degrease the frame mounting surfaces where skid plates will attach.","Locate factory mounting points or install supplied nut plates per kit instructions.","Install the front engine/differential skid plate first \u2014 position, hand-thread all bolts, verify no contact with the front axle housing or differential cover through full suspension travel, then torque.","Install the transmission skid to overlap the rear edge of the engine skid.","Install the transfer case skid \u2014 this may overlap with or replace the factory transfer case skid per kit design.","Install the fuel tank skid last \u2014 verify it clears the tank itself and doesn't contact suspension components at full droop.","Re-inspect all fasteners after the first trail use."],"pros":["Extends factory transfer case skid coverage to oil pan, front differential, and fuel tank","Bolt-on install without fabrication \u2014 accessible to any home mechanic","Protects the low-hanging front differential and oil pan on the solid front axle","Factory skid preserved or reinforced, not removed"],"cons":["Solid front axle sweeps through travel \u2014 the front diff skid must clear the axle at full compression, not bolt flat and rigid against it","Power Wagon-specific fitment needed; the factory winch, axle-disconnect, and transfer-case skid mounts differ from a standard Ram 2500","Adds 40\u201380 lbs of underbody weight depending on coverage and plate gauge","Access to oil drain plug and transfer case drain may require removing a panel for service \u2014 note this before first oil change"]},{"id":"pw-sliders","title":"Side Steps and Rock Rails for the Power Wagon","vehicle":"power-wagon","category":"armor","subcategory":"sliders","type":"part","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":2},"cost_usd":{"low":329,"high":699},"tools_required":["socket set","torque wrench","drill (some kits)","wire brush","anti-seize"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail"],"year_compatibility":["2014-2018","2019-2024"],"tags":["armor","steps","rock-rail","slider","side-protection"],"parts":[{"name":"MORryde Rock Rail Steps for Ram 2500 Power Wagon","vendor":"MORryde","price_estimate_usd":549},{"name":"N-Fab Power Wagon Nerf Steps","vendor":"N-Fab","price_estimate_usd":499},{"name":"Westin HDX Drop Steps for Ram 2500","vendor":"Westin Automotive","price_estimate_usd":349},{"name":"AMP Research BedStep2 for Ram 2500","vendor":"AMP Research","price_estimate_usd":329}],"citations":[{"title":"MORryde Rock Rail Step Product Page","url":"https://www.morryde.com/power-wagon-steps"},{"title":"N-Fab Nerf Bar Power Wagon Install","url":"https://www.n-fab.com/ram-2500-power-wagon"},{"title":"Ram 2500 Power Wagon specifications (ground clearance, wheelbase)","url":"https://www.ramtrucks.com/ram-2500/specs.power-wagon.html"}],"alternatives":["pw-sliders-weld-on","pw-skid-plates","pw-front-bumper"],"summary":"Bolt-on steps are the right answer for most Power Wagons, and the truck's 14.2 inches of factory ground clearance is why. If you want armor that survives the rocker sitting down on a ledge, you want the weld-on frame-tied rails covered in the [weld-on slider guide](/db/power-wagon/pw-sliders-weld-on/) \u2014 this guide is about the bolt-on step and rock-rail market, which is what the vast majority of these trucks actually need.","description":"The Power Wagon leaves the factory with roughly 14.2 inches of ground clearance on a 149.3-inch wheelbase \u2014 more clearance than most lifted Wranglers, on a body that is also considerably wider. That geometry puts the rocker panels further from rock contact than on a lower, narrower rig. It does not make the rockers immune. Side slopes, tight canyon trails, and a loaded truck with the suspension compressed all still bring sheet metal toward rock. But the risk profile is different, and the aftermarket reflects it: nearly everything sold for this truck is a step tube with some degree of protection built in, not a full-coverage rock slider.\n\nThere is a second reason bolt-on steps sell so well here. This truck is tall. Getting a passenger into a crew cab that sits at 14 inches of clearance on 33s is a real reach, and it gets worse the moment you add a leveling kit and 35s. Most Power Wagon owners buy the step for daily access and treat the protection as a bonus. That is an honest reason to buy, and it changes which product is correct.\n\nThe marketing terms blur together, so sort them by what carries the load:\n\n- Drop step / running board. Bolts to the body's rocker pinch weld or to light body brackets. Designed to carry a person stepping up. Contact with rock bends the step and transmits the load straight into sheet metal. Westin's HDX Drop Step ($349) is this category done well.\n- Nerf bar. A round tube with a step pad, mounted to reinforced brackets that pick up factory frame or rocker mounting points. Stiffer than a running board, still not intended to hold up 7,000 pounds on a single point. N-Fab ($499) is the reference here.\n- Rock rail (bolt-on). Heavier-wall tube, brackets that tie into the frame rather than the body, and a profile that stands proud of the rocker so the rail eats the hit. MORryde's Rock Rail Step ($549) is the closest thing the Power Wagon market has to this.\n- Weld-on slider. Frame-tied, no brackets to shear, rated to take the truck's full weight on one point. Not covered here \u2014 see the [weld-on guide](/db/power-wagon/pw-sliders-weld-on/), $700-$1,400 installed.\n\nThe AMP Research BedStep2 ($329) is worth naming because it shows up on so many Power Wagon overland builds, but it solves bed access at the rear bumper, not rocker protection. Buy it as a supplement, not as your side armor.\n\nIf the truck sees fire roads, snow, and a lot of family entry and exit, a drop step or nerf bar is correct and the extra $200 for a rock rail buys you nothing. If you run off-camber shelf trails or rock gardens where the rocker can find granite, MORryde's rail is the floor of what is worth installing. If you routinely put the truck somewhere that a rocker gets loaded hard enough to matter, stop shopping bolt-on parts and go weld-on \u2014 bracket-mounted rails fail at the bracket, and a bracket failure on a 7,000-pound truck bends the rocker anyway, so you paid twice.\n\nThe weight argument cuts against over-buying, too. A Power Wagon's payload is rated at roughly 1,400-1,630 pounds depending on year and configuration, and every armor decision spends some of it. Heavy rails, a steel [front bumper](/db/power-wagon/pw-front-bumper/), full [skid plates](/db/power-wagon/pw-skid-plates/), and a drawer system add up faster than people expect \u2014 the [weight and payload reality guide](/db/power-wagon/hd-3-4-ton-weight-reality/) walks the arithmetic.\n\nEvery failure mode on bolt-on side protection traces back to the mount, not the tube. Three things to check before you buy:\n\nFrame versus body. Ask where the bracket lands. A bracket bolted to the rocker pinch weld transfers rock loads into unibody-thin sheet metal. A bracket bolted to the frame rail transfers them into the ladder frame, which is what you want. Vendors are not always clear about this in the listing \u2014 look at the install instructions before ordering.\n\nBracket count and spacing. Three brackets across a crew-cab rocker is the minimum worth having. Two brackets on a long crew-cab step means the middle of the tube is a lever, and lateral contact bends it into the door sill.\n\nHardware grade and corrosion. Kits ship with grade 8 hardware or they do not. Powder coat over unprepped steel on a truck that sees brine will bubble at the weld seams within two winters \u2014 the tube survives, the finish does not. Touch up any drilled hole with cold galvanizing compound at install time rather than after the rust starts.\n\n- Access only \u2014 Westin HDX Drop Step, $329-$399. Step surface with body or rocker mounting and no meaningful rock protection.\n- Access plus light protection \u2014 N-Fab nerf step, $449-$549. Round tube on reinforced brackets; handles brush and light contact.\n- Bolt-on rock rail \u2014 MORryde Rock Rail Step, $499-$599. Heavy-wall tube on frame-tied brackets; takes moderate rock contact.\n- Frame-tied armor \u2014 weld-on sliders, $700-$1,400. Rated for the truck's full weight, no bracket to shear, requires welding.\n- Bed access supplement \u2014 AMP Research BedStep2, $299-$349. Rear bumper step; unrelated to rocker protection.\n\nInstall is a one-to-two-hour job with hand tools if the frame holes are clean; budget a shop hour or two ($100-$220) if you would rather not lie under the truck. Kits that require drilling take longer and take the install out of reversible territory, which matters on a leased or resale-sensitive truck.","steps":["Clean the frame rail and rocker mounting area \u2014 remove undercoating buildup from the bracket contact points so the bracket seats on metal, not on a compressible layer.","For slip-on tube style steps: locate the factory frame mounting points (pre-drilled on most Ram 2500 frames), position the brackets, and hand-thread all hardware before torquing anything.","For MORryde rock rail steps: position the rail along the frame, verify the step surface is level and every mounting tab is fully seated against the frame, then torque to the manufacturer's spec.","Verify the step does not contact the body anywhere in suspension travel. Compress the suspension by hand or with a jack under the axle and check clearance at full bump.","Apply anti-seize to the mounting bolts, especially in salt states where frame corrosion will make removal miserable in five years.","Re-torque every fastener after the first 100-200 miles. Bracket-to-frame joints settle, and a loose bracket is what turns into a sheared bracket."],"pros":["Meaningfully improves entry and exit on a truck that sits at 14.2 inches of clearance before a lift","MORryde-tier rails provide real rock contact protection for moderate trail use","Bolt-on install without fabrication, reversible if you keep the factory hardware","Wide price range means you can match the spend to actual use instead of over-buying"],"cons":["Heavy-duty rock sliders designed specifically for this truck remain limited compared with the Wrangler market","Bracket-mounted rails fail at the bracket under hard lateral loads \u2014 the tube is rarely the weak link","Step-style designs sit lower and protect less than a frame-tied slider despite similar marketing language","Every pound here comes out of a payload budget that is tighter than the \"3/4-ton\" badge suggests"]},{"id":"pw-sliders-weld-on","title":"Weld-On Rock Sliders \u2014 Frame-Tied Rocker Armor","vehicle":"power-wagon","category":"armor","subcategory":"sliders","type":"part","difficulty":4,"time_hours":{"low":4,"high":10},"cost_usd":{"low":700,"high":1400},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","220V MIG welder","angle grinder","drill","torque wrench","magnetic level","tape measure","wire brush"],"skills_required":["welding","fabrication layout","wrenching"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["2005-2009","2010-2013","2014-2018","2019-2024"],"tags":["armor","sliders","rocker-armor"],"parts":[{"name":"Weld-on rock sliders, crew-cab length (2in x 0.250in DOM class)","vendor":"Chassis Unlimited / custom fab","price_estimate_usd":1000},{"name":"ER70S-6 MIG wire, 0.035in, 11 lb spool","vendor":"Lincoln / Hobart","price_estimate_usd":45},{"name":"Internal frame cavity wax + chassis paint","vendor":"Eastwood / Fluid Film","price_estimate_usd":50}],"citations":[{"title":"Chassis Unlimited \u2014 Power Wagon armor and sliders","url":"https://chassisunlimited.com/collections/powerwagon"},{"title":"FN Tuned \u2014 Power Wagon mods (rock sliders, protection)","url":"https://www.fntuned.com/blogs/apex/best-ram-2500-power-wagon-mods"},{"title":"Ram Body Builder Guide \u2014 frame modification and welding cautions","url":"https://www.rambodybuilder.com/"}],"alternatives":["pw-sliders","pw-skid-plates"],"summary":"The bolt-on sliders in the companion guide handle most Power Wagon trail use. This weld-on alternative exists because a Power Wagon is heavy \u2014 around **7,000 lb at the curb** \u2014 and when that much crew-cab truck pivots on a rocker against a rock, the mount carrying the load needs to tie hard into the frame. For a truck this size that actually wheels rock, the welded joint is the honest answer. It is also the one that puts heat into a high-strength steel frame, so read the frame section before you strike an arc.","description":"A slider's job is to take the truck's full weight on a single point of rock and protect the long structural rocker below the doors. On a 4,000 lb Jeep a bolt-on does that comfortably. On a Power Wagon the same maneuver puts **three to four thousand pounds through one contact point**, and it arrives as a shock load, not a static one.\n\nTwo things fail under that. First, a bolt-only mount eventually **elongates its bolt holes** \u2014 the bracket works against the frame, the holes go oval, the slider develops a wobble, and the geometry that was supposed to hold the rocker off the rock stops holding it. Second, **a bracket welded only at its face** concentrates the load into a small area of frame rail rather than spreading it. A properly built weld-on uses **three or more frame tie-ins per side with gusseted plates** that distribute the load along the rail instead of into one spot.\n\nKnow your cab, because it sets the slider length. **2014 and later Power Wagons are Crew Cab only** on a 149-inch wheelbase with the 6'4\" bed. 2005-2009 trucks were offered as Quad Cab and Mega Cab, and the Mega Cab's rocker is substantially longer \u2014 a slider cut for a 2018 crew cab will not span a 2007 Mega Cab. Measure the actual distance between wheel openings on your truck before ordering; crew-cab bars generally run **60-72 inches**.\n\nThis is where cheap sliders reveal themselves. Look for:\n\n- **Main tube: 2\" x 0.250\" wall DOM**, or 2.5\" x 0.188\" DOM at minimum. **1.75\" x 0.120\" DOM is Jeep spec** and will take a set under a 3/4-ton. HREW tube (welded seam, not drawn) is cheaper and less consistent \u2014 pass on it for a structural bar.\n- **Three or more frame tie-ins per side.** A long bar on two brackets flexes in the middle, which is exactly where a rock finds it.\n- **Gusset plates at each mount**, tying the bracket to both the vertical web and the bottom flange of the rail where the design allows it.\n\nThen decide **step versus tuck**, and be honest about use. A **kick-out step** angles the outer tube away from the body and gives you something to stand on getting into a truck whose door sills sit high \u2014 genuinely useful on a daily-driven Power Wagon, and it deflects rock away from the rocker on a side-hill. A **tucked bar** hugs the body, preserves break-over clearance, and does not catch on a rock ledge you are trying to slide across. On a truck that sees pavement most days, take the step. On a dedicated crawler, tuck it.\n\nThe Ram 2500 frame is **fully boxed and built largely from high-strength steel**, especially on 2019 and later trucks. Ram's own body builder guidance is restrictive about frame welding and drilling for a reason: heat input into high-strength steel creates a **heat-affected zone that is locally softer than the parent metal**, and welds placed on the **frame flanges** (the top and bottom horizontal surfaces) sit where bending stress is highest. The practical rules:\n\n- **Weld to the vertical web of the rail, not the top or bottom flange.** This is the single most important rule and the one most often broken.\n- **Stay clear of factory suspension and body-mount brackets and their heat zones** \u2014 do not weld within a few inches of a mount you depend on.\n- **Grind to bare, bright metal** and remove all coating, wax, and undercoating from both sides of the joint before welding. Frame coatings produce toxic fumes and porous welds.\n- **Locate the fuel tank before you start.** On a crew-cab Ram 2500 the main tank sits **between the frame rails ahead of the rear axle**, and the fill and vent lines run along the rail. Disconnect the battery, and either drop the tank or fit a proper welding blanket and metal shield between your work and anything that carries fuel or wiring. Have a fire extinguisher within arm's reach, not across the shop.\n- **Machine capacity:** 1/4\" plate wants a **220V MIG at roughly 200+ amps with 0.035\" ER70S-6 wire**, run as multiple passes with proper preheat on cold steel. A 120V hobby welder will lay something that looks like a weld and is not one. If that is what you own, this is a job for a shop.\n\nIf any of that reads as beyond your setup, **the bolt-on slider is not a consolation prize** \u2014 a well-designed bolt-on with a wide bracket footprint outperforms a badly welded frame-tie every day of the week.\n\n1. **Support the truck properly** on jack stands under the frame, wheels off the ground, and confirm it does not rock before you get under it.\n2. **Mark the mount locations** off a fixed reference \u2014 measure from the same feature on each side (a body mount, an axle centerline) so the two sliders end up parallel. Dog-tracked sliders are visible from thirty feet away.\n3. **Clean the weld zones** on the rail webs to bright metal, both sides where accessible.\n4. **Tack the brackets and hang the bar loosely.** Check door swing, rocker clearance, and the gap to the cab corner and the bed. Confirm the bar sits **level along its length** with a magnetic level, and that both sides match.\n5. **Check exhaust and brake-line clearance** on the passenger side before final welding \u2014 heat from a slider tube resting near the exhaust is a real problem on a long trail day.\n6. **Weld out fully** in short staggered passes to control distortion, alternating sides so heat does not warp one rail.\n7. **Coat everything.** Paint every weld and every ground area, and shoot **internal cavity wax into the rail** through an existing access hole. Welding on a boxed frame burns off the inside coating exactly where you cannot see it \u2014 on a salt-belt truck this is how a slider install turns into a frame repair five years later.\n8. **Inspect the welds after the first hard trip.** Any crack at a bracket toe means the mount needs another gusset, not a re-weld of the same joint.\n\n| Line item | Typical cost |\n|---|---|\n| Weld-on sliders, crew-cab length, quality DOM tube | $800-1,400 |\n| Weld consumables, discs, wire | $40-80 |\n| Cavity wax + chassis paint | $30-60 |\n| Shop welding, if farmed out (4-6 hr) | $400-700 |\n| **DIY all-in** | **$870-1,540** |\n| **Shop all-in** | **$1,270-2,200** |\n\nBudget on the higher end for a Mega Cab. If a listing does not publish **wall thickness and the number of frame tie-ins**, treat that as the answer to the question you were about to ask.\n\nStep to weld-on sliders when a heavy Power Wagon regularly loads its rockers on rock; the bolt-on covers everything lighter. Demand **2\" x 0.250\" DOM and three-plus frame tie-ins per side**, weld to the rail web rather than the flanges, keep heat away from the fuel tank and factory brackets, and wax the frame cavity afterward. Pair them with the [skid plate guide](/db/power-wagon/pw-skid-plates/) so the underbody is protected as a system, and see the [bolt-on slider guide](/db/power-wagon/pw-sliders/) if welding is not on your table. Done right, this is the rocker armor that survives a 3/4-ton's weight."},{"id":"front-locker-elocker-troubleshooting","title":"Power Wagon Front E-Locker Won't Engage: Diagnosing the Electronic Locking Differentials","vehicle":"power-wagon","category":"axles","subcategory":"differential_lockers","type":"diagnostic","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":1.0,"high":4.0},"cost_usd":{"low":20,"high":350},"tools_required":["multimeter","OBD-II scanner with Ram-specific PID support","basic socket set","electrical contact cleaner"],"skills_required":["basic electrical diagnosis","understanding of 4WD systems"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2005-2009","2010-2018","2019-present"],"tags":["elocker","lockers","differential","electrical","diagnostic","front-axle"],"parts":[{"name":"Locker solenoid","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":95},{"name":"Locker solenoid fuse (check fuse box diagram for rating)","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":5}],"citations":[{"title":"Ram Power Wagon 2500 service manual \u2014 4WD system","url":"https://www.mopar.com/en-us/care/service-records.html"},{"title":"Ram Truck Forum \u2014 locker troubleshooting thread","url":"https://www.ramtruckforum.com"}],"alternatives":["pw-locker-upgrade","pw-regear-456"],"summary":"Both differentials on the Power Wagon are factory electronic lockers. When one won't engage, the diagnosis is almost always one of four things: the speed interlock, a blown fuse, a failed solenoid, or a wiring issue. Work through them in order.","description":"The Power Wagon runs electronic lockers on both the front and rear Dana axles. Both use the same basic engagement mechanism: an electrically actuated solenoid moves a mechanical locking collar inside the differential. The control system reads vehicle speed and 4WD mode before allowing engagement \u2014 this interlock is intentional and not a failure.\n\nThe lockers will not engage above approximately 8 mph. If you're trying to lock a diff at speed, the system is working correctly. The sequence for engagement is:\n\n1. Vehicle must be in 4WD (either 4H or 4L \u2014 lockers don't engage in 2WD)\n2. Vehicle must be at or below ~8 mph\n3. Press the locker switch and hold for 2\u20133 seconds\n\nThe in-cab indicator will flash while the locker is engaging (the mechanical collar is moving) and go solid when locked. If the indicator flashes and never goes solid, the solenoid is receiving a signal but the mechanical collar isn't completing engagement. If nothing happens at all, you have an electrical problem upstream of the solenoid.\n\n**Step 1: Check the fuse**\nThe locker solenoids have dedicated fuses in the Power Distribution Center under the hood. Locate the fuse diagram on the PDC cover or in the owner's manual. Pull the front locker solenoid fuse and inspect it. A blown fuse is the single fastest fix on this list. Replace with the same rated fuse; if it blows again, you have a short downstream.\n\n**Step 2: Check for DTC codes**\nAn OBD-II scanner with Ram-specific PID support will show AXLE-related codes if the locker control module has flagged a fault. Generic OBD scanners often can't read these codes \u2014 you need a scan tool that supports Ram TIPM/body electronics, not powertrain codes. A dealer scan or a shop-level bidirectional scanner (such as the Autel MaxiSys or similar) will give you the specific fault.\n\n**Step 3: Verify signal at the solenoid connector**\nLocate the solenoid connector at the front differential \u2014 it's a sealed waterproof connector on the passenger side of the front diff housing. With the switch pressed in the cab, use a multimeter to check for 12V at the connector (back-probe the terminals or use the appropriate test leads). If you have 12V at the connector and the solenoid doesn't engage, the solenoid has failed. If you have no voltage at the connector, the problem is upstream \u2014 wiring, control module, or fuse.\n\n**Step 4: Solenoid replacement**\nThe solenoid is a straight replacement \u2014 unplug the harness, remove two mounting bolts, pull the old solenoid, install the new one. Torque the mounting bolts to spec (typically 15\u201320 ft-lb). Do not overtighten \u2014 the solenoid housing is aluminum and the mounting boss strips. Reconnect the harness, clear any stored codes, and test engagement.\n\n**Step 5: Wiring inspection**\nIf voltage is absent at the solenoid connector and the fuse is good, trace the harness back to the TIPM. Look for abrasion at frame crossmember transitions \u2014 this is the most common wiring failure point. Also inspect the connector at the TIPM for corrosion.\n\nIf neither locker engages, suspect the main locker control module output or a 4WD engagement issue. Verify 4WD is actually engaging (transfer case indicator on, front driveshaft spinning). If 4WD is confirmed and neither locker works, pull codes \u2014 this pattern typically shows a control module fault or a bad switch cluster signal.\n\nThe locker wiring on Power Wagons that have been through water crossings regularly can develop internal corrosion at the solenoid connectors. The connector looks intact from the outside but the terminals inside are green. Use electrical contact cleaner and a small wire brush to clean the terminals, then apply dielectric grease before reconnecting.","steps":["Confirm vehicle is in 4WD and below 8 mph during engagement attempt","Check and replace locker solenoid fuse if blown","Scan for DTC codes with Ram-capable scan tool","Test for 12V at solenoid connector with switch pressed","Replace solenoid if voltage present but no engagement","Trace wiring to TIPM if no voltage present at connector"],"pros":["Most failures are straightforward: fuse or solenoid \u2014 straightforward fixes","Solenoid replacement is accessible without removing the axle","OEM Mopar solenoid is readily available and inexpensive"],"cons":["Speed interlock at 8 mph is misunderstood by many owners \u2014 know it before you diagnose","Ram-specific scan codes require a capable scan tool, not a generic OBD reader","Wiring failures from water exposure can be time-consuming to trace"]},{"id":"pw-locker-upgrade","title":"Locker Upgrade Path for Power Wagon (Factory + Tru-Lok or ARB Bulletproofing)","vehicle":"power-wagon","category":"axles","subcategory":"lockers","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":4,"time_hours":{"low":6,"high":12},"cost_usd":{"low":0,"high":1800},"tools_required":["bearing puller","torque wrench (250 lb-ft)","compressed air (if installing ARB)"],"skills_required":["differential disassembly","advanced wrenching"],"build_tiers":["rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["2014-2018","2019-2024","2025+"],"tags":["locker","axles","differential","rock-crawl","factory-supplemental"],"parts":[{"name":"(Already factory) Tru-Lok front locker \u2014 solenoid + selector switch","vendor":"Ram Factory","price_estimate_usd":0},{"name":"(Already factory) Tru-Lok rear locker","vendor":"Ram Factory","price_estimate_usd":0},{"name":"ARB Air Locker rear AAM 11.5\" (replacement, if factory rear fails)","part_number":"ARB-RD188","vendor":"ARB","price_estimate_usd":1200},{"name":"Eaton ELocker rear AAM 11.5\" (alternative replacement)","vendor":"Eaton","price_estimate_usd":1100}],"alternatives":["front-locker-elocker-troubleshooting","pw-regear-456"],"citations":[{"title":"ARB Air Locker \u2014 AAM 11.5\" application (RD188)","url":"https://www.arbusa.com/air-lockers/"},{"title":"Eaton ELocker \u2014 electronic selectable locker overview","url":"https://www.eaton.com/us/en-us/products/differentials/elocker-electronic-locking-differential.html"},{"title":"American Axle (AAM) 9.25\" front / 11.5\" rear axle references","url":"https://www.aam.com/"}],"summary":"The Power Wagon comes from the factory with electronic locking front AND rear differentials \u2014 the only production truck in North America that does (no Raptor, no TRX, no Tacoma TRD Pro). For 95% of owners, the factory lockers are bulletproof and never need upgrading. This entry covers the 5% case: aftermarket replacement when a factory locker fails, or when you want a more rebuilable unit for serious rock-crawling.","description":"The Power Wagon's factory Tru-Lok system is genuinely unique:\n- Front locker: AAM 9.25\" axle, electromagnetic engagement, dash-switched\n- Rear locker: AAM 11.5\" axle, electromagnetic engagement, dash-switched (auto-engages in 4WD-Low or manually anytime)\n\nThe factory units are NOT \"limited slip\" \u2014 they're real selectable lockers. When engaged, 100% of axle torque is split 50/50 between the wheels. Performance is on par with aftermarket ARB or Eaton units.\n\n**For 95% of Power Wagon owners:** the factory lockers never need upgrading. Common issues are wiring (solenoid faults from corrosion at the connector) or actuator failures \u2014 these are repairs, not upgrades. See [[front-locker-elocker-troubleshooting]].\n\n**The 5% case** where an aftermarket locker swap makes sense:\n\n1. **Factory locker has physically failed** (cracked case, broken splines) \u2014 out of warranty, a new factory unit is $800-1,400 from Mopar. ARB or Eaton replacement is comparable cost, often with longer-term parts availability.\n\n2. **You want a rebuildable unit for hard rock-crawling.** Factory Tru-Loks are sealed; ARB Air Lockers and Eaton ELockers can be field-rebuilt with replaceable internals. Worth it if you wheel 50+ days/year in rocks.\n\n3. **You've re-geared and don't trust the factory carrier shim stack.** When the diff is open for a re-gear, swapping to ARB or Eaton at the same time saves labor. About $300 extra labor + the locker cost. See [[pw-regear-456]].\n\n**For ARB Air Lockers** ($1,200 + compressor ~$400) \u2014 proven rock-crawl reference, needs an air compressor (Power Wagon owners often install one for tire airing-up anyway, so this is a small marginal cost).\n\n**For Eaton ELockers** ($1,100, no compressor needed) \u2014 electronic activation like the factory unit, more straightforward wiring. The pick if you don't want to plumb air lines.\n\nFor aftermarket front: don't bother. The factory front locker is excellent and rarely fails. If it does fail, replace with factory Mopar.\n\nFor aftermarket rear: ARB or Eaton are both excellent. Choose based on whether you're running an air compressor anyway.\n\nHonest framing: this is a $1,200 swap that 95% of Power Wagon owners will never need. The factory lockers really are that good. Read this entry as \"what to do IF a factory unit fails\" \u2014 not \"what to install on a stock truck.\"\n\nA \"failed\" locker is far more often a wiring or actuator fault than a broken carrier. Before you condemn a sealed Tru-Lok and order a $1,200 ARB, work the cheap causes:\n\n1. Pull codes. The Tru-Lok system reports through the BCM; a C-code pointing at the front or rear locker circuit usually means a solenoid or connector fault, not mechanical failure.\n2. Inspect the axle-mounted connector. The rear locker connector sits low and forward of the AAM 11.5\" pumpkin and collects mud and salt. Corrosion here mimics a dead locker exactly \u2014 clean it, dielectric-grease it, retest.\n3. Confirm engagement conditions. The rear auto-engages in 4-Low and can be switched manually; the front only engages in 4-Low with the rear already locked. Owners regularly report a \"broken\" front locker that is simply being asked to lock in a mode the truck won't allow.\n\nThe full electrical walk-through lives in [[front-locker-elocker-troubleshooting]]. Only after the wiring and actuator check out clean does an aftermarket carrier enter the conversation.\n\nReplacing the rear Tru-Lok with an ARB or Eaton is a full third-member rebuild \u2014 pull the axle shafts, drop the carrier, and re-set up the ring and pinion. This is the job where most owners pay a shop, and rightly so:\n\n1. The AAM 11.5\" carrier comes out as an assembly; mark and save the bearing caps and shims in order.\n2. The new carrier needs backlash and pinion-bearing preload set to spec with a dial indicator and inch-pound torque wrench. Get this wrong and the gearset howls or fails.\n3. ARB requires an air line plumbed from a compressor to the diff with a bulkhead fitting; Eaton needs a switched 12V feed and ground. Route either away from the exhaust and rotating parts.\n4. Because the diff is already apart, this is the moment to re-gear if you're planning it anyway \u2014 combine the labor. See [[pw-regear-456]] for ratio choices on 37s.\n\nRealistic cost: $1,100-1,200 for the locker, plus $400 for an ARB compressor if you don't have one, plus $400-700 shop labor for the setup. Combining with a re-gear adds the gearset but only marginal extra labor.\n\nDon't replace the front with aftermarket. The factory AAM 9.25\" front Tru-Lok is excellent and parts-supported through Mopar; an aftermarket front locker buys you nothing but wiring headaches. If the front truly fails out of warranty, a factory replacement is the right call.\n\nMatch the locker to how you actually wheel. ARB's air system is the rock-crawl reference and is field-rebuildable, but it adds a compressor and air lines that need maintenance. Eaton's ELocker engages electrically like the factory unit with far simpler plumbing \u2014 the better pick if you don't already run air for tire inflation. Picking ARB purely \"because it's the best\" and then never maintaining the compressor leaves you worse off than the factory unit you removed.","pros":["Factory lockers are bulletproof for 95% of use cases","Aftermarket options exist if needed \u2014 not a \"factory only\" situation","ARB/Eaton replacements offer rebuildability that the factory unit lacks","Combining a locker swap with a re-gear saves labor"],"cons":["$1,100-1,200 for the locker, plus install labor unless DIY","Not needed for most owners \u2014 don't upgrade what isn't broken","ARB needs a compressor system if you don't already have one","Factory wiring may need adaptation for aftermarket units"]},{"id":"pw-bed-tailgate-body-mounts","title":"Bed, Tailgate, and Body Mount Service \u2014 Power Wagon","vehicle":"power-wagon","category":"body","subcategory":"body-mounts","type":"procedure","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":8},"cost_usd":{"low":40,"high":500},"tools_required":["breaker bar + 6-point sockets (18mm/21mm)","penetrating oil","torque wrench (up to ~120 lb-ft)","floor jack + blocks (to support bed)","engine hoist or 3-4 helpers (to lift bed)","wire brush / rust converter"],"skills_required":["working with seized fasteners","safe lifting of a heavy bed"],"build_tiers":["stock","mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2014-2018","2019-2024","2025+"],"tags":["body","bed","tailgate","body-mounts","rust","squeak"],"parts":[{"name":"Cab/bed body mount bushing set (polyurethane or OEM rubber)","vendor":"Mopar / Daystar","price_estimate_usd":130},{"name":"Bed bolt + isolator hardware kit","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":45},{"name":"Tailgate latch / handle / cable repair parts","vendor":"Mopar / Dorman","price_estimate_usd":60},{"name":"Tailgate assist strut (damped lowering)","vendor":"DV8 / Mopar","price_estimate_usd":90}],"citations":[{"title":"Mopar parts catalog \u2014 Ram 2500 body mount and bed hardware","url":"https://www.moparpartsgiant.com/"},{"title":"RamForum \u2014 body mount bushing and bed-removal threads","url":"https://www.ramforum.com/"}],"alternatives":["pw-skid-plates","pw-interior-upgrades"],"summary":"Body and bed work on a Power Wagon is mostly about two things: chasing squeaks and clunks from worn body-mount bushings, and dealing with rust-seized bed bolts before they strand you mid-job. The mounts isolate the cab and bed from a flexing HD frame; when the rubber ages or a trail twist tweaks them, you get creaks over every bump. Budget $40 for hardware and an afternoon, or up to $500 if you replace the full mount set. Soak every fastener in penetrating oil the day before \u2014 this is a seized-bolt job, not a quick one.","description":"The Power Wagon's separate cab and bed ride on rubber body-mount bushings bolted to the frame. That isolation is what keeps the cab quiet while the frame articulates over rough terrain \u2014 and it is exactly what wears out on a truck used off-road. Bed and body service is unglamorous but it is what stops the rattles, restores a tailgate that latches right, and keeps water and dust out of the cab.\n\nA creak or clunk that tracks with body flex (turning into a driveway at an angle, cresting a rut) usually traces to a collapsed or torn body-mount bushing. Inspect each mount with the truck on level ground: look for cracked, mushroomed, or oil-soaked rubber and for a gap where the bushing has compressed. Replace them in pairs so the cab sits level. Polyurethane bushings firm up the connection and last longer but transmit a little more noise; OEM rubber stays quietest. Whichever you choose, support the cab or bed with a jack and blocks, back the bolt out, swap the bushing, and torque to the service-manual value \u2014 over-torquing crushes the new bushing and reintroduces the noise.\n\nPulling the bed (to service the frame, fuel tank, or rear body mounts) is a 4-bolt-per-side job on paper and a penetrating-oil battle in practice. The bed bolts thread into captured nuts that corrode, and snapping one turns an afternoon into a weekend. Soak them for 24 hours, use 6-point sockets and a breaker bar, and apply heat if needed. Disconnect the fuel filler, tail-light harness, and any in-bed wiring first. Four strong helpers or an engine hoist with a spreader bar lifts the bed cleanly. On reinstall, anti-seize every bolt so the next service is not a fight.\n\nA tailgate that will not latch or rattles usually needs a latch/handle rebuild or a stretched cable \u2014 cheap Dorman or Mopar parts fix it. A damped tailgate-assist strut stops the heavy gate from slamming down. While the bed is off or the gate is apart, check the cab and bed weatherstrip and drain points; a trail truck packs mud into seams, and a blocked drain rots a bed floor from the inside.\n\nBody-mount bolts on the 2500 run big \u2014 typically **18mm or 21mm heads** into captured frame nuts, torqued in the rough **60-90 ft-lb** range depending on position (verify against the factory manual for your year; cab mounts and bed mounts differ). Snug them progressively and check the **cab-to-bed gap** as you go \u2014 an uneven gap or a bedside rubbing the cab corner means a mount stack is compressed or a spacer is missing. If you have added a **body lift** or switched to polyurethane mounts, re-check the gap plus the shifter and brake-line slack, because moving the cab even an inch can tension a line or a boot. Never reuse a crushed rubber isolator; it will not restore ride height and reintroduces the clunk you were chasing.\n\nThe real enemy on a Power Wagon body is corrosion at the captured nuts and the bed floor \u2014 an ounce of prevention saves the seized-bolt weekend described above. Once the bed is off, wire-brush and treat any surface rust on the frame rails and mount points, then **anti-seize or wax-coat every bolt** on reassembly. Clear the **bed and cab drain plugs** \u2014 mud packs them and traps water against the floor. In a snow-belt or coastal truck, a yearly cavity-wax spray into the frame and rocker seams is cheap insurance. While you are under the truck, look over the [skid plates](/db/power-wagon/pw-skid-plates/) and [rock sliders](/db/power-wagon/pw-sliders/), since they share fasteners and mounting points with the body.","pros":["Fresh body mounts cure the creaks and clunks that worn rubber causes","Anti-seize on reassembly makes every future bed-off job far easier","Tailgate latch and assist parts are cheap and restore daily usability"],"cons":["Seized bed and mount bolts are the real difficulty \u2014 soak and heat them","A heavy bed needs a hoist or several helpers to lift safely","Polyurethane mounts trade a little extra cabin noise for longer life"]},{"id":"pw-front-brakes","title":"Front Brake Pads and Rotors \u2014 2500 Power Wagon","vehicle":"power-wagon","category":"brakes","subcategory":"pads-rotors","type":"procedure","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1.5,"high":3},"cost_usd":{"low":120,"high":400},"tools_required":["floor jack rated to 3+ tons","jack stands rated to 3+ tons","lug wrench / impact (22mm sockets)","C-clamp or caliper piston tool","torque wrench (up to ~130 lb-ft)","micrometer","brake cleaner","wire brush","dial indicator (optional, for runout)"],"skills_required":["solid wrenching","brake-system care (clean, torque to spec, bed the pads)"],"build_tiers":["stock","mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2014-2018","2019-2024","2025+"],"tags":["brakes","safety","pads","rotors","towing","maintenance"],"parts":[{"name":"Front brake pad set (severe-duty / towing compound)","vendor":"Mopar / Hawk LTS / PowerStop Z36","price_estimate_usd":70},{"name":"Front vented rotors, pair (~12.1 in / 307 mm)","vendor":"Mopar / Centric / PowerStop","price_estimate_usd":140},{"name":"Caliper slide-pin kit + high-temp silicone grease","vendor":"Carlson / Mopar","price_estimate_usd":25},{"name":"DOT 3/DOT 4 brake fluid (for bleed/top-off)","vendor":"Mopar / Valvoline","price_estimate_usd":12}],"citations":[{"title":"Dodge Ram brake specifications (rotor sizes / minimum thickness)","url":"http://dodgeram.org/tech/specs/brakespecs.htm"},{"title":"RamForum \u2014 2500 front brake rotor service thread","url":"https://www.ramforum.com/threads/2500-front-brake-rotors.112434/"}],"alternatives":["pw-steering-gear","pw-tie-rod-drag-link","pw-fluids-service"],"summary":"Front brakes on a Power Wagon are a routine job, but this is a roughly 7,000-pound truck that gets used hard, so it eats pads faster than a half-ton \u2014 especially if you tow or crawl. Plan on front vented rotors around 12.1 inches, a severe-duty pad compound, and fresh slide pins. Budget $120-$400 in parts depending on rotor quality, or $450-$800 at a shop. Brakes are safety-critical: clean every mating surface, torque to spec, and bed the pads properly before you trust them on a grade.","description":"The 2500 Power Wagon runs large front vented discs (roughly 12.1 in / 307 mm) with single-piston floating calipers. The hardware is durable, but the truck's weight and the way it gets used \u2014 towing, washboard, low-range descents \u2014 wears pads and glazes rotors faster than the maintenance minder predicts. Replacing pads and rotors as a set, with attention to the slide pins, restores full stopping power and kills the pulsing and squeal that come from worn or sticky hardware.\n\nMeasure the old rotors with a micrometer and compare against the minimum-thickness figure stamped or cast into the rotor hat \u2014 Ram 2500 fronts are commonly machined no thinner than roughly 1.21-1.27 inches depending on the exact casting. On a truck this heavy, do not machine marginal rotors to save money; replace them. Pick a pad compound suited to your use: a severe-duty or towing pad resists fade on long descents far better than a quiet ceramic street pad. If you regularly drag a trailer down grades, that compound choice matters more than the rotor brand.\n\nBuy the slide-pin kit at the same time as the pads. It is a $25 line item that determines whether the job lasts 40,000 miles or 15,000, and it is the part most people skip.\n\nBreak the lug nuts loose, lift the front on rated stands, and pull the wheels. Remove the two caliper guide-pin bolts, lift the caliper off, and hang it from the coil with a wire \u2014 never let it dangle on the hose. Compress the piston with a C-clamp against the old pad before unbolting the caliper bracket and sliding off the rotor. Clean the hub face to bare metal so the new rotor seats flat; a rusty hub causes runout and pedal pulse and is the single most common cause of a \"warped rotor\" complaint after a fresh job. Pull the slide pins, clean them, and re-grease with high-temp silicone brake grease \u2014 seized pins are the number-one cause of uneven Power Wagon pad wear. Reassemble, torque the caliper bracket bolts and guide pins to the service-manual values, and torque the lug nuts to the figure in your owner's manual in a star pattern.\n\nIf you have a dial indicator, check rotor runout on the hub after mounting the new rotor. Anything past about 0.003 inch will come back as a pedal pulse in a few thousand miles, and the fix at that point is cleaning or indexing the rotor, not buying different pads.\n\nBefore driving normally, pump the pedal until firm so the piston takes up the gap. Then bed the new pads: a series of moderate stops from about 35 mph, letting the brakes cool between, transfers an even layer of friction material onto the rotor. Skipping this step is what causes early judder and noise. Check the fluid level, and if the pedal feels soft, bleed the system. Treat any uneven heat, pulling, or a low pedal as a stop-and-diagnose item, not something to ride out.\n\nA surprising share of Power Wagon brake complaints are not worn friction material. Work through these before spending money twice:\n\n- Pulsing under braking. Almost never a genuinely warped rotor. It is uneven friction deposits from a bad bed-in, or runout from a rusty hub face or an out-of-spec wheel bearing. Clean the hub, check runout, re-bed.\n- Pulling to one side. A sticking caliper slide pin or a collapsed flex hose acting as a one-way valve. Feel both front wheels after a drive; a dragging corner runs hot enough that you should not touch the wheel.\n- Pad wear on one side only. Seized pins again, or a caliper bracket that was reinstalled without cleaning the abutment clips.\n- Soft pedal that returns after pumping. Air, a failing master cylinder, or fluid that has absorbed moisture. Power Wagon brake fluid gets cooked on descents; see the [fluids and service guide](/db/power-wagon/pw-fluids-service/) for the flush interval.\n- ABS light with a brake complaint. Pull codes before touching anything mechanical \u2014 a damaged tone ring or wheel speed sensor from trail debris presents as a brake fault. The [OBD2 diagnostics guide](/db/power-wagon/pw-obd2-diagnostics/) covers the read.\n\nOne more: if the truck also shimmies or wanders, do not treat that as a brake problem. Front-end play on a solid-axle truck presents at the steering wheel under braking and gets misdiagnosed constantly. Check the [tie rod and drag link guide](/db/power-wagon/pw-tie-rod-drag-link/) and the [steering gear guide](/db/power-wagon/pw-steering-gear/) before you buy rotors.\n\nThe gas 6.4L Power Wagon has no engine brake \u2014 that is a Cummins feature, and this truck does not have one. Everything that keeps your brakes alive on a grade comes from gearing and technique.\n\nUse Tow/Haul mode. It holds lower gears, downshifts earlier on throttle lift, and does far more work than most owners realize. On a sustained descent, pick a gear that holds your speed without touching the pedal at all, then use firm intermittent applications to shed speed rather than steady light pressure. Continuous light dragging is what cooks fluid and glazes pads; short firm stabs let the rotors shed heat between applications.\n\nAt the truck's roughly 10,600-pound trailer rating, a working trailer brake controller is not optional equipment. Verify gain settings at the start of a trip on a flat road, not at the top of a pass. And if the truck has been re-geared or is on taller tires, the effective engine braking changes \u2014 a truck on 37s with factory 4.10s holds a grade noticeably worse than it did on 33s. The [weight and payload guide](/db/power-wagon/hd-3-4-ton-weight-reality/) covers what the mass actually demands of the system.\n\nParts, doing it yourself: pads $50-$110, rotors $110-$260 for the pair, slide-pin kit and grease $25, fluid $12. Call it $120 on the low end with economy parts and $400 with premium coated rotors and a severe-duty compound. Coated rotors are worth the upcharge in salt states \u2014 bare castings on a truck that sits between trips corrode at the hub face and reintroduce the runout problem you paid to fix.\n\nAt a shop, expect $450-$800 for front pads and rotors depending on parts grade and region, typically 1.5-2.0 hours of labor. A caliper replacement adds roughly $150-$250 per side in parts. A full fluid flush is usually $120-$180 and is worth bundling into the same visit if the fluid has never been changed.","pros":["Restores full braking on a heavy truck that works its brakes hard","Fresh slide pins cure the most common cause of uneven wear and drag","Towing-rated compound dramatically reduces long-descent fade","Entirely within reach of home tools \u2014 no press, no specialty equipment"],"cons":["Heavy rotors and stubborn rust make this more physical than a light-truck job","A quiet street pad fades on grades; match the compound to your actual use","Skipping the bed-in procedure causes judder you will then chase for weeks","No engine brake on the gas 6.4L means the friction system carries the whole load on a descent"]},{"id":"hd-3-4-ton-weight-reality","title":"Power Wagon Weight, Towing, and Payload Reality \u2014 What 7,000 Pounds Means Off-Road","vehicle":"power-wagon","category":"build_recipe","subcategory":"build_planning","type":"reference","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0,"high":0},"cost_usd":{"low":0,"high":0},"tools_required":["CAT scale weight ticket","door-jamb payload sticker","tongue-weight scale","tire load-rating chart"],"skills_required":[],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2005-2009","2010-2013","2014-2018","2019-2024"],"tags":["weight","payload","towing","3/4-ton","build-planning","overland"],"parts":[],"citations":[{"title":"Ram Power Wagon specs \u2014 curb weight, payload, tow rating, ground clearance","url":"https://www.ramtrucks.com/ram-2500/specs.power-wagon.html"},{"title":"Ram HD towing and payload chart (axle ratio / GVWR by configuration)","url":"https://www.ramtrucks.com/content/dam/fca-brands/na/ramtrucks/en_us/towing/towing-capacity-guide/brochure/my25_Ram_HD_Customer_TowPayChart_2.1.pdf"}],"alternatives":["pw-regear-456","pw-tires-mt","pw-front-bumper"],"summary":"The Power Wagon weighs approximately 7,000 lbs at the curb, carries a payload rating of roughly 1,400-1,630 lbs, and tows up to about 10,620 lbs. Those three numbers govern every build decision you will make \u2014 and the payload number is the one that bites, because a typical armor-and-gear build spends most of it before you load a passenger.","description":"The Ram Power Wagon is a 3/4-ton heavy-duty truck. \"3/4-ton\" is a historical payload classification with no relationship to modern capacity; the actual curb weight is what matters, and it lands around 6,900-7,200 lbs depending on generation and options. That is before fuel, gear, passengers, recovery equipment, or anything in the bed.\n\nTwo drivetrain generations matter for the rest of this discussion. The 2005-2013 trucks run the 5.7L HEMI behind 4.56 gears with an AAM 10.5-inch rear axle. The 2014-and-later trucks run the 6.4L HEMI \u2014 410 hp and 429 lb-ft \u2014 behind 4.10 gears with an AAM 11.5-inch rear. Those are different trucks in how they respond to bigger tires, and advice written for one does not transfer to the other.\n\nIn rock crawling, articulation gets you over obstacles, but weight is what commits you. A light vehicle can pick its line and back out. A 7,000-pound truck goes where it started going.\n\nOn sidehills, center-of-gravity height relative to track width sets rollover risk. The Power Wagon is tall and heavy. Factory approach and departure angles are acceptable and factory ground clearance is roughly 14.2 inches, but sidehills on this truck deserve genuine caution \u2014 this is not a vehicle where casual off-camber driving is appropriate at any significant angle.\n\nOn soft terrain \u2014 mud, sand, snow \u2014 ground pressure decides whether you float or sink. A 7,000-lb truck on 33-inch tires carries considerably higher ground pressure than a 4,500-lb Wrangler on 35s. Airing down to 15-18 psi (check your tire's minimum cold rating) helps substantially, but you are always at a disadvantage to a lighter rig in soft conditions.\n\nIn recovery, you are the heaviest object at the scene. Winch and recovery-point ratings need to be sized to actual loaded weight, not curb weight. The factory 12,000-lb WARN on a 7,000-lb truck is appropriately sized; a 9,500-lb winch on this platform works near its limit in a difficult pull.\n\nBrochure curb weight is a configuration average. Your truck, with your options and your gear, is a different number \u2014 and the only way to know it is a scale.\n\nDrive to a CAT scale at a truck stop. It costs roughly $13-$15 for the first weigh and a few dollars for a re-weigh the same day. Weigh the truck twice: once loaded exactly as it sits for a normal trip, once empty if you can. The ticket gives you steer-axle and drive-axle weights separately, which is the part that matters \u2014 you can be under GVWR and still over the front GAWR after adding a steel bumper and winch.\n\nCompare the ticket against three stickers. The door-jamb payload sticker gives the specific payload for your VIN, not the marketing maximum. The door-jamb tire and loading label gives front and rear GAWR. And the tire sidewall gives a per-tire load rating at a specified pressure \u2014 swapping to an aftermarket tire with a lower load index than factory quietly lowers your real capacity regardless of what the door sticker says. See the [tire guide](/db/power-wagon/pw-tires-mt/) for load-range choices that hold up on a truck this heavy.\n\nRated payload is typically 1,400-1,630 lbs depending on year and configuration. That number covers passengers, cargo, and trailer tongue weight combined.\n\nHere is how a normal build spends it:\n\n- Steel front bumper: 100-200 lb\n- Rear bumper with tire carrier: 150-250 lb\n- Full skid plate set: 80-150 lb\n- Rock rails or sliders: 60-120 lb\n- Bed drawer system: 150-250 lb\n- Rooftop tent: 130-200 lb\n- Fridge, water, and recovery gear: 150-250 lb\n- Four adults: roughly 700 lb\n\nAdd a bumper, skids, drawers, and a tent and you are at 500-800 lbs before a single person gets in. Fill a crew cab with four adults and the arithmetic runs out. The [front bumper guide](/db/power-wagon/pw-front-bumper/) and the [skid plate guide](/db/power-wagon/pw-skid-plates/) both note the weight penalty for exactly this reason.\n\nExceeding payload does not cause immediate catastrophic failure, but it lengthens braking distance, squats the truck (which costs you departure angle), pushes tires past their load rating, and gives an insurer or a manufacturer a reason to look hard at a claim. Know the number before you load.\n\nTow rating runs to roughly 10,000-10,620 lbs with the correct hitch, weight distribution, and trailer brake setup. The 3/4-ton chassis is built for it. What limits you in practice is not the frame \u2014 it is the gas engine and the payload budget.\n\nTongue weight counts against payload. A 9,000-lb trailer at a healthy 12% tongue weight puts 1,080 lbs on the hitch, which is most of the truck's rated payload before you or the dog get in. This is the most common way Power Wagon owners end up over GVWR while believing they are well inside the tow rating.\n\nThe 6.4L HEMI at 4.10 gears works near the top of its range at maximum tow weight, and there is no engine brake on a gas truck. Long grades generate real transmission and coolant heat; a transmission cooler is worth adding if you tow heavy regularly. If the truck is on 35s or 37s, the effective ratio has already dropped \u2014 37s on factory 4.10s behave like roughly 3.66 gears \u2014 and towing near the rating on that combination is unpleasant. The [regear guide](/db/power-wagon/pw-regear-456/) covers restoring the ratio.\n\nThe Power Wagon is the most capable factory-built truck for combined trail and recovery use. It is not a rock crawler. The 3/4-ton chassis, the weight, and the cab length make dedicated crawling inefficient next to a Wrangler. The lockers and Articulink help, but the physics of 7,000 pounds on a technical shelf road are what they are.\n\nIt is also not a short-wheelbase agile trail truck. The long wheelbase limits breakover angle, and long trucks belly out on obstacles a Wrangler crosses cleanly. Know your approach, breakover, and departure numbers for your specific configuration rather than the brochure's.\n\nWhere the Power Wagon shines: overland trips with real gear, recovery duty as the rig that pulls others out, trails that demand genuine clearance and articulation without being technical single-track, and towing a trailer to camp before driving trails from there. It is supremely capable for the job it was built for.","pros":["3/4-ton chassis handles real payload and real towing","Weight plus factory lockers plus Articulink is a capable combination for moderate-to-hard trail use","Recovery capacity to self-extract or pull other vehicles out","Factory 14.2 inches of ground clearance means many builds need no lift at all"],"cons":["7,000+ lbs limits what terrain is approachable","High ground pressure in soft terrain relative to lighter rigs","Long wheelbase limits breakover angle and tight trail navigation","Payload is the real constraint \u2014 an armor-and-gear build can consume most of it before passengers"]},{"id":"power-wagon-vs-rebel-vs-rubicon","title":"Ram Power Wagon vs. Ram Rebel vs. Jeep Wrangler Rubicon \u2014 An Honest Comparison","vehicle":"power-wagon","category":"build_recipe","subcategory":"comparison","type":"reference","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0,"high":0},"cost_usd":{"low":0,"high":0},"tools_required":["window sticker / build sheet","tape measure (bed length + track width)","spec comparison worksheet"],"skills_required":[],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2019-present"],"tags":["comparison","buyers-guide","rebel","rubicon","capability","daily-driver"],"parts":[],"citations":[{"title":"Ram Power Wagon official specs","url":"https://www.ramtrucks.com/power-wagon.html"},{"title":"Jeep Wrangler Rubicon official specs","url":"https://www.jeep.com/wrangler"}],"alternatives":["pw-generation-buyers-guide","hd-3-4-ton-weight-reality"],"summary":"The Power Wagon is a purpose-built trail truck on a 3/4-ton chassis. The Rebel is a style package. The Rubicon is a shorter, lighter vehicle built for technical terrain. They're three different products with significant overlap in buyer interest but not much in actual capability.","description":"Comparing these three vehicles shows up constantly in buying discussions. It's worth being direct: they're not the same type of vehicle, and the right choice depends entirely on what you actually need.\n\nThe Rebel is a Ram 1500 half-ton with an appearance package and mild off-road tuning. It gets a 2-inch factory lift over the standard Ram 1500, upgraded all-terrain tires, skid plates, and electronic locking rear differential on most trims. It is a very capable highway and light-trail truck. It is not factory-equipped with a front locker, a factory winch, or an electronic sway bar disconnect.\n\nThe Rebel looks like the Power Wagon. On the showroom floor, the Rebel's styling is arguably more aggressive. In the mud, the Power Wagon is fundamentally a different vehicle.\n\n**Buy a Rebel if:** You want the Ram aesthetic with light trail capability, you don't need a front locker or factory winch, and the half-ton platform's lighter curb weight (~5,400 lbs) and better fuel economy matter to you.\n\n**Don't buy a Rebel expecting Power Wagon capability.** It doesn't have it. The factory locker/winch/sway bar package on the Power Wagon represents significant engineering and cost that the Rebel doesn't have.\n\nThe Power Wagon is a Ram 2500 3/4-ton truck. Heavier, higher payload, higher tow rating. Factory front and rear electronic lockers, factory Warn 12,000 lb winch, factory electronic sway bar disconnect, Articulink (2014+). These are not add-ons \u2014 they're factory-installed and integrated into the truck's structure and control electronics.\n\nCurb weight: approximately 7,000 lbs. That weight buys payload capacity and recovery capability, and it costs you in soft-terrain and tight-trail performance.\n\n**Buy a Power Wagon if:** You need the payload and tow rating, you want a factory winch and locker package without aftermarket installation, and you're doing trail runs that benefit from the full factory capability package.\n\nThe Rubicon is the factory trail-built Wrangler variant, available in both 2-door and 4-door (Unlimited) configurations. It is shorter and lighter than both trucks: the 4-door Unlimited weighs approximately 4,500 lbs. It has factory front and rear electronic lockers (Dana 44 front and rear), a front sway bar disconnect (electronic on newer versions), and 33-inch tires on most configurations. The Rubicon 392 (V8) and the Rubicon 4xe (PHEV) add further performance differentiation.\n\nWheelbase on the 4-door Unlimited is 116 inches vs. 149 inches for the Power Wagon. That shorter wheelbase directly improves breakover angle and makes the Rubicon more capable on tight, technical terrain. The lighter weight floats through soft terrain better and makes it more manageable to self-rescue.\n\nThe Rubicon cannot tow or haul what the Power Wagon can. It is a 1/2-ton equivalent chassis, the interior is smaller, and the payload is typically 1,200 lbs or less. For work use or overland trips with real gear loads, the Power Wagon is the right tool.\n\n**Buy a Rubicon if:** You prioritize technical trail capability over payload and towing, you want a shorter vehicle that fits tighter trails, and you don't need to tow more than 3,500\u20134,500 lbs.\n\n| Spec | Power Wagon | Ram Rebel (1500) | Wrangler Rubicon 4-Door |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Curb weight | ~7,000 lbs | ~5,400 lbs | ~4,500 lbs |\n| Tow rating | ~10,600 lbs | ~9,000 lbs | ~3,500 lbs |\n| Payload | ~1,500 lbs | ~1,800 lbs | ~1,200 lbs |\n| Front locker | Factory | No | Factory |\n| Rear locker | Factory | Factory | Factory |\n| Factory winch | Factory Warn 12k | No | No |\n| Sway bar disconnect | Electronic | No | Electronic |\n| Wheelbase (4-door) | 149\" | 144\" | 116\" |\n\nThe Power Wagon is the correct choice when you need payload, towing, and factory trail capability in one vehicle. The Rubicon is the correct choice when technical terrain and short-wheelbase agility matter more than payload. The Rebel is a daily driver with an off-road aesthetic and limited trail credibility relative to the other two.\n\nNone of these vehicles is wrong \u2014 they serve different needs. But if someone is comparing these three because they want serious trail capability with daily-driver use, the choice is between the Power Wagon and the Rubicon, and the deciding factor is whether payload/towing or trail agility matters more.","pros":["Power Wagon's factory locker/winch/sway package is unmatched at any price point","Rubicon's shorter wheelbase is a genuine capability advantage on technical terrain","Rebel is a solid daily driver and light-trail truck at lower cost"],"cons":["Power Wagon's weight limits soft terrain and tight technical trails","Rubicon cannot replace a truck for payload or towing duty","Rebel's off-road capability doesn't match its off-road appearance"]},{"id":"pw-generation-buyers-guide","title":"Ram Power Wagon: 2005\u20132009 vs 2010\u20132018 vs 2019\u2013Present \u2014 Which Generation to Buy","vehicle":"power-wagon","category":"build_recipe","subcategory":"buyers_guide","type":"reference","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0,"high":0},"cost_usd":{"low":25000,"high":75000},"tools_required":["OBD-II scanner","window sticker / build sheet","flashlight (undercarriage inspection)","tire tread-depth gauge"],"skills_required":["vehicle inspection","test drive evaluation"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2005-2009","2010-2018","2019-present"],"tags":["buyers-guide","generations","used","hemi","articulink"],"parts":[],"citations":[{"title":"Ram Power Wagon history and trims \u2014 Ram Trucks official","url":"https://www.ramtrucks.com/power-wagon.html"},{"title":"Carli Suspension Power Wagon fitment guide","url":"https://carlisuspension.com/power-wagon/"}],"alternatives":["hd-3-4-ton-weight-reality","power-wagon-vs-rebel-vs-rubicon"],"summary":"The Power Wagon has been one of the most capable factory trucks for two decades \u2014 but the three generations are significantly different vehicles. Know what you're buying.","description":"The Ram Power Wagon launched in 2005 as a 3/4-ton HD truck purpose-built for trail and recovery use. Unlike most off-road packages that bolt skid plates onto a stock chassis, Chrysler built the Power Wagon around dedicated components: a solid front axle, factory electronic lockers front and rear, a Warn winch, and an electronic sway bar disconnect. That core formula has persisted through every generation. The significant differences are in electronics, articulation, and long-term reliability.\n\nThe original Power Wagon used the 4th gen Ram body (2002\u20132008 body style carried through early Power Wagon production). The 5.7L Hemi was the only engine offered \u2014 there was no diesel option. Multi-Displacement System (MDS) was present, which means the lifter failure window opens as these trucks approach 100,000 miles.\n\nThe lockers are the same design as later trucks \u2014 electronic solenoid-actuated, both front and rear. The winch is the Warn 9,500 lb unit on 2005\u20132007 trucks, upgraded to the 12,000 lb Warn on 2008+. Suspension is a solid front axle with coil springs, not Articulink \u2014 this is a conventional solid axle setup with a standard track bar and drag link arrangement. That actually makes it more straightforward to understand and service, though the articulation numbers lag the later Articulink design.\n\n**Buy one if:** You want the lowest entry price, you're mechanically comfortable with the MDS concern, and you don't need the Articulink system. These trucks are approaching 20 years old \u2014 inspect everything on the front axle, check the MDS lifters carefully.\n\nThe 2009 Ram refresh brought a completely new body to the Power Wagon lineup. The 5.7L Hemi continued, as did MDS. The big improvement was electronics: the locker and sway bar disconnect controls moved to proper in-cab switches rather than the somewhat awkward 2005\u20132009 dash layout. Winch integration also improved.\n\nStill no Articulink \u2014 the front end is a conventional solid axle with coil springs. Suspension travel and articulation are good but not exceptional compared to what came after. These trucks are solid values on the used market: the body is cleaner than the 2005\u20132009 examples, the electronics are more refined, and they've had time to mature. MDS lifter risk is real at higher mileages.\n\n**Buy one if:** You want a solid used truck in the $25,000\u2013$38,000 range, you're committed to inspecting the MDS lifters or deleting MDS proactively. Good daily driver and moderate trail rig.\n\nThe 2014 model year brought Articulink to the Power Wagon, and it fundamentally changed the front end. Articulink adds an additional articulation joint on each front control arm, allowing the axle to droop significantly further than a conventional solid axle setup. Ram claims up to 25% more axle articulation compared to the previous design.\n\nThe result is measurable: the Power Wagon's approach to ledges and side-hills improved, and the truck can follow terrain that pins the front tires of previous-gen trucks. This is the generation where the Power Wagon stops being \"capable for a big truck\" and starts being genuinely competitive with dedicated trail vehicles.\n\nArticulink also introduces wear points that don't exist on earlier trucks \u2014 those extra joints pivot and load-cycle, and they wear. Replacement intervals and inspection procedures are covered in the Articulink guide in this database.\n\nThe 5.7L Hemi and MDS continued through 2018. MDS lifter failures are well-documented by this mileage range on many examples.\n\n**Buy one if:** You want factory Articulink and are willing to inspect and maintain the system. The 2016\u20132018 trucks are often the sweet spot \u2014 Articulink is proven, trucks are used but not worn, and prices have settled.\n\nThe current-generation Ram 1500 launched in 2019 as the \"DT\" platform. The Power Wagon stayed on the **HD platform** (2500 chassis), so it uses the 2019+ Ram 2500 body \u2014 not the lighter 1500 body \u2014 but the interior and infotainment updated substantially. The 12-inch Uconnect touchscreen came to the Power Wagon with the 2019 updates, and the interior is genuinely good.\n\nThe mechanical package is the same: 5.7L Hemi, Articulink, electronic lockers, Warn 12,000 lb winch, electronic sway bar disconnect. Payload and towing ratings have been refined. The MDS system is still present \u2014 lifter failure is a known concern across all Hemi production.\n\n**Buy one if:** You want the newest electronics, best interior, and warranty coverage. These trucks command premium used prices. New examples are $65,000+.\n\nIf you're buying used, the 2016\u20132018 window (Articulink, settled prices, not worn out) is where most buyers land. If budget is the priority, 2010\u20132013 trucks without Articulink are solid values \u2014 plan for MDS attention. Avoid early 2005\u20132007 trucks unless you know the MDS history well and are prepared for a potential lifter job.\n\n1. Listen for a tick on cold start \u2014 MDS lifter prelude\n2. Cycle both lockers through the in-cab switch \u2014 engagement should be positive, no grinding\n3. Disconnect and reconnect the sway bar \u2014 hydraulic actuator should operate smoothly\n4. Run the winch in and out \u2014 check line wrap, listen for motor strain\n5. Check the Articulink joints for slop (2014+) \u2014 grab the control arm and try to move it in/out; there should be no lateral play\n6. Confirm both locker solenoid fuses are in place under the hood","pros":["Factory locker and winch package unmatched at any price point new","Articulink (2014+) is a genuine capability improvement","5.7L Hemi is proven and parts are everywhere"],"cons":["MDS lifter failure is a real concern at 80k\u2013120k miles across all generations","7,000+ lb curb weight limits what you can do on tight trails","Articulink adds wear points and repair cost that earlier trucks don't have"]},{"id":"pw-radiator-service","title":"Radiator and Cooling Service \u2014 6.4L Hemi Power Wagon","vehicle":"power-wagon","category":"cooling","subcategory":"radiator","type":"procedure","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1.5,"high":3},"cost_usd":{"low":60,"high":700},"tools_required":["drain pan (3+ gal)","metric sockets and ratchet","coolant funnel / spill-free filler","hose pliers","shop rags","gloves and eye protection"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","patience bleeding air from the system"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["2014-2018","2019-2024","2025+"],"tags":["cooling","coolant","maintenance","overheating","towing"],"parts":[{"name":"Mopar OAT Coolant (purple, MS.90032) \u2014 50/50 premix","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":25},{"name":"Engine coolant recovery (degas) bottle","part_number":"68232673","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":90},{"name":"Upper + lower radiator hose set","vendor":"Gates / Mopar","price_estimate_usd":70},{"name":"OEM-spec replacement radiator (plastic end-tank)","vendor":"Mopar / Spectra","price_estimate_usd":350}],"citations":[{"title":"Mopar coolant specification MS.90032 (OAT, 10-year/150k)","url":"https://www.mopar.com/en-us/my-vehicle/fluids.html"},{"title":"RamForum \u2014 6.4L coolant drain-and-fill capacity thread","url":"https://www.ramforum.com/threads/2016-2500-6-4l-engine-coolant-drain-and-fill-questions.217942/"}],"alternatives":["pw-oil-change","pw-obd2-diagnostics"],"summary":"The 6.4L Power Wagon takes roughly 12 quarts of Mopar purple OAT coolant (MS.90032), and the system is good for about 10 years or 150,000 miles from the factory fill. A drain-and-fill runs you $60 in fluid; the parts that actually fail on this truck are the plastic degas bottle and the radiator end-tanks, not the coolant itself. Do a full service before a hot-climate trail trip, because a Power Wagon at 6,900 lb crawling in low range builds heat fast.","description":"The 6.4L Hemi uses a mechanical fan clutch and a single crossflow radiator with plastic end-tanks. It is a robust system, but heavy work \u2014 towing, sustained low-range crawling, or desert running in summer \u2014 pushes it harder than the duty cycle a half-ton sees. Knowing the real capacity and coolant spec keeps you from the two mistakes that cause trouble here: topping off with the wrong (orange HOAT) coolant, and chasing a \"phantom\" overheat that is really a brittle degas bottle leaking under pressure.\n\nUse Mopar OAT coolant, purple, meeting **MS.90032**. Do not mix it with the older orange HOAT (MS-9769) that earlier Hemi trucks used \u2014 mixing organic-acid and hybrid coolants drops the corrosion protection and can gel. Total system capacity on the 6.4L is about **12 quarts**; a basic radiator drain-and-fill recovers roughly 6-7 quarts, so a true flush takes two or three fill-burp cycles to fully exchange the fluid. Buy premixed 50/50 or cut concentrate with distilled water \u2014 never tap water.\n\nLet the engine sit overnight so it is stone cold. Open the radiator cap (on the degas bottle), then open the lower drain or pull the lower radiator hose into your pan. Once drained, reconnect, fill the degas bottle to the cold line, and run the engine with the heater on full hot. The thermostat opens around operating temperature and the level will drop \u2014 keep topping the bottle. The 6.4L is prone to trapping air, so watch for the level to stabilize and the heater to blow hot before you call it done. A spill-free funnel makes the burp far cleaner.\n\nThe **degas (overflow) bottle** goes brittle and cracks at the seams, usually around 80-120k miles; a hairline crack vents pressure and mimics an overheat. Replace it on sight if it is yellowed or weeping. Check the **radiator end-tanks** where the plastic crimps to the aluminum core \u2014 seepage there means the radiator is on its way out. Squeeze the upper and lower **hoses** cold; if they feel rock-hard or mushy, replace the set. Finally, verify the **fan clutch** engages \u2014 a lazy clutch is a common cause of overheating only at low speed or while towing.\n\nFor the broader fluid-service picture on this truck \u2014 engine oil, axle, transfer case, and transmission \u2014 pair this with the maintenance schedule before a big trip.\n\nBefore you tear into the cooling system, confirm the gauge is telling the truth. A cracked degas bottle or a failing cap vents pressure and drops the boiling point, so the truck runs hot only under load \u2014 pressure-test the cap and bottle to about **16 psi** and watch for a steady hold. A genuine overheat climbs steadily and does not recover with airflow: suspect a thermostat stuck closed, a lazy fan clutch, or an airbound system from a sloppy burp. An infrared thermometer swept across the radiator face shows cold spots where the core is clogged. Pull codes with the [OBD-II guide](/db/power-wagon/pw-obd2-diagnostics/) \u2014 a P0128 (coolant below regulating temp) or P0217 (engine over-temp) tells you which way the fault runs before you spend a dollar.\n\nStock cooling handles stock duty, but a heavily loaded truck crawling in summer benefits from a few targeted changes. A **severe-duty fan clutch** engages sooner and moves more air at low speed \u2014 exactly when a crawling Power Wagon struggles and highway airflow is gone. Extending the **axle vent tubes** does not cool the engine, but it keeps water out of the diffs on the same trips that stress cooling, so do both before a desert or mud run. For trucks that tow near the **10,000-lb-plus** range, an auxiliary transmission cooler offloads heat the radiator would otherwise absorb. Keep fresh coolant on schedule from the [full fluid-service list](/db/power-wagon/pw-fluids-service/) so corrosion never narrows the passages in the first place.","pros":["Cheap insurance: $60 of fluid versus a warped head from one overheat on the trail","OAT coolant is long-life \u2014 a correct fill lasts years","Most failure points (bottle, hoses) are visible and cheap to pre-empt"],"cons":["The 6.4L traps air; a sloppy burp leaves you with a phantom overheat","Wrong coolant (orange HOAT) ruins the corrosion package \u2014 read the bottle","A full flush needs multiple cycles, not one drain"]},{"id":"pw-regear-456","title":"Re-gear from 4.10 to 4.56 for 37s on Power Wagon","vehicle":"power-wagon","category":"drivetrain","subcategory":"gearing","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":5,"time_hours":{"low":8,"high":16},"cost_usd":{"low":1400,"high":2400},"tools_required":["bearing puller","case spreader (or shop press)","dial indicator","torque wrench (250 lb-ft)","shop press for carrier bearings","patience"],"skills_required":["advanced driveline work \u2014 most owners pay a shop for this"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["2014-2018","2019-2024","2025+"],"tags":["regear","gearing","drivetrain","axle-shaft","premium"],"parts":[{"name":"Yukon 4.56 Ring & Pinion Set \u2014 AAM 11.5\" Rear (PW)","part_number":"YG-AAM115-456","vendor":"Yukon","price_estimate_usd":380},{"name":"Yukon 4.56 Ring & Pinion Set \u2014 AAM 9.25\" Front (PW)","part_number":"YG-AAM925-456","vendor":"Yukon","price_estimate_usd":360},{"name":"Yukon Master Install Kit \u2014 Front + Rear (bearings, races, shims, seals)","vendor":"Yukon","price_estimate_usd":580},{"name":"Professional install (shop labor for both diffs)","vendor":"local gear shop","price_estimate_usd":1200}],"citations":[{"title":"Yukon Gear & Axle \u2014 AAM 11.5 and 9.25 ring & pinion applications","url":"https://yukongear.com/"},{"title":"American Axle (AAM) \u2014 9.25 solid front / 11.5 rear axle service specifications","url":"https://www.aam.com/"},{"title":"Power Wagon Registry \u2014 re-gear and tire-size discussion threads","url":"https://www.pwregistry.org/"}],"alternatives":["pw-tires-mt","pw-locker-upgrade"],"summary":"37s on the 2014+ Power Wagon's factory 4.10 gears make a soft, slow, fuel-hungry truck. 4.56s restore the factory 33\"-tire feel \u2014 throttle response, low-range crawl ratio, transmission shift points. On the 6.4 HEMI, 4.30s are acceptable and 4.56s are better. Older 2005-2013 trucks left the factory with the 5.7 HEMI behind 4.56 gears already, so their regear conversation starts at 4.88.","description":"The 2014-and-later Power Wagon comes from the factory with 4.10:1 gears in the front AAM 9.25\" axle and rear AAM 11.5\" axle, behind the 6.4L HEMI. (The 2005-2013 truck paired the 5.7L HEMI with 4.56 gears and an AAM 10.5\" rear \u2014 this guide's ratios apply to the 4.10 trucks.) With factory 33\" tires, that gives a comfortable crawl ratio and acceptable highway RPM. Bump tires up to 37\" and:\n\n- Effective ratio drops from 4.10 to ~3.61 (multiply factory gear by 33/37)\n- Throttle response gets sluggish, most noticeably at altitude and under load\n- Transmission hunts for gears, dragging MPG and shift quality\n- Low-range crawl ratio drops from 73:1 to ~64:1 \u2014 noticeable on technical climbs\n\nRe-gearing to 4.56:1 restores the 33\"-tire feel with 37s. The math: 4.56 / 4.10 = 1.112x, which exactly offsets the 37/33 = 1.121x tire growth. Within 1% \u2014 you can't tell the difference from factory.\n\n**For the 6.4 HEMI (2014+, factory 4.10):** 4.30 is acceptable, 4.56 is better. The 6.4's torque masks some of the lost gearing, but for sustained high-altitude crawling or towing, 4.56 is the right call.\n\n**For the 5.7 HEMI (2005-2013, factory 4.56):** you already have 4.56s. Going to 37s on that truck means 4.88s to restore the factory feel, and the older AAM 10.5\" rear limits ratio availability more than the 11.5\" does \u2014 confirm the ring and pinion is offered for your axle before planning the job.\n\n**Cost breakdown:**\n- Front + rear ring & pinion sets: $740 (Yukon or similar)\n- Master install kit (bearings, races, shims, seals, marking compound): $580\n- Shop labor (both diffs): $1,200 \u2014 most owners pay; this is not a DIY job for the average wrenching skill level\n- Total: ~$2,500 done\n\n**DIY considerations:** Setting up a ring and pinion correctly requires:\n- A case spreader or shop press for carrier bearings\n- A dial indicator for backlash measurement (0.005-0.008\" target on AAM)\n- Marking compound for tooth-contact pattern verification\n- Pinion-depth shim experimentation (3-5 attempts is normal)\n- Patience \u2014 first-time installers should expect to disassemble and re-shim 2-3 times\n\nIf you've never set up a ring and pinion before, the AAM 11.5\" rear is NOT a good first-time job. Pay a shop. The $1,200 labor saves a $740 ring & pinion that you'd otherwise scrap from a bad install.\n\nEven if you pay for it, knowing the sequence tells you whether the shop is doing it right and where your money goes.\n\n1. **Both diffs come apart.** The AAM 9.25 solid front and the AAM 11.5 rear are pulled down, old ring & pinion and carrier bearings removed. The factory locker carrier is reused \u2014 you are changing gears, not the locking hardware.\n2. **Pinion depth is set first.** The installer presses on a pinion bearing, installs a starting shim, and reads the tooth-contact pattern with marking compound. This is the step that takes time; 3-5 shim iterations is normal even for a pro.\n3. **Backlash is dialed in.** Carrier shims move the ring gear until backlash measures 0.005-0.008\" on the AAM, verified with a dial indicator. Pattern and backlash get cross-checked until both are in spec.\n4. **Pinion preload is set** with the crush sleeve (or solid spacer), then the whole thing is buttoned up with new seals.\n5. **The other axle gets the identical ratio.** Front and rear MUST match \u2014 a 4.56 rear behind a 4.10 front binds the driveline in 4WD and destroys parts.\n6. **Speedometer is corrected** afterward, either at the dealer or with a tuner cable (~$150), or your odometer and shift logic stay off.\n\nIf you have set up a ring & pinion before, the 11.5 rear is doable; if you have not, this is the wrong axle to learn on \u2014 see the parts list and the [[pw-tires-mt]] tire decision that drives the whole choice.\n\nThe single most expensive mistake is mismatched ratios front to rear \u2014 it feels fine in 2WD and then binds and grinds the moment you engage 4WD on dry pavement, and by then the damage is done. Verify both axles got the same gear before the truck leaves the shop. The second is a rushed pattern: a contact pattern that is close but not centered will whine at cruise and wear early, so do not accept \"good enough\" on the marking-compound check. For a DIY attempt, the realistic failure is scrapping a $740 gear set on a bad first install \u2014 which is exactly why the $1,200 labor is cheap insurance for most owners.","pros":["Restores factory drivability with 37\" tires","Improves MPG by 1-2 (counter-intuitive but true \u2014 engine works less hard at cruise)","Required for proper transmission shift logic on any 37\" build","Re-uses the factory locker hardware (no other axle changes needed)"],"cons":["$2,000-2,500 done at a shop","Both diffs must be re-geared together (mismatched ratios = drivetrain bind in 4WD)","DIY is a steep learning curve; failed first attempts are common","Adds ~150 RPM at 65 mph cruise \u2014 slight highway noise increase","Speedometer correction needed (or use a tuner like a HP Tuners cable, $150)"]},{"id":"pw-alternator","title":"Alternator and Charging System Service \u2014 Power Wagon","vehicle":"power-wagon","category":"electrical","subcategory":"charging","type":"procedure","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":3},"cost_usd":{"low":180,"high":500},"tools_required":["multimeter (DC volts + amps)","metric sockets + ratchet","serpentine belt tool","torque wrench","battery terminal brush","insulated gloves"],"skills_required":["charging-system diagnosis","voltage-drop testing"],"build_tiers":["stock","mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["2014-2018","2019-2024","2025+"],"tags":["electrical","alternator","charging","battery","safety","voltage-drop"],"parts":[{"name":"220A alternator (6.4L, e.g. P/N 56029644AB)","part_number":"56029644AB","vendor":"Mopar / Denso / Remy","price_estimate_usd":280},{"name":"Serpentine belt (replace if glazed/cracked)","vendor":"Gates / Mopar","price_estimate_usd":45},{"name":"Heavy-gauge charge wire (2-gauge, for HD upgrade)","vendor":"Genuine marine-grade tinned","price_estimate_usd":40}],"citations":[{"title":"Mopar / aftermarket reference \u2014 Ram 2500 6.4L 220A alternator P/N 56029644","url":"https://www.rareelectrical.com/i-26934905-new-220-amp-alternator-fits-ram-2500-6-4l-v8-2016-2018-56029-644ab-56029644aa.html"},{"title":"RamForum \u2014 6.4L alternator upgrade and charge-wire gauge","url":"https://www.ramforum.com/threads/alternator-upgrade-on-6-4-hemi.156048/"}],"alternatives":["pw-dual-battery","pw-obd2-diagnostics"],"summary":"The 6.4L Power Wagon ships with a **220-amp alternator** (P/N in the 56029644 family) \u2014 plenty for a stock truck, but it can run short once you add a winch, light bar, air compressor, and a fridge. Diagnose a charging complaint with a multimeter before you buy anything: a healthy system shows roughly **13.8-14.7 V** at the battery with the engine running. This is a safety-critical electrical job \u2014 clean every connection, torque the output stud, and never work the charge cable with the battery connected.","description":"A charging-system fault on a trail truck is more than an inconvenience: lose the alternator miles from pavement and you are running on battery alone with a winch you may need. Knowing how to test the system, and how to size it for your accessory load, keeps you from both a dead-truck recovery and the temptation to throw a parts-store alternator at a problem that is really a corroded ground.\n\nStart at the battery with the engine off \u2014 a good battery rests near 12.6 V. Start the engine and measure again: **13.8-14.7 V** indicates the alternator is charging. A reading stuck at battery voltage (around 12.x V) means the alternator is not charging; a reading climbing above 15 V means an overcharge (regulator) fault. Before condemning the alternator, do a **voltage-drop test**: probe between the alternator output stud and the battery positive while the system is loaded \u2014 more than a few tenths of a volt of drop means a bad cable or ground, not a bad alternator. The Power Wagon's charge path and grounds corrode, and a clean-and-retorque often fixes a \"weak alternator\" for free.\n\nDisconnect the negative battery cable first \u2014 the alternator output stud is always hot. Release the serpentine belt with the tensioner tool (inspect the belt while it is off; replace it if glazed or cracked). Unbolt the alternator, transfer the output cable and field connector to the new unit, and torque the output stud to spec \u2014 a loose output stud arcs, overheats, and melts the connection. Route the belt per the diagram, reconnect the battery, and re-test charging voltage under load.\n\nIf you run a winch plus lighting plus a compressor and a fridge, the factory 220 A and its charge wire become the bottleneck. The proven path is a higher-output alternator fed by an upgraded **2-gauge** charge wire (the factory wire is too small for sustained high output) and a corresponding fuse upgrade. For sustained winching or a camp-power habit, combine the bigger alternator with a dual-battery setup so the starting battery is protected. Plan the wiring as a system \u2014 upgrading the alternator without upgrading the cable only moves the bottleneck.\n\nBefore you buy a bigger alternator, add up what you actually run at once. A winch pulls **300-400+ amps** in a hard recovery but only for seconds, so it leans on the **battery** far more than the alternator; steady loads are what tax charging. A typical build draws roughly: LED light bar **8-15 A**, ARB-style compressor **20-40 A** while filling, 12 V fridge **3-6 A** cycling, cab and trail lighting **10-20 A**. Sum those and add a **30% headroom** margin \u2014 most trucks running lights-plus-fridge-plus-occasional-compressor stay under the factory **220 A** at cruise, which is why the first fix is usually cleaning grounds, not a new alternator. You genuinely outgrow the stock unit when you add **sustained** high-draw gear (a welder, an inverter over ~2 kW, or long-duration winching), and at that point the [dual-battery build](/db/power-wagon/pw-dual-battery/) matters more than raw alternator amps because it buffers the surges.\n\nThe factory charge path is the quiet limiter on these trucks. The stock output cable is sized for stock loads, so bolting a 320 A alternator to a thin wire only moves the choke point and heats the cable. If you upgrade output, run a matched **2-gauge (or 1/0 for big inverter builds) charge wire** with a fuse at the battery sized to the cable, and add a **second engine-to-frame and frame-to-body ground strap** \u2014 voltage-drop faults on the 2500 are as often a corroded body ground as a cable. Re-run the voltage-drop test after any upgrade: probe alternator-stud-to-battery-positive and battery-negative-to-engine-block under load, and chase anything over **0.2 V**. Keep grounds clean and torqued, because a high-output alternator forcing current through a marginal ground is what cooks connectors and back-feeds noise into the electronics.","pros":["A multimeter tells you in two minutes whether it is the alternator or a cable","The factory 220 A is robust for a stock truck and cheap to test","A planned high-output upgrade supports winch-plus-camp electrical loads"],"cons":["Many \"dead alternators\" are really corroded grounds \u2014 test voltage-drop first","The output stud is always hot; disconnect the battery and torque it properly","Upgrading output without upgrading the 2-gauge charge wire moves the bottleneck"]},{"id":"pw-dual-battery","title":"Dual Battery and Auxiliary Power Wiring \u2014 Power Wagon","vehicle":"power-wagon","category":"electrical","subcategory":"dual-battery","type":"upgrade","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":3,"high":8},"cost_usd":{"low":250,"high":900},"tools_required":["multimeter","crimping tool for large-gauge lugs","metric sockets + ratchet","drill + step bit (for tray mounting)","heat-shrink + torch","wire loom + fuse holders"],"skills_required":["high-current wiring","fusing and circuit protection","secure mechanical mounting"],"build_tiers":["overland","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["2014-2018","2019-2024","2025+"],"tags":["electrical","dual-battery","auxiliary-power","overland","winch","fridge","safety"],"parts":[{"name":"Dual-battery tray / mount kit (Power Wagon / Ram 2500 fitment)","vendor":"Genesis Offroad / sPOD","price_estimate_usd":350},{"name":"Deep-cycle / AGM auxiliary battery (Group 65 or kit-specified)","vendor":"Odyssey / Northstar","price_estimate_usd":250},{"name":"DC-DC charger or smart isolator (voltage-sensing relay)","vendor":"Redarc / National Luna / Blue Sea","price_estimate_usd":200},{"name":"2/0 and 4-gauge cable, lugs, and ANL fuses","vendor":"Blue Sea Systems","price_estimate_usd":120}],"citations":[{"title":"Genesis Offroad \u2014 Ram 2500/Power Wagon dual battery kit fitment","url":"https://genesisoffroad.com/"},{"title":"Blue Sea Systems \u2014 dual-battery isolation and circuit protection guide","url":"https://www.bluesea.com/resources"}],"alternatives":["pw-alternator","pw-winch-upgrade"],"summary":"A second battery is the upgrade that lets you run a winch, fridge, and camp lights without waking up to a truck that will not start. The clean way to do it on a Power Wagon is a fitment-specific tray, an AGM auxiliary battery, and a **DC-DC charger or smart isolator** so the house load can never drain your starting battery. Budget $250 for a basic isolated setup, up to $900 for a DC-DC system with heavy cabling. This is high-current, safety-critical wiring: fuse every cable at the source and torque every lug.","description":"The Power Wagon's engine bay has room for a second battery, and the factory 220 A alternator can support a properly managed dual setup. The point of the system is isolation \u2014 the starting battery stays untouched so the truck always cranks, while the auxiliary battery feeds the fridge, lights, inverter, and air compressor. Done wrong, a hard-wired second battery drains both and leaves you stranded; done right, it is the backbone of an overland electrical system.\n\nA **voltage-sensing relay (smart isolator)** connects the two batteries only when the alternator is charging, then separates them when the engine is off \u2014 cheap, proven, and enough for moderate loads. A **DC-DC charger** does more: it charges the auxiliary battery on a correct multi-stage profile regardless of the alternator's behavior, which matters for AGM and lithium house batteries and for newer trucks whose smart charging varies output voltage. For a fridge-and-camp build, the DC-DC path protects battery life and is worth the extra cost; for a winch-backup-only build, an isolator is adequate.\n\nUse a tray built for the Ram 2500 / Power Wagon so the battery is bolted down hard \u2014 a loose battery in an off-road truck is a fire and acid hazard. Run heavy cable (2/0 from the alternator/main battery, 4-gauge for accessory feeds) in protective loom away from heat and chafe points. **Fuse every cable within a few inches of its power source** with an ANL or MEGA fuse sized to the wire \u2014 an unfused battery cable that shorts on the frame is a fire. Crimp and heat-shrink every lug; do not rely on hardware-store butt connectors for high current.\n\nFeed the fridge, light bar, and compressor from the auxiliary battery through a fused distribution block or a switch panel, not straight off the post. Keep the winch on the starting battery (or a heavy combined feed when winching) so it has full cranking-battery current available. Re-check voltage at both batteries with the engine running and under load to confirm the isolator/charger is doing its job before you trust it on a trip.\n\nDo the amp-hour math before buying the battery, not after. A 12 V fridge averages 1\u20132.5 A depending on ambient heat, so an overnight stop costs 15\u201330 Ah; add camp lights (0.5\u20131 A) and phone/radio charging and a realistic weekend budget is 60\u201390 Ah between charges. A Group 65 AGM delivers roughly 70\u201375 Ah \u2014 but drawing an AGM below 50% regularly kills it, so usable capacity is nearer 35 Ah. That covers one night with margin, not two. If the itinerary is multi-night with little driving, either step up capacity (a larger tray-compatible AGM, or lithium with a DC-DC charger) or plan on 30\u201345 minutes of engine runtime per day \u2014 at highway alternator output, that restores a typical overnight draw. Log actual loads with a cheap inline DC meter on the first trip; measured numbers beat estimates.\n\nThe factory Warn winch is the wildcard load this platform adds. Keep the winch on the starting battery: it is engineered around cranking-battery current delivery, and isolating it from the house system means a long winching session can never leave the fridge circuit flat \u2014 see the [factory winch service guide](/db/power-wagon/factory-warn-winch-service/) for its electrical connections, and the [winch upgrade guide](/db/power-wagon/pw-winch-upgrade/) if a synthetic-rope or higher-capacity unit is in the plan. Winch hard with the engine running so the 220 A alternator carries most of the draw. Under the hood, the Hemi bay runs hot \u2014 that is why the auxiliary battery should be AGM (or the lithium relocated out of the bay entirely; most lithium batteries derate or fault above roughly 140\u00b0F charging temperature). Finally, after the install, verify charging behavior at idle and at cruise with the truck's parasitic loads on; if the auxiliary battery chronically undercharges, check output at the [alternator](/db/power-wagon/pw-alternator/) before blaming the isolator.","pros":["The starting battery is protected \u2014 the truck always cranks","Supports fridge, lights, inverter, and compressor for overland use","A DC-DC charger keeps AGM/lithium house batteries healthy long-term"],"cons":["High-current wiring done wrong is a genuine fire risk \u2014 fuse at the source","A DC-DC system adds real cost over a basic isolator","Battery weight and mounting must be solid for off-road use"]},{"id":"5-7-hemi-mds-disable-power-wagon","title":"5.7L Hemi MDS Lifter Failure on the Power Wagon: What It Sounds Like and What to Do","vehicle":"power-wagon","category":"engine","subcategory":"valvetrain","type":"diagnostic","difficulty":4,"time_hours":{"low":8.0,"high":20.0},"cost_usd":{"low":400,"high":3500},"tools_required":["complete socket and wrench set","valve cover gasket set","camshaft removal tools","torque wrench","engine assembly lube","OBD scanner"],"skills_required":["engine disassembly","valvetrain familiarity","torque-to-yield bolt understanding"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2005-2009","2010-2018","2019-present"],"tags":["hemi","mds","lifters","engine","reliability","valvetrain","MDS-delete"],"parts":[{"name":"Non-MDS lifter set (16 lifters)","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":280},{"name":"MDS delete kit with camshaft","vendor":"Dorman","price_estimate_usd":420},{"name":"Camshaft (non-MDS profile)","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":350},{"name":"Lifter retainer kit","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":45},{"name":"Valley cover with plugs (non-MDS)","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":85}],"citations":[{"title":"NHTSA complaints \u2014 5.7L Hemi MDS lifter failures","url":"https://www.nhtsa.gov"},{"title":"Chrysler Technical Service Bulletin on Hemi lifter noise","url":"https://www.mopar.com"}],"alternatives":["pw-spark-plugs","pw-oil-change"],"summary":"MDS lifter failure on the 5.7L Hemi is a real problem across all Ram and Chrysler vehicles \u2014 the Power Wagon is no exception. Knowing the early symptoms buys you time to plan. Ignoring them costs you an engine.","description":"The Multi-Displacement System (MDS) is Chrysler's cylinder deactivation technology, present on every 5.7L Hemi from 2005 onward. In cruise conditions, MDS deactivates cylinders 1, 4, 6, and 7 by collapsing the lifters on those cylinders \u2014 the pushrods retract, the valves stay closed, and those cylinders stop firing. The fuel economy benefit is real but modest.\n\nThe problem: the MDS lifters that collapse and expand repeatedly eventually fail. When they fail, they typically stick in the collapsed position. The cylinder stops firing permanently. The engine runs on seven cylinders, throws a misfire code, and eventually the lifter body can disintegrate \u2014 sending debris through the oiling system and into the remaining engine.\n\nMDS lifter failures cluster between 80,000 and 120,000 miles, though some have failed earlier and many trucks never fail at all. The failure rate is high enough to warrant proactive attention at 80k \u2014 this isn't a rare edge case. It's well-documented across Ram 1500, Ram 2500/3500 (where MDS is less common but still present), Jeep Grand Cherokee, and Dodge Challenger/Charger applications with the same engine.\n\n**Early stage:** A subtle tick on startup that clears within 30\u201360 seconds of warm oil circulation. This can be dismissed as cold-start noise \u2014 it often is \u2014 but if it becomes more frequent or takes longer to clear, the MDS lifters are the first suspect.\n\n**Mid stage:** A more persistent tick or tap at idle that doesn't fully clear. You may or may not get a misfire code at this point. Oil pressure reads normal. The truck still runs, but the sound is clearly coming from the valvetrain.\n\n**Late stage:** A misfire code on one of the MDS cylinders (P0301, P0304, P0306, P0307, or similar). The engine runs rough at light throttle. At this point the lifter has likely collapsed and stuck. If you hear a sudden metallic knock rather than a tick, the lifter body may have started to disintegrate \u2014 this requires immediate shutdown and towing.\n\n**Option 1: Replace the failed lifters only.** This works if caught early \u2014 replace the collapsed lifters with OEM-spec non-MDS lifters and the matched camshaft. The MDS system will be non-functional (it won't try to deactivate cylinders) but the engine runs. This is the least expensive path if the failure is caught before debris spreads.\n\n**Option 2: Full MDS delete.** Replace all 16 lifters with non-MDS units, swap the camshaft for a non-MDS cam profile, install valley cover plugs, and tune the ECM to disable the MDS system in software. A full delete eliminates the failure mode entirely. Cost for parts is $700\u2013$900 DIY; shop labor for the full job runs $1,200\u2013$2,500 additional depending on the shop.\n\n**Option 3: Replace the engine.** If debris has spread through the oil passages \u2014 visible in the oil as metallic glitter, or confirmed by a borescope inspection of the other cylinders \u2014 the engine needs to come out. A reman 5.7L Hemi from a reputable rebuilder runs $2,500\u2013$3,500 plus installation.\n\nThe MDS delete requires: all 16 lifters swapped to non-MDS units, the camshaft replaced with a non-MDS cam (because the MDS cam has different lobe profiles on the deactivation cylinders), the valley cover holes plugged (the MDS oil passages are plugged off), and an ECM tune to disable the MDS in software. The ECM tune matters \u2014 without it, the computer will continue trying to collapse lifters that no longer collapse, which triggers codes and potentially confuses fuel trims.\n\nThe mechanical work requires removing both valve covers, the intake manifold, and access to the valley. It's not a beginner job \u2014 you're handling a camshaft, which means understanding cam-to-crank timing marks, torque-to-yield bolts, and assembly lube requirements. If you're not comfortable with valvetrain work, this is a shop job.","steps":["Scan for active misfire codes \u2014 P030x codes on MDS cylinders confirm suspected failure","Check oil for metallic particles \u2014 drain a small sample into a white container and look","Listen for the tick pattern (startup only vs. persistent vs. knock)","Decide: lifter-only replacement, full MDS delete, or engine replacement based on damage assessment","For MDS delete: remove intake, remove valve covers, replace lifters and cam, plug valley passages, install new valley cover, ECM tune","Verify oil pressure and check for tick at next startup after repair"],"pros":["MDS delete eliminates the failure mode permanently","Non-MDS lifters are available OEM and aftermarket","The 5.7L Hemi is otherwise a durable long-lived engine"],"cons":["Full MDS delete requires camshaft replacement and ECM tune \u2014 not a straightforward parts swap","If caught late with debris in the oil, cleaning the passages fully is difficult","ECM tune is necessary but adds cost and requires a trusted tuner for Power Wagon calibrations"]},{"id":"pw-fuel-pump","title":"Fuel Pump and Filter Service \u2014 Power Wagon","vehicle":"power-wagon","category":"fuel","subcategory":"fuel-pump","type":"procedure","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":5},"cost_usd":{"low":250,"high":600},"tools_required":["fuel-pressure test gauge (Schrader-port adapter or in-line)","floor jack and rated stands","transmission/tank support jack OR a second set of hands","metric sockets and ratchet","lock-ring tool or brass drift","rags and a drain container"],"skills_required":["fuel-system safety (depressurize, no ignition sources)","patience dropping or supporting the tank"],"build_tiers":["stock","mild_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2014-2018","2019-2024","2025+"],"tags":["fuel","fuel-pump","no-start","long-crank","maintenance"],"parts":[{"name":"Complete in-tank fuel pump module (pump + sender + strainer)","vendor":"Mopar / Delphi / Spectra","price_estimate_usd":280},{"name":"Tank lock-ring seal / gasket","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":18},{"name":"Fuel-pressure test gauge kit","vendor":"OTC / Mityvac","price_estimate_usd":45}],"citations":[{"title":"Mopar parts catalog \u2014 Ram 2500 6.4L fuel pump module assembly","url":"https://www.moparpartsgiant.com/"},{"title":"RamForum \u2014 gas Hemi returnless fuel system / no serviceable inline filter","url":"https://www.ramforum.com/"}],"alternatives":["pw-obd2-diagnostics","pw-fluids-service"],"summary":"The gas 6.4L (and earlier 5.7L) Power Wagon uses a returnless EFI system with the pump, pressure regulator, and filter all built into a single in-tank module \u2014 there is no serviceable inline fuel filter to swap. When you get long cranks, hard hot-starts, or a no-start with a dead fuel-pump prime, you measure rail pressure first and replace the whole module if it is low. Budget around $280 for a quality module and the better part of an afternoon, because the tank has to come down.","description":"A lot of owners go looking for a \"fuel filter\" on this truck and never find one. On the gas Hemi the filter is the in-tank strainer/sock that comes as part of the pump module \u2014 Ram designed it as a lifetime, non-serviceable assembly. That changes the service strategy: you do not do a routine filter swap, you diagnose pressure and replace the module when it fails. The good news is the system is reliable; the catch is that the job means dropping or substantially lowering a long, often-full tank under a heavy frame.\n\nSymptoms that point at the pump module are extended cranking before start, hard restarts when hot, stumbling under load, or a no-start where you cannot hear the two-second pump prime when you cycle the key to RUN. Before buying parts, put a gauge on the fuel rail's Schrader test port (or splice an in-line gauge) and key on: a healthy gas Hemi should build to spec (roughly 58 psi class, verify against the service manual) and hold pressure after shutoff. Low or slowly-bleeding pressure with the engine off can be the pump, a leaking regulator inside the module, or a failing check valve \u2014 all of which live in the same module, so the fix is the same. Pull codes too; a P0order in the P0230 family supports a pump-circuit fault.\n\nRun the tank as low as you can before you start \u2014 a full tank is heavy and dangerous to support. Relieve fuel pressure (pull the pump relay and crank briefly, or follow the manual's procedure), then disconnect the battery. Raise the truck on rated stands. Support the tank on a transmission jack, remove the straps and the filler/vent and electrical connections, and lower it enough to reach the top. Rotate the lock ring off with a brass drift or lock-ring tool, lift the old module out carefully (the float arm bends), set the new seal, and drop the new module in clocked the same way. Reconnect, raise the tank, and cycle the key several times to prime before cranking.\n\nRe-check rail pressure with the gauge, then look for weeping at the lock ring and lines under pressure before lowering the truck. Clear any stored codes. If the original failure was contamination (a tank of bad fuel), inspect the strainer condition and consider a tank clean-out so you are not feeding debris into the new module.\n\nThe module itself is the whole cost story. A quality complete assembly \u2014 **Mopar OE, Delphi, or Spectra** \u2014 runs about **$220-320**; the lock-ring seal is another **$15-20**, and a fuel-pressure gauge you'll reuse is **$40-50**. Doing it yourself, call it **$280 in parts and an afternoon**. A shop charges **$500-700** for the same job because the labor is the tank drop, not the swap. Skip the cheapest no-name modules \u2014 a $90 pump that fails in a year means dropping the tank twice, which erases every dollar saved. If the truck is high-mileage, doing the pump also gets the aging **strainer sock** replaced as part of the module, which is a quiet reliability win.\n\nMost Power Wagon pump failures are either high-mileage wear or **contamination**, and the second is partly in your control. Don't run the tank down to fumes as a habit \u2014 the fuel in the tank cools and lubricates the in-tank pump, and chronic near-empty running shortens its life. On a truck that fords water, make sure the tank **vent and filler are sealed and routed high** so a deep crossing doesn't pull water past a bad seal into the tank; if you find water or debris, plan a tank clean-out before it reaches the new module. Pair a suspected fuel issue with a code scan from the [OBD-II diagnostic guide](/db/power-wagon/pw-obd2-diagnostics/) so you separate a pump-circuit fault from a plugged strainer, and keep the whole fuel-and-ignition side honest with the [fluid-service schedule](/db/power-wagon/pw-fluids-service/).","pros":["One assembly fixes pump, regulator, and strainer faults at once","The system is otherwise reliable \u2014 there is no routine filter to maintain","Diagnosing pressure first avoids throwing parts at a relay or check-valve fault"],"cons":["No serviceable inline filter \u2014 the whole module is the service item","Dropping a long HD tank is the real work; do it with the tank near empty","Fuel work demands strict safety: depressurize, ventilate, no ignition sources"]},{"id":"pw-interior-upgrades","title":"Interior Upgrades and Sound Deadening \u2014 Power Wagon Cab","vehicle":"power-wagon","category":"interior","subcategory":"comfort","type":"upgrade","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":14},"cost_usd":{"low":80,"high":1200},"tools_required":["trim removal tools","panel clips assortment","utility knife / scissors","roller (for sound mat)","heat gun (for MLV/foam fitment)","trim panel pry bars","shop vacuum","socket set (seat bolts)"],"skills_required":["careful trim removal without breaking clips","patience layering deadening material","basic 12V wiring"],"build_tiers":["overland","daily","moderate_trail"],"year_compatibility":["2014-2018","2019-2024","2025+"],"tags":["interior","sound-deadening","noise","floor-liners","storage","overland"],"parts":[{"name":"Constrained-layer damper mat (butyl, ~36-50 sq ft)","vendor":"Kilmat / Noico / Dynamat","price_estimate_usd":120},{"name":"Closed-cell foam decoupler (1/8 in, ~36 sq ft)","vendor":"Second Skin / SoundSkins","price_estimate_usd":90},{"name":"Mass-loaded vinyl barrier, 1 lb/sq ft (~36 sq ft)","vendor":"Second Skin / TMS","price_estimate_usd":180},{"name":"All-weather floor liners, front + rear","vendor":"WeatherTech / Husky X-act","price_estimate_usd":180},{"name":"Switch panel / auxiliary control (8-circuit, fused, relay-driven)","vendor":"sPOD / Switch-Pros","price_estimate_usd":350},{"name":"Under-seat storage organizer","vendor":"DU-HA / Husky","price_estimate_usd":130}],"citations":[{"title":"Second Skin Audio \u2014 automotive sound-deadening layering guide","url":"https://www.secondskinaudio.com/sound-deadening/"},{"title":"RamForum \u2014 2500 cab sound-deadening and interior build threads","url":"https://www.ramforum.com/"},{"title":"sPOD \u2014 switch panel installation and airbag clearance notes","url":"https://4x4spod.com/pages/installation"}],"alternatives":["pw-dual-battery","pw-overland-drawer"],"summary":"Start with honest expectations. A well-executed floor and firewall treatment on this cab is worth roughly **3-6 dB** of perceived road noise \u2014 noticeable, worth the money, not silence. If aggressive mud-terrains are on the truck, they are contributing more than any material you can install, and swapping to a quieter all-terrain will beat a full deadening job for less money. Once that is settled, the order of value is floor liners, targeted damping on the floor and firewall, then a switch panel if you run accessories. Spend $80 and an afternoon, or $1,200 and two weekends.","description":"Cabin noise in a Power Wagon comes from three places: tires, exhaust and drivetrain, and wind over a tall body. Owners reliably attack the third and cheapest-to-hear source and ignore the first.\n\nKnobby mud-terrains generate a broadband roar that rises with speed and gets worse as the tread wears into a saw-tooth pattern. If highway comfort matters more to you than mud performance, read the [all-terrain tire guide](/db/power-wagon/pw-tires-at/) against the [mud-terrain guide](/db/power-wagon/pw-tires-mt/) before you buy a single square foot of butyl. Regular rotation matters too \u2014 irregular wear is what turns a merely loud tire into an unbearable one.\n\nThat is not an argument against deadening. It is an argument for doing the free diagnosis first.\n\nEach noise mechanism wants a different material, and using the wrong one is why some expensive jobs disappoint.\n\n**1. Butyl constrained-layer damper (CLD).** Kills panel resonance and rattle \u2014 door skins, floor pan, roof, cab back. You only need **25-40% coverage** on a given panel to break up its resonant modes. Full coverage adds cost and 40-60 lb of weight for almost nothing. Roll it down hard; adhesion is the whole mechanism. Budget **$2-3 per sq ft**.\n\n**2. Closed-cell foam decoupler.** A thin (1/8 in) layer that separates the barrier from the metal so the barrier can float. Skipping it is the most common mistake \u2014 MLV laid directly on steel is mechanically coupled and does much less. Budget **$1.50-2.50 per sq ft**.\n\n**3. Mass-loaded vinyl (MLV) barrier.** This is what actually blocks the low-frequency road roar. It works by mass, at about **1 lb per sq ft**, so a full floor treatment adds **35-50 lb**. Seal the seams \u2014 a gap in a barrier is a hole in a wall. Budget **$4-6 per sq ft**.\n\nLayer in that order, metal outward: damper, foam, MLV, then carpet. Treat in this priority: **floor pan and transmission tunnel first** (biggest gain per hour), then the **firewall**, then the front doors, then the rear doors and cab back. The roof is last and mostly helps with rain drum.\n\n1. Pull the front seats \u2014 four bolts each on the rails, and disconnect the seat wiring harness first. **On a truck with side airbags, disconnect the battery and wait per the factory service information before unplugging any seat connector**, or you risk setting an airbag light that requires a scan tool to clear.\n2. Remove door sill plates, kick panels, and the console. Have a **clip assortment on hand** before you start; you will break some.\n3. Roll the carpet back rather than removing it entirely if you can \u2014 it is far easier to reinstall.\n4. Vacuum thoroughly and wipe with isopropyl alcohol. Butyl will not stick to dust.\n5. Apply CLD to 25-40% of each floor section and roll it firmly until the pattern embosses.\n6. Lay foam over the full area, then MLV cut to fit, seams overlapped and taped.\n7. Cut around every seat bolt hole, harness pass-through, and drain plug **before** the carpet goes back. Trapped water under MLV is how a floor pan rusts from the inside.\n8. Reassemble, torque the seat bolts to spec, reconnect the battery, and confirm no airbag warning light.\n\nPlan **6-10 hours** for a proper floor and firewall job on a crew cab.\n\nAll-weather liners are the best return on the list for a truck that sees mud and water: they trap it, lift out for a hose-down, and protect the carpet you spent a weekend under. **WeatherTech FloorLiner** or **Husky X-act Contour** front-and-rear sets run **$150-220**. Do the liners the same day the carpet goes back.\n\nUnder-seat storage \u2014 a DU-HA-style organizer at **$120-160** \u2014 turns the dead space behind the rear seat into a lockable home for recovery gear, tools, and a first-aid kit. Anything loose in a cab is a projectile in a rollover, which is a real consideration on a truck built to be driven off pavement.\n\nIf you run a light bar, ditch lights, an air compressor, or a fridge, an **sPOD or Switch-Pros** panel (**$350-500**) replaces a nest of inline fuses and toggle switches with one relay-driven, fused, labeled box. Three install rules:\n\n- **Mount clear of airbag deployment zones.** Nothing hard goes on the airbag cover, the A-pillar trim, or the upper dash on a truck with curtain airbags.\n- **Feed it from a fused source at the battery** with correctly sized cable, through a grommet, never through a drilled hole with a bare edge.\n- **Ground it to clean chassis metal**, paint scraped back \u2014 most mystery accessory faults are ground faults.\n\nIf your accessory load includes a fridge or an inverter, plan a [dual-battery system](/db/power-wagon/pw-dual-battery/) so overnight loads never touch the crank battery, and feed cab accessories from a distribution block rather than daisy-chaining off the cigarette socket. For organized, powered storage in the bed, a [slide-out drawer system](/db/power-wagon/pw-overland-drawer/) routes wiring cleanly. If an accessory ever throws a module communication fault after a wiring change, the [OBD-II diagnostics guide](/db/power-wagon/pw-obd2-diagnostics/) covers reading it before you start pulling connectors.\n\nA dark Power Wagon cab bakes, and the tall glass area makes it worse. **Ceramic window tint** (**$300-600** professionally installed) cuts infrared heat load far more than recirculation ever will; front-side legal limits vary by state, so check your current state law rather than the shop's assurance. Add a reflective windshield sunshade (**$25-45**) and breathable seat covers so surfaces stay touchable after the truck sits at a trailhead. The MLV you laid on the firewall for noise also blocks engine-bay heat, so that job pays back twice in a hot climate.\n\n| Upgrade | Cost | Time | Payoff |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| All-weather floor liners | $150-220 | 15 min | Highest per dollar |\n| Quieter tire choice (at next replacement) | $0 extra | \u2014 | Bigger dB reduction than any material |\n| Floor + firewall damper/foam/MLV | $300-450 | 6-10 hr | 3-6 dB, the real gain |\n| Doors (CLD + foam) | $100-150 | 3-4 hr | Better audio, modest road noise gain |\n| Under-seat storage | $120-160 | 30 min | Organization and safety |\n| Switch panel | $350-500 | 3-5 hr | Accessory wiring done once, properly |\n| Ceramic tint | $300-600 | Shop | Cabin heat, hot climates |","pros":["Targeted deadening genuinely quiets the cab without piling on weight","Floor liners protect and clean up a hard-used trail interior fast","A switch panel turns scattered accessory wiring into one tidy, fused system","The firewall barrier reduces heat as well as noise, which matters in desert country"],"cons":["Full-coverage sound mat is wasted money and 40-60 lb of wasted weight","MLV alone is heavy for the benefit \u2014 expect 3-6 dB, not silence","Pulling trim breaks clips; seat removal near side airbags demands the battery-disconnect procedure","A heavy accessory load needs charging-system planning, not a daisy-chain","If mud-terrains are the noise source, no material fixes what the tire is doing"]},{"id":"pw-fluids-service","title":"Differential, Transfer Case, and Transmission Fluid Service \u2014 Power Wagon","vehicle":"power-wagon","category":"maintenance","subcategory":"drivetrain-fluids","type":"procedure","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":4},"cost_usd":{"low":120,"high":350},"tools_required":["fluid transfer pump","metric sockets + Allen/hex fill plugs","torque wrench","drain pans","RTV (for diff covers without gasket)","shop rags"],"skills_required":["fluid service","correct fill levels for axles and t-case"],"build_tiers":["stock","mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["2014-2018","2019-2024","2025+"],"tags":["maintenance","differential","transfer-case","transmission","gear-oil","water-crossing"],"parts":[{"name":"Synthetic gear oil 75W-90 (front AAM 9.25)","vendor":"Mopar / Amsoil / Red Line","price_estimate_usd":30},{"name":"Synthetic gear oil 75W-140 (rear AAM 11.5, towing-rated)","vendor":"Mopar / Amsoil","price_estimate_usd":45},{"name":"ATF+4 (transfer case BW44-47 and 66RFE transmission)","vendor":"Mopar ATF+4","price_estimate_usd":60},{"name":"Friction modifier (if required by locker/diff)","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":12},{"name":"Transmission filter / pan gasket kit (for trans service)","vendor":"Mopar / WIX","price_estimate_usd":45}],"citations":[{"title":"AAM 11.5 rear differential specifications and fluid (Power Wagon axle)","url":"https://www.differentials.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/04/AAM-11.5-11.8-Rear-Differential-Information-Specifications-1.pdf"},{"title":"Mopar ATF+4 fluid specification (transmission / transfer case)","url":"https://www.mopar.com/en-us/my-vehicle/fluids.html"}],"alternatives":["pw-oil-change","front-locker-elocker-troubleshooting"],"summary":"The Power Wagon runs an **AAM 9.25 front** and **AAM 11.5 rear** axle, a **BorgWarner BW44-47** transfer case, and a **66RFE** automatic (some later trucks use an 8-speed \u2014 verify by year). Axles take synthetic gear oil (75W-90 front, 75W-140 rear for towing duty), the transfer case and transmission take **ATF+4**. The reason this service matters more on a Power Wagon than on a street truck: the electronic-locking axles and frequent water crossings make contaminated diff fluid a real risk. Service the diffs after deep water, not on a calendar.","description":"This truck's drivetrain is heavy-duty and reliable, but it earns its keep in mud and water, and gear oil is hygroscopic \u2014 it pulls in water through the axle vents during a crossing. Milky gear oil means water intrusion and an axle that will rust its bearings if you ignore it. A full drivetrain fluid service refreshes all four reservoirs and is the right time to inspect the electronic locker wiring and the axle vents.\n\nThe front **AAM 9.25** and rear **AAM 11.5** both house factory electronic lockers. Pull the diff cover (or drain plug where fitted), inspect the fluid for water (milky) or metal (failing bearings/gears), clean the cover and magnet, and refill: **75W-90** synthetic in the front, and **75W-140** synthetic in the rear for trucks that tow or carry weight. If your locker or limited-slip calls for friction modifier, add it. After deep water crossings, check both diffs for milky oil and service immediately if found \u2014 this is the single most overlooked Power Wagon maintenance item.\n\nThe **BW44-47** transfer case uses **ATF+4** \u2014 drain the case via its plug, refill to the fill-plug level, and verify the shift motor connector is clean. The **66RFE** transmission also runs **ATF+4**; a proper service drops the pan, replaces the filter and pan gasket, and refills to level at temperature. A full fluid exchange takes more than a pan drop, but a pan-and-filter service on the interval is enough for most trucks. Verify your model year's transmission before buying parts \u2014 later Power Wagons may use a different unit and filter.\n\nCalendar intervals assume a street truck. If you cross water above the axle tubes or wheel in mud, inspect the diff fluid afterward and re-service when it shows water. Extending the axle vent tubes higher up the body is a cheap modification that keeps water out of the diffs in the first place \u2014 pair it with this service.\n\nThe diffs and transfer case use a fill-to-the-hole rule: with the truck level, the correct level is the bottom of the fill-plug threads \u2014 overfilling does nothing but push fluid past the pinion seal. The AAM 11.5 rear takes roughly **3.5 quarts**, the AAM 9.25 front about **2.8 quarts**, the BW44-47 transfer case about **2 quarts** of ATF+4, and a 66RFE pan-and-filter service returns roughly **5-6 quarts** (a full exchange is closer to 15). Torque diff-cover bolts in a cross pattern to about **30 ft-lb** and fill/drain plugs to about **24 ft-lb** \u2014 the aluminum threads strip if you lean on them. Use a fresh gasket or a clean RTV bead on covers without a gasket, and let RTV skin over before refilling or it weeps at the seam. If your electronic locker or limited-slip specifies **friction modifier**, add the stated ounces or the clutches chatter on turns.\n\nFactory intervals put axle and transfer-case fluid around **60,000 miles** under normal use and transmission fluid around **60,000-120,000**, but a Power Wagon rarely lives a normal life \u2014 treat deep water crossings and hard towing as interval-enders, not calendar events. DIY cost is roughly **$120-180** in fluid for all four reservoirs; a shop charges **$300-350**, mostly labor. The job pairs naturally with an [engine oil change](/db/power-wagon/pw-oil-change/), and any milky or metal-flecked diff fluid you find is your cue to read the [front locker troubleshooting guide](/db/power-wagon/front-locker-elocker-troubleshooting/) before water reaches the locker wiring.","pros":["Refreshes all four drivetrain reservoirs with the correct, year-verified fluids","Catches water intrusion before it destroys axle bearings","A chance to inspect locker wiring and extend axle vents"],"cons":["Four different fluids \u2014 get front vs rear gear weight and ATF+4 right","Transmission type varies by year; confirm before buying a filter kit","Water crossings void the calendar interval \u2014 inspect after every deep one"]},{"id":"pw-oil-change","title":"Oil Change \u2014 6.4L Hemi Power Wagon","vehicle":"power-wagon","category":"maintenance","subcategory":"oil-change","type":"procedure","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0.5,"high":1},"cost_usd":{"low":70,"high":130},"tools_required":["13mm/16mm socket or oil-drain wrench","oil filter cap wrench","drain pan (8+ qt)","funnel","torque wrench","nitrile gloves"],"skills_required":["basic maintenance","correct fill level check"],"build_tiers":["stock","daily","mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["2014-2018","2019-2024","2025+"],"tags":["maintenance","oil-change","6.4L","hemi","service"],"parts":[{"name":"0W-40 full synthetic, 7 qt (must meet MS-12633 / MS-A0921)","vendor":"Pennzoil Ultra Platinum / Mopar","price_estimate_usd":55},{"name":"Engine oil filter (Mopar MO-339 or equivalent)","part_number":"MO-339","vendor":"Mopar / WIX","price_estimate_usd":12},{"name":"Drain plug gasket / crush washer","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":3}],"citations":[{"title":"Mopar 6.4L oil specification MS-12633 / MS-A0921 (0W-40)","url":"https://www.blauparts.com/ram-2500-oil-change-kit-64l-hemi-2014-17-0w-40.html"},{"title":"Ram 2500 oil type and capacity reference (6.4L = 7 qt 0W-40)","url":"https://www.jcofontario.com/service-department/service-and-parts-tips/ram-2500-oil-type/"}],"alternatives":["pw-spark-plugs","pw-fluids-service"],"summary":"The 6.4L Power Wagon takes **7 quarts of 0W-40 full synthetic** meeting Chrysler **MS-12633** (2014-2021) or **MS-A0921** (2022+) \u2014 not any random 0W-40, and not the 5W-20 a 5.7L Hemi uses. Pennzoil Ultra Platinum Euro 0W-40 is the common over-the-counter match. With the spin-on filter it is a 30-45 minute job for about $70 in materials. Get the oil grade right; the 6.4L is sensitive to the MS-12633 additive package for its lifter and MDS hardware.","description":"This is the most frequent service the truck needs, and the one most often done wrong by grabbing whatever 0W-40 is on the shelf. The 6.4L's specification exists because the engine's hydraulic lifters and cylinder-deactivation (MDS) hardware depend on the friction and shear properties of an oil that meets MS-12633. Using a non-spec oil is the kind of corner-cut that shows up later as lifter tick.\n\nCapacity with filter is **7 quarts**. Use a full-synthetic **0W-40** carrying the **MS-12633** approval (or **MS-A0921** on 2022-and-newer trucks). The oil-life monitor will call for a change on its own schedule, but severe duty \u2014 towing, dusty trails, lots of idling, short trips \u2014 means changing on time/mileage rather than waiting for the monitor. A practical severe-service interval is in the 4,000-6,000 mile range; highway-only trucks can ride the monitor closer to its limit. Always reset the oil-life monitor after the change so it tracks correctly.\n\nWarm the engine briefly so the oil flows, then shut it off and let it settle a few minutes. Position the pan, crack the drain plug, and let it fully drain \u2014 replace the crush washer when you reinstall the plug and torque it to spec (do not gorilla it into the aluminum pan). The filter on the 6.4L is a spin-on; use a cap-style wrench, pre-fill the new filter where orientation allows, and wipe the mating surface clean. Lube the new filter gasket with fresh oil and hand-tighten plus the marked turn. Add 7 quarts, run the engine, check for leaks at the plug and filter, then shut down, wait, and verify the level on the dipstick.\n\nCheck the level with the truck on level ground \u2014 overfilling a Hemi can aerate the oil and upset MDS operation. Confirm the dash oil-pressure reading comes up normally on start. Reset the oil-life monitor through the dash menu. Note the date and mileage; pairing oil changes with a quick under-truck look (axle seals, drive-shaft, skid plate hardware) catches trail damage early.\n\nThe 6.4L uses an **aluminum oil pan**, and that is where careless changes go wrong. The M12 drain plug wants roughly **20 ft-lb (about 25 Nm)** \u2014 snug, not muscle. Run it in past that and you pull or strip the soft aluminum threads, which turns a $3 crush washer into a pan replacement or a thread-repair insert. Always fit a **fresh crush washer** each change; a reused washer is the most common source of a slow drain-plug weep. The spin-on filter needs only a clean gasket wiped with fresh oil and the maker's marked turn past contact (typically 3/4 turn) \u2014 a filter cranked on with a wrench is how you round the cap and glue it in place for the next person. If a plug ever feels like it is fighting the threads going in, stop and chase it by hand rather than forcing it. For the rest of the drivetrain fluids on this truck, work from [the full fluid-service schedule](/db/power-wagon/pw-fluids-service/).\n\nThe change interval is also a free inspection. Wipe the **magnetic drain plug** \u2014 a light gray fuzz is normal, but glitter or metal flakes point at bearing or lifter wear worth investigating. Smell and look at the drained oil: a strong **fuel smell** or a level that reads *above* full on the dipstick can mean fuel dilution from short-trip or MDS cylinder wash, which shortens the real interval. A milky, coffee-with-cream look means coolant intrusion \u2014 stop and diagnose before you refill. Owners who tow or run dusty trails hard should pull a **used-oil analysis** ($30 a sample) once or twice to set their own interval instead of trusting the monitor blindly. If a persistent lifter tick shows up after a change, pair the next service with a code scan using [the OBD-II guide](/db/power-wagon/pw-obd2-diagnostics/) to catch an MDS or VVT fault early.","pros":["Cheap, fast, and the single most important service the 6.4L needs","Correct MS-12633 oil protects the lifters and MDS hardware long-term","A clean DIY change is a chance to inspect the underside after trail use"],"cons":["Wrong-spec 0W-40 (or 5.7L 5W-20) risks lifter noise and MDS faults","Overfilling aerates the oil and upsets cylinder deactivation","Severe off-road duty shortens the real interval below the monitor's call"]},{"id":"pw-spark-plugs","title":"Spark Plug Replacement \u2014 6.4L Hemi Power Wagon","vehicle":"power-wagon","category":"maintenance","subcategory":"ignition","type":"procedure","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1.5,"high":3},"cost_usd":{"low":60,"high":200},"tools_required":["5/8 spark plug socket + extensions","swivel/U-joint","torque wrench (in-lb and ft-lb)","gap gauge","dielectric grease","anti-seize (sparingly)","compressed air"],"skills_required":["working in tight engine bay","torquing plugs to spec without cross-threading"],"build_tiers":["stock","daily","mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2014-2018","2019-2024","2025+"],"tags":["maintenance","spark-plugs","ignition","misfire","6.4L","hemi"],"parts":[{"name":"Spark plugs \u2014 set of 16 (OEM heat range, gapped to spec)","vendor":"Mopar / NGK / Champion","price_estimate_usd":80},{"name":"Coil-on-plug (replace only if a coil tests bad)","vendor":"Mopar / NGK","price_estimate_usd":45},{"name":"Dielectric grease + anti-seize","vendor":"Permatex","price_estimate_usd":10}],"citations":[{"title":"DodgeGarage \u2014 Hemi spark plug service (two plugs per cylinder)","url":"https://www.dodgegarage.com/news/article/how-to/2019/03/sparks-and-recreation"},{"title":"6.4L Hemi spark plug change interval reference (~96k)","url":"https://ranwhenparked.net/6-4-hemi-spark-plug-change-interval/"}],"alternatives":["pw-obd2-diagnostics","pw-oil-change"],"summary":"The 6.4L Hemi has **16 spark plugs \u2014 two per cylinder** \u2014 and the factory interval runs out around **96,000 miles**, though many owners running copper-tip plugs change them sooner. It is a straightforward job made fiddly by tight access to the rear plugs and the two-coil-per-bank ignition layout. Budget $60-$200 depending on whether any coils need replacing. The two things that bite people: cross-threading a plug into a hot aluminum head, and losing track of which of the 16 plugs you have done.","description":"The Hemi's twin-plug design fires two plugs per cylinder for cleaner, more complete combustion and lower emissions. It also means twice the parts count and a bit more labor than a typical V8. Fresh plugs cure the most common 6.4L misfire codes and restore the smooth idle and throttle response that worn plugs erode. This is a high-value tune-up on a high-mileage truck.\n\nThe published service interval lands near **96,000 miles**, but plug life depends on the tip material. Factory-style copper-core plugs wear faster and some owners replace them in the 30,000-50,000 mile range, especially if they tow or run the engine hard; longer-life platinum/iridium plugs hold the gap closer to the full interval. Use the OEM-specified plug and heat range, and confirm the gap against the placard \u2014 pre-gapped plugs can shift in shipping. Do not over-gap a Hemi; the coils are happiest at the factory gap.\n\nWork on a cold engine to avoid galling the aluminum threads. Blow out each plug well with compressed air so debris does not fall into the cylinder when the plug comes out. Disconnect and remove the coil over each plug (label or work one at a time so you keep the firing components organized \u2014 there are eight coils feeding 16 plugs). Use a swivel and extensions to reach the rear cylinders, which are the tight ones against the firewall. Thread each new plug **by hand first** to avoid cross-threading, then torque to the service-manual value \u2014 a torque wrench matters here because an over-torqued plug damages the head and an under-torqued one blows out. Apply a thin film of anti-seize sparingly (or follow the plug maker's guidance) and dielectric grease on the coil boots.\n\nReconnect the coils, start the truck, and confirm a smooth idle with no misfire. If a misfire code (P0300 or a specific cylinder) appears or persists, the cause may be a tired coil rather than the plug \u2014 swap the suspect coil to a neighboring cylinder and re-scan to see if the misfire follows it. Pair this job with the OBD-II guide so you can read and clear codes as you verify.\n\nTwin plugs mean **eight coil packs feeding 16 plugs**, and a rough-running 6.4L is as often a tired coil as a worn plug. Before condemning anything, pull the freshest codes: a **P0301-P0308** names the exact misfiring cylinder, while a bare **P0300** is a random/multiple misfire that points more at fuel, vacuum, or a batch of worn plugs. The field test is the swap \u2014 move the suspect coil to a neighboring cylinder, clear the code, and drive it: if the misfire **follows the coil**, replace the coil; if it stays put, the plug or that cylinder is the problem. A coil boot that is oil-soaked or arc-tracked (gray carbon lines up the side) will misfire under load even with a fresh plug, so inspect every boot and re-grease with dielectric. Read and clear codes with [the OBD-II diagnostic guide](/db/power-wagon/pw-obd2-diagnostics/).\n\nAluminum heads are unforgiving, so the numbers matter. Gap the 6.4L plugs to the **placard spec (near 0.043 in)** and verify each one \u2014 pre-gapped plugs drift in shipping and a dropped plug is a bent electrode. Torque to the **service-manual value, roughly 13 ft-lb (about 156 in-lb)** on a warm-to-cold head; a torque wrench is not optional here because an over-torqued plug cracks the porcelain or damages the threads and an under-torqued one can blow out under compression. On **anti-seize**, the plug makers are split: NGK ships plated shells and says none is needed, while many techs run a *very* thin film on the threads only \u2014 never on the electrode. If you use it, back the torque off about 10-15% because lubricated threads read higher clamp load at the same wrench value. Pair the tune-up with fresh oil from [the oil-change guide](/db/power-wagon/pw-oil-change/) so the whole ignition-and-lubrication side is fresh at once.","pros":["Cures the most common 6.4L misfire and rough-idle complaints","A high-value tune-up that restores throttle response on a high-mileage truck","Doing it yourself saves the shop premium on a 16-plug labor job"],"cons":["16 plugs and tight rear-cylinder access make it longer than a typical V8","Cross-threading or over-torquing into the aluminum head is a costly mistake","Copper-tip plugs may need replacing well before the 96k interval"]},{"id":"pw-overland-awning","title":"270\u00b0 Awning for Power Wagon (ARB Touring / 23ZERO Peregrine)","vehicle":"power-wagon","category":"recovery","subcategory":"camping","type":"part","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":5},"cost_usd":{"low":400,"high":1100},"tools_required":["metric sockets","torque wrench","drill (if mounting brackets need new holes)"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","two-person install"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2014-2018","2019-2024","2025+"],"tags":["awning","overland","camping","shade"],"parts":[{"name":"ARB Touring Awning 2500mm (8'2\" \u2014 fits PW bed-rack or cab-mount)","part_number":"ARB-814408","vendor":"ARB","price_estimate_usd":520},{"name":"23ZERO Peregrine 270\u00b0 Awning (wraps around the back of the truck)","vendor":"23ZERO","price_estimate_usd":950},{"name":"Roam Adventure Co. Rambler Awning (budget straight awning)","vendor":"Roam Adventure Co.","price_estimate_usd":380},{"name":"Bed-rack-side mounting brackets (depend on rack)","vendor":"rack manufacturer","price_estimate_usd":90}],"citations":[{"title":"ARB \u2014 Touring Awning mounting and fitment guide","url":"https://www.arbusa.com/"},{"title":"23ZERO \u2014 Peregrine 270\u00b0 awning specifications and load notes","url":"https://www.23zero.com/"},{"title":"Front Runner \u2014 Slimline II rack awning channel mounting","url":"https://www.frontrunneroutfitters.com/"}],"alternatives":["pw-rooftop-tent"],"summary":"An awning is one of the highest-ROI overland upgrades for the Power Wagon. Mount it to a bed rack or to the cab roof rack and you've got shade or rain cover at camp in 60 seconds. ARB Touring is the value reference. 23ZERO Peregrine 270\u00b0 is the premium pick \u2014 wraps around the back of the truck so you've got shade three sides of the rig.","description":"A Power Wagon-mounted awning needs a place to attach. Three common locations:\n\n1. **Bed-rack side** \u2014 most common. A bed rack (Front Runner, Decked CargoGlide, or a custom welded rack) with side awning channels. This is the standard pickup-truck overland setup.\n2. **Cab roof rack** \u2014 if you're running a cab-mount rack for lights/RTT, an awning mounts to its side rail. Less common because most PW owners want bed space free.\n3. **Bed rail direct-mount** \u2014 some kits mount to factory bed rails with brackets. Lower mounting height (less head room under the awning) but no rack required.\n\n**ARB Touring 2500mm** ($520) \u2014 the value reference. 8'2\" straight awning, deploys in 60 seconds, supports rain or sun. Includes ground poles. Mounts to any standard awning channel. Proven 10+ year design.\n\n**23ZERO Peregrine 270\u00b0** ($950) \u2014 the premium pick. Wraps around the rear corner of the truck, covering driver side + rear + chunk of passenger side. Gives you shade across the back of the bed for cooking and seating. Double the price of straight awning but covers ~2x the area.\n\n**Roam Adventure Co. Rambler** ($380) \u2014 budget straight awning. Same idea as ARB Touring, slightly less robust fabric, perfectly fine for weekend camping.\n\n**Mounting bracket cost** is on top of awning cost. Most awnings mount to standard 80/20-style channel rails, which most overland racks include. If your rack doesn't have channels, plan $50-100 for bracket adapters.\n\nFor Power Wagon-specific consideration: bed-rack-mounted awnings put weight on the rack at the highest point. A 270\u00b0 awning is 50-65 lb. Make sure your bed rack is rated for that plus whatever else (RTT, gear baskets) you're putting up there. The Power Wagon's high CG with a loaded bed rack is the trade-off you accept for the convenience.\n\n**Wind:** all awnings tear in sustained 25+ mph wind. Take them down before sleeping if weather's coming in.\n\nThe awning itself deploys in a minute; the install is a one-time job that decides whether it stays solid for years.\n\n1. Confirm your mount surface. Most awnings clamp to 80/20-style channel on a bed rack or roof rack. If your rack has no channel, order the bracket adapters first \u2014 they are $50-100 and you do not want to discover the gap on assembly day.\n2. Set the height. On a bed rack, mount the awning so its arms clear the bed rail when folded and give standing headroom when deployed. Too low and you duck under it all weekend; too high and short arms will not reach the ground.\n3. Bolt the channel brackets to the rack with the awning held in place by a second person \u2014 these are awkward and heavy enough that a one-person install ends in scratched paint or a dropped awning.\n4. Torque the bracket bolts and add thread-locker. The awning lives on the highest, most vibration-exposed point on the truck; loose brackets are the number-one failure.\n5. Deploy it once at home, stake the legs, and confirm the fabric tensions flat with no sag. Adjust arm angle before you rely on it at camp.\n\nWeigh the whole rack load before you commit: a 270\u00b0 awning is 50-65 lb on top of any [[pw-rooftop-tent]] and gear baskets, all riding high on a truck that already has a tall center of gravity.\n\nWind is what destroys awnings \u2014 any of them will tear in sustained 25+ mph gusts, so take it down before you sleep if weather is moving in, no matter how well it is staked. The other watch item is cumulative rack weight and center of gravity: the Power Wagon already sits tall, and stacking an awning, a tent, and baskets at the top of the rack changes how the truck leans on off-camber trail, so respect your rack's rated load and keep the heaviest items low. Cheap awnings save money up front and then shred a fabric panel in the first real storm, so for anything beyond occasional fair-weather use the mid-tier fabric pays for itself.","pros":["Shade and rain cover at camp in 60 seconds","270\u00b0 awnings cover three sides of the truck \u2014 game-changing for cooking","Bolts to any standard overland rack","High-ROI upgrade (cheap relative to a tent, big quality-of-life gain)"],"cons":["Adds 30-65 lb to the highest point of the rack","270\u00b0 awnings double the cost of straight","Cheap awnings tear quickly in wind","Mounting depends on having a bed rack or roof rack already"]},{"id":"pw-overland-drawer","title":"Bed Drawer System for Power Wagon (Decked DS-Series / Goose Gear)","vehicle":"power-wagon","category":"interior","subcategory":"storage","type":"kit","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":5},"cost_usd":{"low":1400,"high":4500},"tools_required":["metric sockets","torque wrench (35-50 lb-ft for bed bolts)","two-person lift assist"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2014-2018","2019-2024","2025+"],"tags":["drawer-system","storage","overland","premium"],"parts":[{"name":"Decked DS-Series Drawer System for Ram 2500 6.4' bed","part_number":"DECKED-DS-RAM-64","vendor":"Decked","price_estimate_usd":1400},{"name":"Goose Gear Modular Bed Drawer System (premium \u2014 fully customizable)","vendor":"Goose Gear","price_estimate_usd":3800},{"name":"ARB Outback Solutions drawer kit","vendor":"ARB","price_estimate_usd":2400},{"name":"Custom bed-fab drawer (welded steel, varies by builder)","vendor":"local fab shop","price_estimate_usd":2500}],"alternatives":["pw-overland-sleep-platform","pw-overland-fridge"],"citations":[{"title":"Decked DS-Series drawer system \u2014 Ram 2500 6.4' bed fitment","url":"https://decked.com/products/drawer-system"},{"title":"Goose Gear modular bed systems \u2014 overview","url":"https://goosegear.com/"},{"title":"ARB Outback Solutions roller drawers","url":"https://www.arbusa.com/storage-solutions/"}],"summary":"A bed drawer system organizes the chaos. The Power Wagon's 6.4' bed swallows gear, and finding anything in it after a week on the trail is a misery. Decked is the universal answer at $1,400 \u2014 bolts in, no drilling, two slide-out drawers rated 200 lb each, top surface doubles as a sleep platform. Goose Gear is the premium customizable alternative at $3,800.","description":"The Power Wagon's bed is excellent for hauling \u2014 6.4 feet long, deep walls \u2014 but terrible for organized overland gear. Without organization:\n- Tools migrate to wherever you can reach them\n- Heavy stuff slides forward under braking, light stuff bounces around\n- Loose gear scratches the bed liner and dents body panels\n- Finding the spare batteries at 9pm at camp = unpacking everything\n\nA drawer system fixes this. Three tiers:\n\n**Decked DS-Series** ($1,400) \u2014 the universal answer. Tough polymer construction, two large slide-out drawers running the length of the bed (each ~71\" \u00d7 25\" \u00d7 11\"), each rated 200 lb capacity. Bolts to the factory bed cleats \u2014 no drilling. Install is 2 hours with two people. Top surface is flat and at wheel-well height \u2014 works as a sleep platform (see [[pw-overland-sleep-platform]]). Survives any abuse you throw at it.\n\n**Goose Gear Modular** ($3,800) \u2014 premium. Marine-grade plywood + steel frame construction, fully customizable layout (drawer + fridge slide + electronics tray combinations). The choice if you want a specific layout (e.g., dual-zone fridge on the left, drawer on the right, water on the slide). Way more capable, twice the price.\n\n**ARB Outback Solutions** ($2,400) \u2014 middle ground. Steel construction, modular drawers, designed by ARB for their accessory ecosystem. Good fit if you're already in the ARB ecosystem (compressor, awning, etc.).\n\n**Custom welded steel** ($2,500+) \u2014 local fabricator route. Heaviest, most durable, no resale value. Worth it if you have a specific need no off-the-shelf product addresses.\n\n**Trade-off vs. open bed:** drawers eat 11-13\" of vertical bed space. If you regularly haul tall stuff (motorcycle, kayaks standing up, hay bales for the horse), drawers are wrong for you. If you regularly haul organized gear (camping, photography, fishing), drawers are the right answer.\n\n**Power Wagon-specific note:** the Decked DS-Series for the 6.4' Ram bed is bolt-in compatible with all 4th-gen and 5th-gen Power Wagons. The 2025+ Power Wagon (new gen) may need confirmation \u2014 check Decked's fitment chart before ordering.\n\nThe Decked is the most common path, and it's a real two-person afternoon, not a 20-minute drop-in. The unit ships in a long box and weighs ~230 lb assembled.\n\n1. Clear the bed and decide on a spray-in liner first. Decked bolts to the factory bed cleats and sits on the bed floor; if you want a liner, have it done before the install, not after.\n2. Unpack and pre-assemble the drawers on sawhorses per the instructions. The frame and drawer boxes ship partially knocked down to fit shipping.\n3. With two people, lower the assembled unit straight down into the bed \u2014 don't drag it, the wheel-well clearance is tight and the lower edges will scuff the bedsides.\n4. Locate the factory bed cleats / stake pockets the kit uses and run the supplied bolts hand-tight first, then torque to 35-50 lb-ft. No drilling on the 4th/5th-gen 6.4' bed.\n5. Cycle both drawers fully open and closed to confirm the slides aren't binding against the wheel wells, then load-test each to a fraction of its 200 lb rating before trusting it on the trail.\n\nThe flat top deck sits at wheel-well height, which is exactly the surface a [[pw-overland-sleep-platform]] needs \u2014 pair the two and you get storage and a bed in one. If you're also running a fridge, plan its slide location now; see [[pw-overland-fridge]].\n\nThe single biggest regret is vertical space. The Decked eats 11-13 inches of bed depth. Measure the tallest thing you regularly haul \u2014 a dirt bike's bars, a kayak standing on edge, stacked firewood \u2014 before committing. Owners who haul tall loads end up pulling the system back out.\n\nWatch the payload. At ~230 lb empty plus whatever you load into it, a full drawer system can add 400+ lb to a truck that already carries its own modest payload ceiling. That weight rides behind the cab, so it stacks with a rear bumper, tire carrier, and water tank against the rear GAWR.\n\nFinally, confirm 2025+ fitment specifically. The new-generation Power Wagon bed cleats and dimensions differ from the 2014-2024 trucks; Decked's chart is the authority, and a kit cut for the older bed will not bolt cleanly to the newest one.","pros":["Organized storage transforms overland life","Decked is bolt-in to factory bed cleats \u2014 no drilling","Top surface doubles as sleep platform (sleeps 2 on Exped MegaMat)","Lockable drawers (Decked) \u2014 security for tools at trailhead","Strong resale value (Decked + Goose Gear especially)"],"cons":["Loses 11-13\" of vertical bed space \u2014 bad for tall cargo","Decked at $1,400 is a real commitment; not casual","Goose Gear configurations can balloon past $5,000 with fridge slides + electronics","Heavy \u2014 Decked is 230 lb installed, eats payload"]},{"id":"pw-overland-fridge","title":"12V Fridge for Power Wagon (Dometic CFX3 / ARB Zero / ICECO VL)","vehicle":"power-wagon","category":"interior","subcategory":"fridge","type":"part","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":4},"cost_usd":{"low":550,"high":1600},"tools_required":["wire strippers","fuse holder + Anderson Powerpole connector (for direct wire)","voltmeter"],"skills_required":["basic 12V wiring"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2014-2018","2019-2024","2025+"],"tags":["fridge","12v-cooler","overland","camping","dual-battery-recommended"],"parts":[{"name":"Dometic CFX3 45 (45L \u2014 full-size, dual-zone)","part_number":"Dometic-9600025383","vendor":"Dometic","price_estimate_usd":1100},{"name":"ARB Zero 47 (47L \u2014 premium, ultra-efficient)","vendor":"ARB","price_estimate_usd":1500},{"name":"ICECO VL45 ProS (45L \u2014 value pick)","vendor":"ICECO","price_estimate_usd":580},{"name":"Front Runner Fridge Slide (for accessible from bed-mounted fridge)","vendor":"Front Runner","price_estimate_usd":380}],"citations":[{"title":"Dometic \u2014 CFX3 series amp draw and dual-zone specifications","url":"https://www.dometic.com/"},{"title":"ARB \u2014 Zero fridge insulation and power-consumption data","url":"https://www.arbusa.com/"},{"title":"Secop \u2014 variable-speed compressor application notes (12V fridges)","url":"https://www.secop.com/"}],"alternatives":["pw-overland-drawer","pw-dual-battery"],"summary":"A 12V compressor fridge is the single highest-ROI overland upgrade. Set the temp, forget about it, no ice management. For a Power Wagon-sized rig with a 6.4' bed, a 45L fridge is the sweet spot \u2014 enough for a week of food for two, not so big it eats half the cargo bay. Mount on a fridge slide so you can pull it out without climbing into the bed.","description":"A 12V compressor fridge (not thermoelectric \u2014 those are junk for camping) is the universal Power Wagon overland upgrade. Three tiers:\n\n**ICECO VL45 ProS** ($580) \u2014 value pick. 45L capacity, Secop compressor (the same Danish compressor used in premium units), digital temperature display, USB charging port. Slightly noisier than Dometic/ARB but works fine for occasional use. Best $/L on the market.\n\n**Dometic CFX3 45** ($1,100) \u2014 the reference. Same compressor class as ICECO but with better insulation (lower amp draw \u2014 typically 0.5-1.0 A/hr in 90\u00b0F ambient), WiFi monitoring, dual-zone capability (fridge + freezer in one unit). The most popular overland fridge in North America. 5-year warranty.\n\n**ARB Zero 47** ($1,500) \u2014 premium. Best insulation in class (lowest amp draw of any 45L unit on the market), titanium-reinforced corners (drop-rated), removable basket. The pick if you camp 50+ days/year or live in hot climates.\n\n**Power Wagon-specific wiring:** the bed has a factory 12V outlet at the rear right corner (rated 20A) \u2014 fine for any of these fridges. For serious off-grid use, wire direct from the battery through a 15A inline fuse and Anderson Powerpole. That bypasses the ignition-off cut-off (some PWs cut bed-outlet power 30 min after key-off) and gives you a clean 24/7 connection.\n\n**Dual battery consideration:** a single factory battery and an ignition-off fridge will discharge to low-voltage cutoff in 8-14 hours depending on temperature. For multi-day camping with the engine off, plan on a dual-battery setup (Genesis Offroad or REDARC isolator + auxiliary battery). The Power Wagon has factory provisions for a second battery \u2014 there's a mounting tray and dedicated stud already there.\n\n**Fridge slide:** if you mount the fridge in the bed (most common location), a fridge slide ($380) extends it out the tailgate for access without climbing in. The Power Wagon's tall bed makes this almost mandatory \u2014 without a slide, you're reaching down 36\" to grab beer.\n\nThe factory bed outlet works for a weekend, but a direct-wired feed is what makes a fridge a true overland appliance.\n\n1. Decide the feed. The rear-right bed outlet (20A) runs any 45L fridge fine \u2014 until the truck cuts bed-outlet power some minutes after key-off, which kills your fridge overnight. For multi-day use, wire direct.\n2. For a direct feed, run a fused line from the battery (or the auxiliary battery) to the bed: 12-gauge wire, a 15A inline fuse within a few inches of the power source, and an Anderson Powerpole or SAE connector at the fridge end.\n3. Route the wire along an existing harness path, through the cab grommet or bed-floor pass-through, away from the exhaust and any pinch points. Loom it where it crosses sheet-metal edges.\n4. Check voltage at the fridge end with the engine off and running \u2014 you want to see battery voltage with minimal drop. A long undersized run that sags below the fridge's low-voltage cutoff will cycle the compressor off when you need it most.\n5. Set the fridge's battery-protection level to match your setup: low or medium on a single battery (so it shuts off before stranding you), low on a managed dual-battery bank.\n\nA single starting battery and an ignition-off fridge discharge to cutoff in 8-14 hours, so plan the [[pw-dual-battery]] setup alongside the fridge if you camp without running the engine, and mount the fridge on a slide so the [[pw-overland-drawer]] and cargo plan stay accessible.\n\nThe defining mistake is buying a thermoelectric \"cooler\" instead of a compressor fridge \u2014 they cannot hold a safe food temperature in a hot truck bed and have no place in a build. After that, the two things that bite people are power and access. Undersized or overly long wiring causes nuisance low-voltage shutoffs that look like a broken fridge but are really a wiring problem, and a 45L unit dropped into the Power Wagon's tall bed without a slide means reaching 36 inches down every time, so the slide is functionally part of the install, not an option. Buy the fridge used if you can \u2014 Dometic and ARB units hold value, so a lightly used one saves 30-50% with no real downside.","pros":["Set-and-forget cold food for a week+","No ice management","Doubles as a freezer (dual-zone models)","Dometic/ARB resale value is strong \u2014 buy used to save 30-50%"],"cons":["$580-1,500 for the fridge alone","Needs dual battery for serious off-grid use (+$400-800)","45L eats meaningful bed space \u2014 fridge slide is functionally required","Power Wagon's bed-outlet ignition cutoff may force direct-wire install"]},{"id":"pw-overland-kitchen","title":"Slide-Out Camp Kitchen for Power Wagon (Drifta / DECKED CrossBox / DIY Tailgate Setup)","vehicle":"power-wagon","category":"interior","subcategory":"kitchen","type":"kit","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":3,"high":8},"cost_usd":{"low":350,"high":2400},"tools_required":["metric sockets","drill (some setups)","stove-fuel handling \u2014 propane or isobutane"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2014-2018","2019-2024","2025+"],"tags":["camp-kitchen","camp-stove","overland","camping"],"parts":[{"name":"Drifta Stockton Slide-Out Kitchen Module (Australian premium \u2014 most popular)","part_number":"DRIFTA-K1","vendor":"Drifta","price_estimate_usd":1800},{"name":"DECKED CrossBox (bolts to Decked drawer system as a kitchen module)","vendor":"Decked","price_estimate_usd":580},{"name":"Camp Chef Everest 2X stove (2-burner, integrated regulator, propane)","part_number":"CC-Everest-2X","vendor":"Camp Chef","price_estimate_usd":180},{"name":"DIY tailgate kitchen \u2014 folding aluminum table + stove + tote","vendor":"varies","price_estimate_usd":350}],"alternatives":["pw-overland-drawer","pw-overland-fridge"],"citations":[{"title":"Drifta Stockton \u2014 slide-out camp kitchen modules","url":"https://driftastockton.com/"},{"title":"Decked CrossBox \u2014 bed drawer accessory","url":"https://decked.com/"},{"title":"Camp Chef Everest 2X \u2014 two-burner stove specs","url":"https://www.campchef.com/"}],"summary":"The Power Wagon's tailgate is the right size for cooking \u2014 56\" wide, drops to a stable horizontal surface at counter height. Three paths: a Drifta slide-out kitchen module ($1,800), the more straightforward Decked CrossBox if you already have their drawers ($580), or DIY with a folding table + Camp Chef stove for $350. Pick based on how often you camp and how much you cook.","description":"A \"camp kitchen\" on a Power Wagon ranges from minimal (a stove on the tailgate, a cooler nearby) to a full slide-out module with sink, propane plumbing, prep surface, and integrated storage. Where you land depends on how often you camp and what you cook.\n\n**Drifta Stockton Slide-Out Kitchen** ($1,800) \u2014 Australian-built, the reference. Mounts in the bed or to a drawer system, slides out the back of the truck, includes a sink, stove mount, prep surface, storage for utensils + dry goods. The \"I am serious about camping\" answer. Common 2-3 week build wait time.\n\n**Decked CrossBox** ($580) \u2014 bolts to a Decked drawer system (which you'd need separately, $1,400 \u2014 see [[pw-overland-drawer]]). Provides a kitchen module above the drawers with stove platform and storage. Less feature-dense than Drifta but bundles into the Decked ecosystem cleanly.\n\n**DIY tailgate kitchen** ($350) \u2014 folding aluminum prep table ($120) + 2-burner Camp Chef Everest stove ($180) + plastic dry-goods tote ($30). Setup at camp is 3 minutes. Pack down is 3 minutes. The right answer for 80% of camping use cases.\n\n**Camp Chef Everest 2X** ($180) \u2014 the universal stove pick. 20,000 BTU per burner, runs on standard 1lb propane bottles or 20lb tank via adapter, ignition lights with a click, integrated regulator. Replaceable parts. Lasts forever.\n\n**Power Wagon-specific:** the tailgate has a 56\" wide drop surface at exactly counter height (~32\" from ground). It's almost custom-designed as a cooking workbench. Don't fight that \u2014 embrace it. A DIY tailgate setup uses what the truck already provides.\n\n**Propane handling:** any stove burning propane in the bed needs ventilation. Don't cook in the bed under a closed topper. Stoves with low-CO emissions (Camp Chef Everest is one) are fine in the open air with the tailgate down.\n\n**Honest framing:** the Drifta is beautiful and most owners who buy one love them. But for occasional camping (5-15 nights/year), the DIY tailgate setup is the right answer \u2014 you save $1,500+ and pack down to a tote that lives in the garage between trips. The Drifta is for full-time overland life or commercial use (guide service, expedition outfitting).\n\nThis is the setup most Power Wagon owners actually want, and it costs $350 to assemble from parts you can source in a weekend.\n\n1. Start with the table. A folding aluminum roll-top table (~$120) at tailgate height gives you prep space beside the truck so the tailgate itself stays clear for the stove. Aluminum because it wipes clean and shrugs off heat.\n2. Mount the stove on the tailgate, not the table. The Camp Chef Everest 2X sits flat on the 56\" tailgate surface at ~32\" working height. Keep it within reach of the prep table but out over the open tailgate for ventilation.\n3. Pack the dry goods and cookware in one sealed tote (~$30) that lives in the bed or a [[pw-overland-drawer]]. One tote = one trip's pack-in and pack-out.\n4. Run the stove off a 1 lb propane bottle for short trips, or add a hose-and-adapter to a refillable 20 lb tank for week-long use \u2014 far cheaper per meal and far less waste.\n5. Keep a dedicated water source nearby for washing; pairing this with a [[pw-overland-fridge]] slide turns the tailgate into a full galley without any bed fabrication.\n\nThe whole setup pitches and strikes in about three minutes and stores in the garage between trips \u2014 which is exactly why it beats a fixed module for anyone camping fewer than ~20 nights a year.\n\nCarbon monoxide is the real hazard, and it doesn't care how nice your setup is. Never run a propane stove in the bed under a closed topper or in any enclosed space \u2014 cook with the tailgate down in open air. The Camp Chef Everest is a low-CO design, but \"low\" is not \"zero,\" and a tarp pulled over the cooking area in rain has killed campers.\n\nWatch the wind. The Everest's open burners struggle above about 15 mph of wind; a $25 folding wind screen pays for itself in fuel and patience. And mind the tailgate's finish \u2014 a hot stove base will discolor a painted or plastic-capped tailgate over time, so put a silicone mat or thin cutting board under it.\n\nFinally, be honest about how often you cook. A Drifta or CrossBox that lives bolted in the bed costs you payload and bed space year-round for convenience you use a few weeks a year. Most owners over-buy here.","pros":["Tailgate is a natural kitchen surface \u2014 work with it","Drifta is gorgeous and functional if you camp constantly","Camp Chef Everest is the universal stove pick","DIY setup is light, cheap, and lives in a tote when not in use"],"cons":["Drifta is $1,800 + needs install \u2014 overkill for occasional camping","Decked CrossBox requires Decked drawers underneath ($2,000+ total)","Propane needs ventilation \u2014 don't cook under a closed topper","Cleanup at camp is on you no matter which setup you pick"]},{"id":"pw-rooftop-tent","title":"Overland Setup \u2014 Rooftop Tent and Bed Rack for the Power Wagon","vehicle":"power-wagon","category":"overland","type":"part","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":3,"high":6},"cost_usd":{"low":599,"high":4200},"tools_required":["socket set","torque wrench","tape measure","drill (some installations)","level","ladder or step stool","second set of hands"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","weight distribution awareness","reading a door-jamb payload sticker"],"build_tiers":["overland"],"year_compatibility":["2014-2018","2019-2024"],"tags":["overland","rooftop-tent","camping","bed-rack"],"parts":[{"name":"Decked AR Bed Rack for Ram 2500 (base for bed-mount tents)","vendor":"Decked","price_estimate_usd":699},{"name":"Front Runner Slimline II bed rack kit (alternative base)","vendor":"Front Runner","price_estimate_usd":1100},{"name":"iKamper Skycamp 3.0 hard-shell rooftop tent","vendor":"iKamper","price_estimate_usd":2499},{"name":"Roofnest Falcon / CVT hard-shell RTT (lighter alternative)","vendor":"Roofnest / CVT Overland","price_estimate_usd":1499},{"name":"ARB 2500 awning (2.5 m)","vendor":"ARB","price_estimate_usd":299}],"citations":[{"title":"iKamper Skycamp 3.0 specifications","url":"https://www.ikamper.com/products/skycamp-3-0"},{"title":"Decked AR bed rack \u2014 load ratings and fitment","url":"https://www.decked.com/products/decked-ar-bed-rack"},{"title":"ARB \u2014 2500 awning dimensions and fitting","url":"https://www.arbusa.com/awnings/"}],"alternatives":["pw-recovery-gear-kit","pw-overland-sleep-platform","pw-overland-drawer"],"summary":"Two numbers decide this build and neither one is on the tent's box. The first is the **dynamic** load rating of your bed rack \u2014 what it holds while the truck is moving, typically 150-300 lb on quality systems, versus the 800-1,000 lb *static* rating manufacturers advertise. The second is the payload on your door-jamb sticker, which on a Power Wagon is roughly **1,400-1,500 lb** \u2014 the lowest in the Ram 2500 lineup, because the coil rear suspension and off-road hardware eat into it. Size the tent to the rack, then weigh the loaded truck. Everything else here is detail.","description":"Rooftop-tent makers publish a static rating \u2014 the weight the tent's structure supports while it is open and the truck is parked, which is why an 800 lb number is plausible for two adults and bedding. That figure tells you nothing about driving.\n\nThe rack underneath is rated dynamically: the load it can carry while the truck moves, bounces, and corners. Quality bed racks land at **150-300 lb dynamic**. That lower number is the ceiling on tent weight, and it is the spec to shop against.\n\nHard-shell tents run **120-180 lb**. Soft-shell tents run **100-140 lb** but pack taller and are slower to deploy. A 165 lb hard shell on a 300 lb dynamic rack is fine. The same tent on a 150 lb rack is not, no matter what the tent's own sticker says.\n\nDo this before you buy anything.\n\n1. Read the yellow sticker in the driver's door jamb: \"The combined weight of occupants and cargo should never exceed XXXX lb.\" That is *your* truck's payload, after its specific options.\n2. Subtract every human who rides in it. Two adults is roughly 350 lb.\n3. Subtract the build: bed rack **60-90 lb**, hard-shell tent **120-180 lb**, awning **60-70 lb**, bedding and ladder **25 lb**.\n4. Subtract the rest of the trip: water at **8.3 lb per gallon** (a 20 gal tank is 166 lb), fuel cans, a fridge at 40-60 lb loaded, recovery gear at 60-100 lb, tools, food.\n\nA representative overland Power Wagon: 350 lb of people + 240 lb of rack and tent + 65 lb awning + 165 lb of water + 90 lb of recovery gear + 60 lb fridge + 100 lb of everything else = **1,070 lb**, against a payload near 1,450 lb. It fits, with less margin than most people assume, and it stops fitting the moment you add a second passenger row or a tank of extra fuel.\n\nThen verify it. A **CAT scale run costs about $15** and gives you steer axle, drive axle, and gross weights you can check against the GAWR and GVWR on the same door-jamb sticker. Front GAWR on the 2014+ Power Wagon is 6,000 lb; GVWR is around 8,510 lb. Guessing here is how people run a rear axle over its rating without knowing.\n\n**Bed rack (recommended).** The tent sits over the bed, keeps the load lower, and leaves the cab roof alone. The **Decked AR bed rack** (~$699) is the common entry point \u2014 a rigid aluminum ladder rack that bolts to the bed rail system, usually without drilling, and carries a 300 lb dynamic rating. **Front Runner's Slimline II** truck bed kit (~$900-1,300) is the modular alternative if you want to hang accessories off the rack itself. The tradeoff is that the rack occupies the bed, which conflicts with drawer systems and long cargo.\n\n**Cab-over.** Mounts above the cab on brackets or a cab-height crossbar and keeps the bed completely clear. It is the right call if the bed is doing real work. The costs are more visual height, more wind noise, and a more involved mounting system that must not load the cab roof skin.\n\n**Height matters.** A stock Power Wagon is already around **6 ft 6 in** tall. A bed rack plus a closed hard-shell tent commonly puts the truck at **8 ft or more** \u2014 over most residential garage doors, at the limit of some parking structures, and firmly into \"check the clearance bar\" territory. Measure the finished truck and write the number on a label inside the cab. People shear tents off on parking garages every season.\n\nExpect a real fuel economy penalty as well: **1-3 mpg** is typical on a truck with this frontal area once a tent and awning are up top.\n\nThe **ARB 2500** (~$299) is the highest value-per-dollar item on this list. It mounts to a bed rack or roof rail in about an hour and deploys in a couple of minutes. On dimensions, be accurate rather than optimistic: the 2500 is a **2.5 m (roughly 8 ft) wall length with about 7 ft of projection**, not a square 8.5 x 8.5 ft footprint \u2014 check ARB's current spec for your model. In desert country, shade during the middle of the day is a safety item, not a comfort item.\n\n1. Measure the bed rail width and inside bed length to confirm rack fitment. The 2014+ Power Wagon is **crew cab only** with the **6'4\"** bed on a 149.3 in wheelbase, so there is one bed configuration to shop for.\n2. Install the bed rack per the manufacturer's instructions \u2014 most systems bolt to the factory stake pockets or bed rail mounts without drilling. Torque to spec and note which fasteners the maker wants re-torqued.\n3. Check the rack is level front-to-back with a spirit level before the tent goes on. An unlevel rack is an unlevel bed at 2 a.m.\n4. Set the tent on the crossbars with a second person. A 165 lb hard shell at chest height is a two-person lift and a common source of dented bed rails.\n5. Mount using the tent's supplied T-slot hardware or channel brackets, torqued to spec. Undertorqued tent hardware vibrates loose on washboard \u2014 this is the single most common failure on a truck that actually goes off pavement.\n6. Open the tent and verify the hinge and ladder clear the rear cab glass, the third brake light, and the tailgate through their full arc.\n7. Mount the awning to the driver-side rack rail so it opens away from the bed and does not foul the tent's opening direction.\n8. Full open-and-close rehearsal in the driveway before the first trip, then **re-torque everything after the first 100 miles** and again after the first washboard day.\n\n| Item | Cost | Weight |\n|---|---|---|\n| Decked AR bed rack | $699 | 60-90 lb |\n| Front Runner Slimline II bed kit | $900-1,300 | 70-100 lb |\n| Hard-shell RTT (Roofnest / CVT class) | $1,499-1,900 | 120-160 lb |\n| iKamper Skycamp 3.0 | $2,499 | ~165 lb |\n| ARB 2500 awning | $299 | 60-70 lb |\n| **Typical all-in** | **$2,500-4,200** | **250-320 lb** |\n\nIf the numbers or the height are more than you want, the cheaper and lighter answer is a [bed sleep platform](/db/power-wagon/pw-overland-sleep-platform/) \u2014 no height penalty, no dynamic load question, a fraction of the cost. If the rack is going in regardless, plan it alongside a [drawer system](/db/power-wagon/pw-overland-drawer/) so they do not fight for the same bed space, and read the [heavy-truck weight reality guide](/db/power-wagon/hd-3-4-ton-weight-reality/) before you finalize the load plan. A [fridge](/db/power-wagon/pw-overland-fridge/) and camp lighting will want a [dual-battery setup](/db/power-wagon/pw-dual-battery/) so overnight loads never touch the crank battery, and the [awning guide](/db/power-wagon/pw-overland-awning/) covers mounting options in more depth.","pros":["The 6'4\" bed is a good rooftop-tent platform and longer than most competitors' crew-cab beds","Hard shells deploy and pack in under three minutes \u2014 no poles, no stakes, no wet-tent wrestling","Bed-rack mounting keeps the sleeping platform lower and more stable than a cab roof","The awning integrates with the same rack for a complete camp footprint"],"cons":["Dynamic rack rating (150-300 lb) governs the build, not the tent's static number","The Power Wagon has the lowest payload of the Ram 2500 line \u2014 roughly 1,400-1,500 lb before anything is added","A finished setup commonly exceeds 8 ft tall and clears fewer garages and structures than owners expect","Expect 1-3 mpg lost and noticeably more crosswind sensitivity","The rack occupies the bed and limits drawer access or long cargo without removing it"]},{"id":"pw-overland-sleep-platform","title":"Bed Sleep Platform + Mattress (Budget Overlanding)","vehicle":"power-wagon","category":"interior","subcategory":"sleep","type":"kit","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":4,"high":12},"cost_usd":{"low":300,"high":1400},"tools_required":["circular saw or jigsaw","drill","measuring tape","tie-down hardware"],"skills_required":["basic carpentry"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2014-2018","2019-2024","2025+"],"tags":["sleep-platform","cargo-mattress","overland","camping","budget","diy"],"parts":[{"name":"DIY platform \u2014 3/4\" Baltic birch + 2x3 supports, sized to 6.4' Power Wagon bed (~76\" \u00d7 64\")","vendor":"local hardware","price_estimate_usd":140},{"name":"Exped MegaMat 10 LWX (XL self-inflating mattress)","vendor":"Exped","price_estimate_usd":380},{"name":"Decked Drawer System (premium pre-fab; serves as platform + storage)","vendor":"Decked","price_estimate_usd":1400},{"name":"Tonneau or hard topper (mandatory for weather protection)","vendor":"varies","price_estimate_usd":0}],"alternatives":["pw-rooftop-tent","pw-overland-drawer"],"citations":[{"title":"Exped MegaMat 10 LWX \u2014 R-value and dimensions","url":"https://www.exped.com/"},{"title":"Decked DS-Series drawer system \u2014 Ram 2500 6.4' bed","url":"https://decked.com/products/drawer-system"},{"title":"Power Wagon (Ram 2500) 6.4' bed dimensions \u2014 Ram body specs","url":"https://www.ramtrucks.com/"}],"summary":"The Power Wagon's 6.4-foot bed sleeps two adults if you build a platform that bridges the wheel wells. Cheapest path: DIY plywood platform + Exped MegaMat mattress = $500. Premium path: Decked drawer system that doubles as sleep platform + storage = $1,400. Either way, you need a topper or tonneau cover for weather protection.","description":"The Power Wagon's bed is 76\" long \u00d7 66\" wide between the wheel wells (with the tailgate up). Add the tailgate down and you get 100\" of length \u2014 enough for one tall person diagonally or two cozy. The catch: the wheel wells eat into the floor about 8\" on each side, so a bare-bed setup means sleeping on a narrow 50\" \u00d7 76\" strip.\n\n**A sleep platform fixes that.** Build a level surface that bridges the wheel wells at wheel-well-top height (~24\" above the bed floor) and you get a true 64\" \u00d7 76\" sleeping surface \u2014 wider than a queen mattress.\n\n**DIY platform path** ($300 for platform + $380 for mattress = $680 total):\n- 3/4\" Baltic birch plywood, cut to 76\" \u00d7 64\" with notches for tie-down loops\n- 2x3 pine supports underneath, 4 cross-members\n- Cargo carpet on top for finish\n- Storage underneath the platform \u2014 the 24\" of dead space is huge for gear\n\n**Premium path \u2014 Decked Drawer System** ($1,400):\n- Pre-fab tough polymer drawer system specifically sized to the Power Wagon bed\n- Top surface is sleep-platform height\n- Two huge drawers underneath for gear (each rated 200 lb)\n- Bolts to the factory bed cleats \u2014 no drilling\n- The \"I want to be done in 2 hours\" answer\n\n**Mattress:** The Exped MegaMat 10 LWX is the reference. 4\" thick self-inflating mattress, R-value 8.1 (4-season), 6'6\" long \u00d7 30\" wide (you'd want two for a couple). About $380 each.\n\n**Mandatory weather protection.** A Power Wagon bed without a topper or tonneau cover floods in any rain. Three options:\n- **Soft tri-fold tonneau** ($400-700) \u2014 manageable install, removable, won't stand up to wind sleeping inside\n- **Hard tri-fold tonneau** ($800-1,200) \u2014 better wind sealing, still removable\n- **Hard topper** (Leer, ARE) ($2,500-4,500) \u2014 proper headroom inside, sealed weather, the right answer for serious overlanding\n- **Rooftop tent** ([[pw-rooftop-tent]]) \u2014 sidesteps the issue, sleep on the roof\n\nComparison vs. the [[pw-rooftop-tent]] route: bed-sleep is $500-1,400 + topper. RTT is $1,200-5,500 + bed rack. RTT is faster setup, bed-sleep is cheaper and works without a roof rack rating concern.\n\nThe plywood-and-mat path is the one most owners land on once they price a Decked unit. Plan a weekend and basic carpentry.\n\n1. Measure your specific bed. The 6.4' Power Wagon bed runs ~76\" long between the bulkhead and closed tailgate and ~66\" wide at the rails, but the wheel wells pinch it to ~50\" at the floor. You're building a deck that bridges those wells at their top height (~24\" up).\n2. Cut a 3/4\" Baltic birch top to ~76\" \u00d7 64\", notching the corners to clear the tie-down loops. Birch over construction ply \u2014 it won't sag under a sleeper and finishes cleanly.\n3. Build a 2x3 pine frame underneath with four cross-members, sized so the deck rests on the wheel-well tops and the bedsides, not on the bare floor. That keeps the 24\" of under-deck space open for gear.\n4. Skin the top in cargo carpet or indoor-outdoor carpet so a mattress doesn't slide and so you don't gouge the wood.\n5. Add the mattress. The Exped MegaMat 10 LWX (R-value 8.1, 4\" thick) is the reference for cold nights; two side-by-side make a true couple's bed on the 64\" deck.\n\nIf you'd rather skip the saw entirely, the [[pw-overland-drawer]] system arrives at the same deck height and adds two lockable drawers underneath \u2014 more money, but storage and platform in one bolt-in unit.\n\nWeather protection is not optional. An open Power Wagon bed floods in any rain, so budget for a topper or tonneau alongside the platform \u2014 a soft tri-fold runs $400-700, a hard topper $2,500-4,500. Skipping this is the mistake that ends bed-sleeping for most people after one wet night.\n\nCondensation is the second issue. A sealed topper over two breathing adults soaks the underside of the lid by morning. Crack a window or run a small vent; a 12V fan moves enough air to keep the bedding dry. And mind the under-deck gear \u2014 anything stored beneath the platform is directly over the rear axle, so heavy totes there stack with a rear bumper and water tank against payload.","pros":["Cheapest sleep solution if you accept the topper requirement","Decked option doubles as storage (massive functional value)","Inside the truck \u2014 warmer in winter, more discreet for stealth camping","No roof load \u2014 no rack capacity concern"],"cons":["Needs topper/tonneau for weather (additional $400-4,500)","Climbing in and out via tailgate is awkward at night","Bed floor with a topper has condensation issues without ventilation","Decked system at $1,400 is close to entry RTT pricing \u2014 consider both"]},{"id":"pw-overland-water-shower","title":"Pressurized Water System + Shower for Power Wagon (RoadShower / Front Runner)","vehicle":"power-wagon","category":"recovery","subcategory":"camping","type":"kit","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":6},"cost_usd":{"low":280,"high":900},"tools_required":["metric sockets","drill (for some bracket installs)"],"skills_required":["basic plumbing","rack-mounting work"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2014-2018","2019-2024","2025+"],"tags":["water-system","overland-shower","overland","camping"],"parts":[{"name":"Yakima RoadShower 7 (7 gallon, solar-heated, bed-rack mount)","part_number":"Yakima-8007406-7","vendor":"Yakima","price_estimate_usd":520},{"name":"Front Runner 42L Pressurized Water Tank + 12V pump kit","vendor":"Front Runner","price_estimate_usd":680},{"name":"Geyser Systems portable shower (4-gallon, separate from rig)","vendor":"Geyser Systems","price_estimate_usd":320},{"name":"Mounting brackets for bed rack (depends on rack)","vendor":"rack manufacturer","price_estimate_usd":60}],"alternatives":["pw-overland-awning","pw-overland-fridge"],"citations":[{"title":"Yakima RoadShower \u2014 pressurized solar water tank specs","url":"https://www.yakima.com/"},{"title":"Front Runner pressurized water tank + 12V pump kit","url":"https://www.frontrunneroutfitters.com/"},{"title":"Geyser Systems portable shower \u2014 overview","url":"https://geysersystems.com/"}],"summary":"A Power Wagon-mounted water tank gives you 7+ gallons of pressurized water at camp \u2014 for showers, dish washing, cooking, refilling bottles. Mount the RoadShower to a bed rack and the black tank solar-heats to 80-100\u00b0F on a typical day. The right answer for multi-night camping or any trip where you don't want to depend on stream-water filtration.","description":"A pressurized water system upgrades a Power Wagon from \"camping trip\" to \"multi-night basecamp\" comfortably. Three options:\n\n**Yakima RoadShower 7** ($520) \u2014 the reference. 7-gallon black aluminum tank with hose, pressurized via bicycle pump or 12V air compressor. Mounts to bed rack crossbars on standard 80/20 channels. The black tank solar-heats during a day of driving \u2014 you get warm water at camp without any heater. Yakima also makes a 4-gallon version ($350) and a 10-gallon ($680).\n\n**Front Runner 42L Tank + 12V Pump** ($680) \u2014 better for cooking + dishes use case. 42L plastic tank (about 11 gallons), 12V pump for genuine pressurized flow (not trickle), proper RV-style faucet. Mounts in the bed (not on the rack \u2014 lower CG). Doesn't solar-heat unless you add a heater module.\n\n**Geyser Systems portable shower** ($320) \u2014 different approach. A 4-gallon insulated tank that lives in the bed or cab, pumps water through a shower head via 12V. Lighter (no roof weight), more compact, but only useful AT camp (not a mounted-system for drinking water on the road).\n\n**Power Wagon-specific:** the bed rack is the natural mounting point for the RoadShower. Your rack needs to be rated for 60-95 lb dynamic load (7 gallons of water + tank = 65 lb minimum). Most quality overland racks (Front Runner Slimline, Eezi-Awn K9, BAJA Designs) handle that fine. Cheap eBay racks may not.\n\n**Plumbing:** RoadShower comes with a 6' hose and shower head. For dish-washing or refilling water bottles, the standard hose is fine. If you want a proper sink at the back of the bed, you'll need to plumb a longer hose (Yakima sells extensions).\n\n**Water quality:** the RoadShower's aluminum tank is potable-rated. The Front Runner plastic tank is the same. You can drink from both. The Geyser uses a removable refillable bag \u2014 also potable.\n\n**Cold weather:** any system with water in it freezes. Drain in winter or accept that you're camping without water below 32\u00b0F.\n\n**Honest framing:** a $520 RoadShower is a quality-of-life luxury. For weekend trips, a 5-gallon jerry can + cooking pot ($30) does the same job. For multi-night camping or 5+ days of base-camping, the convenience of pressurized hot water at the rear of the truck is worth the money.\n\nThe RoadShower is the common pick, and mounting it correctly is mostly about rack rating and routing.\n\n1. Confirm the rack first. A full 7-gallon RoadShower is ~65 lb; the 10-gallon is closer to 95 lb. Your bed rack needs that as a rated dynamic load \u2014 quality racks (Front Runner Slimline, Eezi-Awn K9) handle it, bargain racks flex and crack.\n2. Position the tank low and centered side-to-side on the rack to keep the truck's already-tall center of gravity in check. Mounting a heavy tank high and off-center is what makes a Power Wagon feel tippy on off-camber trail.\n3. Bolt the tank to the rack crossbars with the supplied 80/20 channel hardware. Use the tank's full bracket set \u2014 two brackets is not enough for a sloshing 65 lb tank over washboard.\n4. Route the hose and shower head to the rear of the truck where the tailgate gives you a natural shower or wash station. Yakima sells hose extensions if you want it to reach a tailgate sink.\n5. Pressurize with a bicycle pump or a 12V compressor \u2014 the same one many owners carry for [[pw-tires-at]] airing-up duty does double work here.\n\nFor shade over the wash-up area, the [[pw-overland-awning]] mounts to the same rack and turns the rear corner into a usable space.\n\nFreezing is the failure that ruins hardware. Any water left in a tank, pump, or hose can split a fitting or crack a pump body below 32\u00b0F \u2014 drain the whole system before a cold night or before winter storage. This is the most common way these systems die.\n\nWatch the loaded weight on the rack and the rear axle. A full tank plus the rack plus a tire carrier and tent stacks up fast, and water is dense \u2014 7 gallons is ~58 lb of payload that moves to the very top and rear of the truck. Mind potability too: keep a drinking-water tank separate from one you've used for greasy dish water, even though both tanks are potable-rated, because flavors and films carry over.","pros":["Hot water at camp without setup","Doubles as dish-washing + drinking water source","Solar-heats during drive (RoadShower)","Pressurized \u2014 real flow, not trickle"],"cons":["Heavy when full (60-95 lb on the rack for a full RoadShower)","Needs rated bed rack","Pure luxury for short trips","Freezes below 32\u00b0F \u2014 drain or accept damage"]},{"id":"factory-warn-winch-service","title":"Servicing the Factory Warn 12,000 lb Winch on the Ram Power Wagon","vehicle":"power-wagon","category":"recovery","subcategory":"winch_service","type":"how_to","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":2.0,"high":5.0},"cost_usd":{"low":80,"high":450},"tools_required":["ratchet set","screwdrivers","wire brush","electrical contact cleaner","dielectric grease","work gloves"],"skills_required":["basic electrical","patience with cable/rope management"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2008-2009","2010-2018","2019-present"],"tags":["winch","warn","synthetic-rope","recovery","service"],"parts":[{"name":"Warn Spydura synthetic rope 3/8\" x 90'","vendor":"Warn Industries","price_estimate_usd":180},{"name":"Hawse fairlead for synthetic rope","vendor":"Warn Industries","price_estimate_usd":55},{"name":"Winch gear grease","vendor":"Warn Industries","price_estimate_usd":18},{"name":"Winch contactor service kit","vendor":"Warn Industries","price_estimate_usd":45}],"citations":[{"title":"Warn VR12 winch service manual","url":"https://www.warn.com/service-manuals"},{"title":"Warn synthetic rope installation guide","url":"https://www.warn.com/synthetic-rope"}],"alternatives":["pw-winch-upgrade","pw-recovery-points-install"],"summary":"The factory Warn winch is genuinely good equipment, but wire rope, motor brushes, and the wiring harness routing on early trucks all need periodic attention. Don't assume it works because it worked last time.","description":"The Power Wagon's factory Warn winch is the Warn VR12 (12,000 lb rated) on 2008+ trucks. It's a capable unit that doesn't need replacement for most owners \u2014 but it does need service, and the service points differ between the factory installation and a typical aftermarket winch mount. The biggest issues: wire rope vs. synthetic, the wire bundle routing on 2008\u20132013 units, and contactor corrosion.\n\nFactory trucks shipped with steel wire rope through 2015 on many builds. Steel wire rope has one major liability in recovery use: when it breaks under load, it stores and releases enormous kinetic energy \u2014 the snap can be lethal at close range. It also kinks, which creates stress points that aren't visible until the rope fails.\n\nThe synthetic rope swap is the most important service item on this list. Warn's Spydura rope is the stock equivalent and fits the factory drum. The swap requires a hawse fairlead to replace the roller fairlead \u2014 synthetic rope cuts on sharp roller edges.\n\n**Rope swap procedure:**\n1. Spool out all the wire rope, freeing the drum\n2. The factory wire rope is secured to the drum with a wedge clamp inside the drum opening \u2014 use a screwdriver to pry out the wedge, then pull the rope end free\n3. Clean the drum of any rust or debris\n4. Thread the synthetic rope through the hawse fairlead and into the drum slot\n5. Create a figure-8 knot or use the drum's rope anchor point (varies by model year) \u2014 do not rely on wrapping the rope\n6. Spool in the rope under light tension, keeping even layering for the first three wraps \u2014 these base wraps carry the actual load\n\nKeep a minimum of five wraps on the drum at all times under load. The rope anchor and the first few wraps are not rated to carry full winch load.\n\nThe Warn VR12 uses a series-wound DC motor and a set of contactors (heavy-duty relays) to switch direction. Contactors corrode \u2014 especially on trucks that see water crossings. The symptom is intermittent winch operation: it works at rest but hesitates or fails mid-pull.\n\nAccess the contactors through the winch body cover (four screws on most VR12 installations). Clean the contactor contact surfaces with electrical contact cleaner and fine emery cloth. Apply dielectric grease to the connection terminals. Replace if pitting is visible.\n\nBrush inspection: the motor brushes wear at roughly 50\u201380 hours of winch use. This is more cumulative than most users reach, but on a truck that sees regular trail use and recovery work, the brushes are worth inspecting at 5\u20136 years of active service.\n\nEarly Power Wagon installations routed the winch control wiring through a path that can chafe against the frame or winch body under repeated cable-in/cable-out cycling. The symptom is intermittent remote failure or winch running in one direction only. If your winch runs out but won't come in (or vice versa), inspect the control wire bundle for abrasion before condemning the contactors.\n\nFix: reroute the bundle with additional cushioned cable clamps and protect any contact points with split loom. This takes 30 minutes and prevents a failure in the field.\n\nThe winch gear assembly is lubricated with a heavy grease (not oil). Warn sells a service kit for the gear housing. Under heavy trail use, a gear grease refresh at 3\u20134 years is good practice \u2014 the gear grease can thin under heat and develop moisture contamination in trucks that cross water regularly.\n\nRebuild is worth it if the motor brushes are worn and the gear grease looks clean. A new brush set costs $40\u2013$60 and restores motor performance. If the winch motor itself shows signs of armature damage (burning smell, heavy draw with no load), replacement is the right call \u2014 motor rebuilds on factory winch units rarely save enough money to justify the labor.","steps":["Spool out all rope/wire completely and inspect full length for damage","Swap wire rope to synthetic if not already done \u2014 replace roller fairlead with hawse","Inspect and clean contactors; check wire bundle routing for chafe","Lubricate gear housing per Warn service procedure","Test under light load (5\u201310% rated capacity) before relying on it in the field","Check remote control wiring and plug connection for corrosion"],"pros":["Factory Warn VR12 is rebuildable with widely available parts","Synthetic rope upgrade is straightforward and significantly safer than wire rope","Contactor service is accessible without removing the winch from the truck"],"cons":["Full drum disassembly for gear service requires removing the winch from the bumper","Early wire bundle routing is a known weakness that needs correction","12,000 lb rating is for single-line pull \u2014 plan recovery rigging accordingly on a 7,000+ lb truck"]},{"id":"pw-recovery-gear-kit","title":"Recovery Gear Kit for the Power Wagon \u2014 Beyond the Factory Winch","vehicle":"power-wagon","category":"recovery","type":"kit","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":1},"cost_usd":{"low":250,"high":600},"tools_required":["none \u2014 gear assembly and storage only"],"skills_required":["basic recovery technique"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2016-2024"],"tags":["recovery","winch","kinetic-rope","traction-boards"],"parts":[{"name":"Bubba Rope 30' Gator-Jaw Kinetic Rope (3/4\" diameter)","vendor":"Bubba Rope","price_estimate_usd":149},{"name":"ARB Recovery Bag Deluxe Kit","vendor":"ARB","price_estimate_usd":499},{"name":"Maxtrax MKII Traction Boards (pair)","vendor":"Maxtrax","price_estimate_usd":349},{"name":"Rhino USA 3/4\" Bow Shackles (2-pack)","vendor":"Rhino USA","price_estimate_usd":29},{"name":"Factor 55 FlatLink Rope-to-Shackle Connector","vendor":"Factor 55","price_estimate_usd":65}],"citations":[{"title":"ARB Recovery Gear Guide","url":"https://www.arb.com.au/recovery"},{"title":"Maxtrax MKII Product Page","url":"https://www.maxtrax.com.au/mkii"}],"alternatives":["pw-front-bumper"],"summary":"The Power Wagon's factory Warn 12,000 lb winch is the recovery anchor \u2014 this kit builds out the rest of what you need when the winch can't reach a tree, when you're helping someone else, or when the mud is deeper than the winch line is long.","description":"The Power Wagon's factory winch is one of the best-specced production recovery setups ever sold on a truck. It covers the self-recovery scenario with 150' of steel cable and full remote control. What it doesn't cover is everything else: a recovery when there's no anchor point forward, a soft ground situation where traction boards are faster than winching, the pull to get a friend's rig out of a wash using the Power Wagon as the anchor, or the basic shackle connections you need to put any of it to work.\n\nKinetic rope is the highest-impact addition. A kinetic recovery rope stores energy as it stretches, using that stored energy to pull the stuck vehicle free \u2014 it's more effective than a static tow strap in deep mud or sand because it adds a slingshot dynamic to the recovery. The Power Wagon's GVWR of 8,510 lbs means you need a rope rated for that load: Bubba Rope's 3/4\" Gator-Jaw ($149) is rated for 18,900 lbs MBS (minimum breaking strength), which gives appropriate safety margin when the Wagon is doing the pulling or being pulled. The 30' length is the practical minimum for most recoveries.\n\nMaxtrax MKII boards ($349) are the other high-value addition. When the truck is in sand, mud, or snow with the wheels spinning free, boards under the driven tires restore traction in minutes without requiring an anchor point or bystander assistance. The Power Wagon's weight (roughly 6,500 lbs curb) means you want the full-size MKII rather than lighter boards \u2014 they're rated for the load. The ARB Recovery Bag Kit ($499) is the comprehensive bundle option that includes kinetic rope, snatch block, shackles, and storage bag in one purchase \u2014 good value if you're equipping from scratch.\n\nThe Power Wagon is usually the heaviest vehicle in the recovery, and the loads scale with the weight. Know the difference between MBS (minimum breaking strength \u2014 the failure point) and working load limit (typically MBS divided by 4\u20135); rate every component in the chain for the pull, because the chain is only as strong as the shackle you grabbed without looking. Never rig to a hitch ball or a wheel \u2014 factory tow hooks, the winch, or [rated recovery points](/db/power-wagon/pw-recovery-points-install/) only, and if the front bumper is aftermarket, confirm its recovery points are load-rated rather than cosmetic (the [front bumper guide](/db/power-wagon/pw-front-bumper/) covers which are which). Drape a line damper (a recovery blanket, or a heavy jacket in a pinch) over the middle of any loaded line to drive it into the ground if something lets go. Kinetic pulls start with 2\u20133 feet of slack and a rolling 5\u201310 mph tug \u2014 never a running start \u2014 and everyone not in a vehicle stands outside the arc of the line by at least its full length. Soft shackles are worth adding to the kit: lighter than steel bow shackles, nothing metallic to become a projectile, and kinder to synthetic rope eyes.\n\nThe factory Warn's steel cable works, but it stores more energy under load, develops fish-hook burrs that shred bare hands, and weighs a great deal at the front bumper. Converting to synthetic winch rope of equal or higher rating than the winch is the standard follow-on to this kit: safer failure behavior, roughly 4x lighter, and hand-spoolable. The conversion is not a straight swap \u2014 the drum must be inspected and cleaned of burrs that would abrade the rope, synthetic wants a hawse-style fairlead rather than a steel roller unit with cable-worn grooves, and the rope needs re-tensioning under load after the first use. The [factory winch service guide](/db/power-wagon/factory-warn-winch-service/) covers drum access and respooling technique, and if the winch is being upgraded outright, the [winch upgrade guide](/db/power-wagon/pw-winch-upgrade/) covers rope choice as part of that decision. Until the conversion happens, treat the steel cable with gloves, keep the damper on it, and inspect for burrs and flat spots every time it spools out.","steps":["Verify the factory rear recovery hooks (frame-mounted tow hooks behind the rear bumper) are torqued and undamaged \u2014 these are your primary rear attachment points.","Inspect the factory winch cable and remote; ensure the fairlead, hook, and electrical connections are intact.","Store kinetic rope in a mesh or ventilated bag \u2014 kinetic rope holds mud and should drain after wet use.","Store traction boards in the bed or a tailgate-access bag where they're reachable without unloading gear.","Store shackles where you can access them one-handed \u2014 a magnetic dash mount or door pocket works.","Practice rigging your kinetic rope to the rear hooks and the front winch hook before your first trail day so the technique is familiar when you need it."],"pros":["Covers the recovery scenarios the factory winch cannot: no anchor forward, soft ground, and assisting others","Kinetic rope is the highest-return recovery tool per dollar after the winch","Maxtrax boards work without bystander help or an anchor point","Factory rear tow hooks are GVWR-rated \u2014 no aftermarket shackle mount required for most recovery rigs"],"cons":["Factory winch uses steel cable \u2014 synthetic rope conversion is a worthwhile follow-on upgrade for safety and weight","Kinetic rope is not a substitute for winch \u2014 it requires a recovery vehicle to provide pull","Maxtrax at this truck's weight means full-size boards only; light-duty alternatives are undersized for the Power Wagon","Recovery gear only works if you've practiced using it; dead-weight gear bag is a false safety net"]},{"id":"recovery-points-and-front-bumper","title":"Factory Recovery Points on the Power Wagon \u2014 What You Can Trust and What You Can't","vehicle":"power-wagon","category":"recovery","subcategory":"recovery_points","type":"reference","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0,"high":1.0},"cost_usd":{"low":60,"high":400},"tools_required":["flashlight (frame-mount inspection)","torque wrench","rated D-ring shackle or soft shackle","tape measure","recovery damper / line blanket","leather gloves"],"skills_required":["recovery rigging awareness","reading a working load limit"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2005-2009","2010-2018","2019-present"],"tags":["recovery","recovery-points","winch-bumper","shackles","rear-recovery"],"parts":[{"name":"Rated recovery shackle 3/4 in D-ring, 4.75 ton WLL","vendor":"Factor 55 / Van Beest","price_estimate_usd":45},{"name":"Soft shackle, 7/16 in, ~40,000 lb MBS","vendor":"Factor 55 / Bubba Rope","price_estimate_usd":60},{"name":"Rated receiver-tube recovery point (2 in receiver)","vendor":"Mopar / Factor 55 HitchLink","price_estimate_usd":95},{"name":"Weld-on or bolt-on rear frame recovery point (pair)","vendor":"various","price_estimate_usd":120},{"name":"Recovery damper / line blanket","vendor":"ARB / Warn","price_estimate_usd":35}],"citations":[{"title":"Ram \u2014 Power Wagon factory equipment and winch documentation","url":"https://www.ramtrucks.com/power-wagon.html"},{"title":"WARN Industries \u2014 recovery rigging basics and safety","url":"https://www.warn.com/recovery"},{"title":"Factor 55 \u2014 working load limit vs. minimum breaking strength","url":"https://www.factor55.com/pages/education"}],"alternatives":["pw-recovery-points-install","pw-front-bumper","pw-recovery-gear-kit"],"summary":"Front: use the factory winch bumper's shackle tabs. They are purpose-built, tied to the frame through a heavy bumper-to-frame bracket, and you do not need to improvise. Rear: you do not have an equivalent. The factory rear bumper has a receiver hitch but no rated shackle mounts, so the correct answer is a rated receiver-tube recovery point for most owners, or a frame-mounted point if you want something permanent. Everything below is about knowing which of those you are attached to before somebody puts 20,000 lb into it.","description":"Every number on recovery gear is one of two things, and confusing them gets people hurt.\n\n**MBS (minimum breaking strength)** is where the part fails in a lab, new, in a straight pull. **WLL (working load limit)** is MBS divided by a design factor \u2014 commonly 5:1 or 6:1 on rigging hardware \u2014 and it is the number you rig to. A 3/4 in D-ring rated **4.75 ton WLL** has an MBS in the neighborhood of 47,000 lb. Marketing copy quotes MBS because it is the bigger number. Rig to WLL.\n\nTwo rules follow directly. First, the *weakest* item in the system sets the system's rating, and it is usually not the strap. Second, a shock load \u2014 a running-start snatch \u2014 multiplies force far beyond static vehicle weight, which is why anything not rated for dynamic recovery does not belong in a kinetic pull.\n\nThe factory Power Wagon winch bumper carries dedicated shackle mount tabs integrated into the bumper structure, which connects to the frame rails through a heavy steel bumper-to-frame bracket. Ram builds them as recovery points and they are not decorative. A properly rated 3/4 in D-ring or a soft shackle through those tabs is the correct front attachment.\n\n**The winch hook is not a recovery point.** The hook on the end of the winch line is for rigging the winch to an *anchor*. If someone is pulling your truck, they attach to the bumper shackle tabs, not to your winch hook, and not to the fairlead.\n\nWhat the front bumper does *not* do is protect the engine from a direct hit. It gives approach-angle clearance and covers the winch motor; in most frontal impacts the body contacts before the frame does. If you are doing aggressive approach work, skid plates come before a bumper swap.\n\n**Receiver-tube recovery point (the practical answer).** A rated receiver-mount recovery point drops into the 2 in receiver, pins in place, and gives you a proper shackle mount. Mopar and several aftermarket makers build them; expect **$60-120**. This is the right solution for most Power Wagon owners because it is bolt-free, removable, and rated as a unit.\n\n**What you must not do:**\n\n- Do not run a strap through the bare receiver tube. The hollow square tube is not rated for recovery and it deforms.\n- Do not attach to a tow ball. Tow balls fail under angular and lateral loads and become projectiles.\n- Do not attach to the trailer wiring housing, the bumper step, or a license plate bracket.\n\n**Frame horns.** The Ram 2500's rear frame rails are accessible at the back of the truck, and a weld-on or bolt-on shackle mount at the rail is the correct permanent rear point. Two cautions: weld to the vertical **web** of the rail rather than the flanges, and know where the fuel tank sits on a crew cab before any heat or drilling goes near the frame. If you are not confident on both counts, this is a shop job \u2014 see the [recovery point install guide](/db/power-wagon/pw-recovery-points-install/) for the full procedure.\n\n**Factory tow hooks.** Some configurations include rear tow hooks from the towing package. These are designed for low-speed towing connections, not snatch recovery. They will take a steady pull with favorable geometry; they are not equivalent to the front bumper's purpose-built tabs, and they are not where a kinetic rope belongs.\n\nThe factory winch is a **Warn 12,000 lb** unit. Earlier trucks ran steel cable; **2019+ trucks came with synthetic rope**, which changes the fairlead (hawse for synthetic, roller for steel) and the handling rules \u2014 verify what is actually on your truck rather than assuming by year.\n\nThe rating is calculated at the **first layer of rope on the drum**. As rope stacks up, the effective moment arm grows and pulling force drops \u2014 with a full drum you may see closer to **8,000-9,000 lb** of usable pull. On a ~6,900 lb truck buried to the frame, that margin disappears quickly. The fix is a snatch block or recovery pulley to double the line, which roughly doubles pulling force and halves line speed. When you double the line, the anchor and every component in the doubled leg see roughly twice the load, so rate the anchor accordingly.\n\nKeep **at least five wraps** on the drum at all times \u2014 the drum anchor is not rated to hold a full-force pull.\n\n### Rigging a front winch pull\n\n1. Assess the anchor: a healthy tree with a tree-saver strap, another vehicle's rated point, or a buried anchor. Never a rock spike, a fence post, or a small trunk.\n2. Free-spool the line out by hand with gloves on rather than powering it out.\n3. Install a rated shackle through the factory bumper tab on the side nearest the anchor.\n4. Attach the strap or snatch block to the shackle \u2014 never directly to the winch hook.\n5. Lay a **recovery damper over the line** at roughly its midpoint. If a line or shackle lets go, the damper drops the energy instead of launching the hardware.\n6. Clear everyone from the line and from the snap zone \u2014 one and a half times the line length, on both sides.\n7. Take up slack, pause, and inspect the whole system under light tension before committing.\n8. Winch with steady tension. Do not jerk, and do not run the motor to a stall \u2014 let it cool between pulls.\n9. Re-spool under light tension so the rope stacks evenly.\n\nA kinetic (energy-storing) rope stretches and releases, which is gentler than a static strap but puts far higher peak loads into whatever it is attached to. Size the rope to the *stuck* vehicle's gross weight per the manufacturer's chart; for a truck in the Power Wagon's class that is typically a **7/8 in or 1 in** rope with an MBS in the **28,000-33,000 lb** range. Undersizing is dangerous; oversizing is merely less effective, because a rope too stiff for the load will not stretch.\n\nBoth ends of a kinetic pull must be on rated, dynamic-capable points. On the Power Wagon that means the front bumper tabs, or a rated receiver point at the rear \u2014 never the hitch tube, never a ball, never a tow hook.\n\n| Item | Cost | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| 3/4 in rated D-ring shackle | $30-50 | Buy branded and rated, not a hardware-store pin |\n| Soft shackle 7/16 in | $40-70 | Lighter, will not fly, easier on the bumper tab |\n| Receiver-tube recovery point | $60-120 | The rear fix for most owners |\n| Recovery damper | $25-45 | The cheapest item that prevents the worst outcome |\n| Frame-mounted rear points (parts) | $100-160 | Plus weld or install labor |\n| Snatch block / recovery pulley | $80-200 | Doubles winch force; rate the anchor for double |\n\nFull kit context is in the [recovery gear kit guide](/db/power-wagon/pw-recovery-gear-kit/); winch maintenance is in the [factory Warn winch service guide](/db/power-wagon/factory-warn-winch-service/); if you are considering more line pull, the [winch upgrade guide](/db/power-wagon/pw-winch-upgrade/) covers what the factory mount and wiring will support. If you are already replacing the rear bumper, an [aftermarket rear bumper with integrated tabs](/db/power-wagon/pw-rear-bumper-tire-carrier/) solves the rear recovery-point problem and the tire-carrier problem in one purchase.","pros":["Factory front bumper shackle tabs are purpose-built and rated \u2014 do not second-guess them","The factory 12,000 lb winch makes self-recovery workable in most stuck scenarios","Receiver-mount recovery points are inexpensive, removable, and genuinely rated"],"cons":["No factory rear shackle tabs \u2014 a receiver point or frame-mounted point is required","Winch line pull falls off as the drum fills; a snatch block is frequently necessary on a truck this heavy","Tow hooks, where present, are not the equivalent of rated recovery mounts","Every recovery on a ~6,900 lb truck is a high-energy event \u2014 WLL, dampers, and clear snap zones are not optional"]},{"id":"pw-recovery-points-install","title":"Recovery Points Install for Power Wagon (Front Frame Mount + Rear D-Rings)","vehicle":"power-wagon","category":"recovery","subcategory":"recovery_points","type":"part","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":5},"cost_usd":{"low":180,"high":480},"tools_required":["metric sockets through 22mm","torque wrench (250 lb-ft)","drill","thread tap (M14x1.5 if drilling)"],"skills_required":["careful frame-bolt work"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2014-2018","2019-2024","2025+"],"tags":["recovery-points","recovery","shackle","essential","safety-critical"],"parts":[{"name":"Smittybilt Front Frame-Mount Recovery Shackle Mount (pair)","part_number":"SMB-PW-RSM","vendor":"Smittybilt","price_estimate_usd":140},{"name":"Factor 55 ProLink XTV soft shackle (rated 16,800 lb)","vendor":"Factor 55","price_estimate_usd":80},{"name":"ARB 4.75-ton D-ring shackle (set of 2)","vendor":"ARB","price_estimate_usd":60},{"name":"AEV-spec rear recovery points (if not using AEV rear bumper)","vendor":"AEV","price_estimate_usd":220}],"citations":[{"title":"Factor 55 \u2014 soft shackle and recovery point rated-strength data","url":"https://www.factor55.com/"},{"title":"ARB \u2014 recovery point and rated shackle engineering notes","url":"https://www.arbusa.com/"},{"title":"I-4WDTA \u2014 recovery point vs. factory tow hook safety guidance","url":"https://i4wdta.org/"}],"alternatives":["pw-front-bumper","pw-rear-bumper-tire-carrier"],"summary":"The Power Wagon's factory front tow hooks are rated for static towing, not dynamic recovery. The plastic-capped factory rear has no recovery points at all. Real recovery points (rated D-ring mounts on frame steel) are a safety-critical upgrade \u2014 the factory hooks fail under snatch loads and become projectiles.","description":"The Power Wagon ships with two front tow hooks on the frame. They look beefy but they're rated for **static towing only** \u2014 pulling a stalled vehicle from a parking spot, slow steady pull. They are explicitly **not rated** for dynamic recovery (snatch straps, kinetic ropes, winching where loads spike 2-3x rated capacity). Multiple documented cases of factory tow hooks failing and becoming high-velocity projectiles during recovery.\n\nThree install paths:\n\n**1. Front frame-mount shackle brackets** ($140) \u2014 bolts to the factory bumper bracket bolts using a thicker steel plate that distributes load across more frame area. Accepts standard 3/4\" D-ring shackles or soft shackles. Rated for dynamic recovery loads. Smittybilt makes a Power Wagon-specific bracket; install is 30 min with hand tools.\n\n**2. Front bumper integrated recovery points** \u2014 if you've already swapped to an AEV/Carli/Expedition One front bumper (see [[pw-front-bumper]]), those have rated D-ring mounts integrated. Skip this entry \u2014 you already have what you need.\n\n**3. Rear recovery points** \u2014 the factory rear has NO recovery points. The factory bed-mount spare tire cradle is decorative, not recovery-rated. Options:\n- AEV rear bumper with carrier ($2,800, see [[pw-rear-bumper-tire-carrier]]) \u2014 has integrated D-rings, solves multiple problems at once\n- AEV-spec rear frame-mount recovery points ($220) \u2014 bolt to the rear frame rails through factory holes plus 2 additional drilled mounts. Cheaper than a full rear bumper.\n\n**Pair with soft shackles, not steel D-rings, for most recoveries.** A Factor 55 ProLink or similar soft shackle is rated 16,800+ lb, weighs 4 oz, floats, and doesn't become a projectile if it fails. Steel D-rings are still useful for static work but for snatch recoveries, soft shackles are the modern best practice. Plan to carry both \u2014 at $80 for a soft shackle and $30 each for a steel D-ring, the kit is $200 of essential gear.\n\nInstall for the front frame brackets: jack the front, pull the factory tow hooks (4 bolts each, 18mm), install the new bracket using the included longer-grade bolts and the supplied torque spec (typically 95-110 lb-ft for the M14 hardware). Loctite the threads. Re-torque after 100 miles.\n\nThis is a hand-tool job, but it is safety-critical hardware, so treat the torque values and thread-locker as non-negotiable.\n\n1. Chock the rear wheels, jack the front, and set it on stands. You want the front bumper area accessible without lying under an unsupported truck.\n2. Remove the factory tow hooks \u2014 typically four bolts each, 18mm. Keep them; they are still fine for static, slow-pull towing.\n3. Position the frame-mount bracket so it sandwiches against the boxed frame horn, not merely the bumper skin. The whole point is spreading load into frame steel.\n4. Install the supplied grade-8 (or better) bolts. Do not reuse the factory tow-hook bolts unless the kit explicitly calls for it \u2014 the bracket relies on the longer, higher-grade hardware that ships with it.\n5. Apply medium thread-locker, then torque to the kit spec \u2014 typically 95-110 lb-ft for M14 hardware. Verify against the printed instructions; do not guess.\n6. Re-torque after the first 100 miles. Frame brackets settle, and a recovery point that backs off is worse than none because you will trust it.\n\nFor the rear, the honest call is whether you are also doing a bumper \u2014 the [[pw-rear-bumper-tire-carrier]] integrates rated D-rings and solves it in one move, otherwise the bolt-on rear frame points are the cheaper path.\n\nThe trap is trusting the factory tow hooks because they look heavy \u2014 they are rated for static towing only and have failed as projectiles under snatch loads, so this upgrade exists to close a genuine safety gap, not to look tougher. Match the mount to the strap: a rated mount with a worn or under-rated shackle is only as strong as its weakest link, so pair frame brackets with a soft shackle (Factor 55 ProLink class, 16,000+ lb) and inspect it for chafing every season. Never rig a recovery off the factory bed-mount spare cradle or a hitch pin that is not load-rated \u2014 and clear bystanders well outside the strap's reach before any kinetic pull. See [[pw-front-bumper]] for the integrated-recovery-point alternative if you are already swapping the bumper.","pros":["Factory tow hooks are not recovery-rated \u2014 this fixes a real safety gap","Frame-mount brackets are cheaper than a full bumper swap","Soft shackles + rated mounts = modern best-practice recovery setup","Compatible with the factory bumper (no need to swap the bumper to add real recovery points up front)"],"cons":["Rear still needs something \u2014 either a real bumper or the frame-mount points","Soft shackles have a finite life \u2014 inspect for chafing, replace every 5-10 years","Drilling the rear frame for added recovery points is committing","The \"looks beefy\" factory hooks must be ignored \u2014 they really aren't recovery-rated"]},{"id":"pw-winch-upgrade","title":"Winch Line and Capacity Upgrade for Power Wagon (Synthetic Rope + Warn ZEON)","vehicle":"power-wagon","category":"recovery","subcategory":"winch","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":3,"high":6},"cost_usd":{"low":350,"high":2400},"tools_required":["metric sockets","torque wrench","synthetic rope spooling jig (or patience)"],"skills_required":["wiring basics if upgrading the winch motor"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2014-2018","2019-2024","2025+"],"tags":["winch","recovery","synthetic","upgrade","premium"],"parts":[{"name":"Warn Synthetic Rope 7/16\" x 100' (replaces factory steel cable)","part_number":"WARN-87915","vendor":"Warn","price_estimate_usd":320},{"name":"Master Pull Superline XD 3/8\" x 100' synthetic","vendor":"Master Pull","price_estimate_usd":280},{"name":"Warn ZEON Platinum 12-S (12,000 lb, synthetic, integrated controls)","vendor":"Warn","price_estimate_usd":2100},{"name":"Hawse fairlead (aluminum) for synthetic rope conversion","vendor":"Warn","price_estimate_usd":90}],"alternatives":["factory-warn-winch-service","pw-recovery-gear-kit"],"citations":[{"title":"Warn synthetic rope \u2014 spooling and care instructions","url":"https://www.warn.com/synthetic-rope"},{"title":"Warn ZEON Platinum 12-S \u2014 winch specifications","url":"https://www.warn.com/"},{"title":"Master Pull Superline XD \u2014 synthetic winch rope specs","url":"https://www.masterpull.com/"}],"summary":"The Power Wagon ships with a factory Warn ZEON 12-S \u2014 a real winch, not a token. The two upgrades worth doing: swap the steel cable for synthetic rope (safer, lighter, more manageable to handle), and if you've added weight (bumper, armor, 37s pushing you over GVWR), step up the winch itself to a 12,000-lb Warn ZEON Platinum or similar.","description":"The factory winch on every modern Power Wagon (2014+) is the Warn ZEON 12-S \u2014 a 12,000-lb capacity winch with steel cable, mounted to a hidden front bumper bracket behind the plastic factory cover. It's a real winch, not a marketing winch. Most owners never need to upgrade the unit itself.\n\n**The synthetic rope swap** is the universal first upgrade. The factory steel cable is rated to 12k but has three failure modes:\n1. Frays \u2014 a single broken strand is a finger-impaling hazard during handling\n2. Stores kinetic energy under load \u2014 if it snaps, it can kill someone\n3. Heavy \u2014 about 25 lb of cable + drum, all unsprung at the front\n\nSynthetic rope (12-strand UHMWPE, brand-name Dyneema) at $300 for a 7/16\" x 100' Warn rope solves all three:\n- No frays \u2014 the rope wears smooth\n- Failure mode is \"pop\" not \"whip\" \u2014 synthetic doesn't store kinetic energy\n- About 5 lb total (80% weight reduction)\n- Floats, doesn't rust, more manageable to spool\n\nMandatory accessory: an aluminum hawse fairlead replaces the steel roller fairlead (steel rollers chew synthetic rope). $90 from Warn.\n\n**Winch upgrade** \u2014 if you've added a 200 lb bumper, 150 lb of armor, and 100+ lb of bigger tires, your loaded weight is well above factory. The 12k winch is still adequate for most recoveries (rule of thumb: 1.5x vehicle weight), but for serious self-recovery from deep mud or snow, a 16,500-lb winch (Warn 16.5ti) or a Warn ZEON Platinum 12-S with the upgraded clutch is worth the money. Plan $1,800-2,400 for the unit plus install.\n\nThe Power Wagon's factory wiring + battery handles the factory winch fine. If you upgrade to a higher-capacity winch, check the battery \u2014 a single Group 65 may struggle with sustained 400+ amp pulls. Consider dual battery or an Optima Yellowtop upgrade.\n\nInstall for synthetic rope swap is 30 minutes: pull the cable off the drum, mount the synthetic rope's end loop, spool it on under hand tension. Most kits include the spooling washer and detailed instructions.\n\nThis is the upgrade nearly every owner should do, and it's a half-hour job with two real safety steps you can't skip.\n\n1. Power the steel cable out under no load and disconnect it from the drum anchor. Wear leather gloves \u2014 a used steel cable almost always has a broken strand waiting to bite.\n2. Swap the fairlead. Pull the steel roller fairlead and bolt on the aluminum hawse. This is mandatory, not optional \u2014 steel rollers and sharp edges saw through synthetic rope. Deburr any rough edge on the bumper opening while you're in there.\n3. Anchor the synthetic rope's drum end per the winch maker's method (set screw or clamp), then spool it on under hand tension, keeping the wraps tight and even. Loose wraps let a loaded top wrap bury itself between lower wraps and damage the rope.\n4. Seat the line: do one moderate-load pull against an immovable anchor to bed the wraps before you ever trust it for a real recovery.\n5. Keep the rope clear of the exhaust and headers when routing \u2014 synthetic melts where steel only heats.\n\nIf the factory unit ever acts up rather than the line, the motor/contactor service is covered in [[factory-warn-winch-service]]; stage a proper [[pw-recovery-gear-kit]] (rated shackles, tree saver, damper) before you need it.\n\nA bigger winch pulls bigger amps, and the Power Wagon's single Group 65 battery is the limit before the winch is. Sustained 400+ amp pulls on an upgraded 16.5k winch will drag the system voltage down and can leave you with a no-start after a long recovery \u2014 audit the battery and alternator, and consider a [[pw-dual-battery]] setup before stepping up winch capacity.\n\nInspect synthetic rope every trip. Run it through a gloved hand looking for fuzzing, glazing (heat damage), or core showing through the sheath; retire the rope at the first sign rather than gambling on a loaded pull. And always lay a winch damper over the line during a pull \u2014 synthetic fails with a \"pop\" rather than a steel cable's lethal whip, but a thrown shackle or fairlead still carries deadly energy. Treat every winch pull as a load that can kill, because it can.","pros":["Synthetic rope is the universal upgrade \u2014 safer, lighter, more manageable","Factory ZEON 12-S is a real winch and rarely needs replacement","Hawse fairlead + synthetic rope is $400 of safety and convenience","Compatible with factory winch wiring and controls"],"cons":["Synthetic rope wears faster than steel (5-10 year life vs 20+) with regular use","Synthetic rope can melt against hot exhaust \u2014 keep clear of headers","Upgrading the winch unit itself is $2k+ \u2014 only worth it for serious self-recovery use","Higher-capacity winch needs charging system audit (battery, alternator)"]},{"id":"pw-steering-gear","title":"Steering Gear Box and Track Bar Service \u2014 Power Wagon","vehicle":"power-wagon","category":"steering","subcategory":"steering-gear","type":"procedure","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":4},"cost_usd":{"low":60,"high":700},"tools_required":["floor jack + rated stands","pry bar (for play check)","metric sockets incl. large (21mm/36mm)","torque wrench (high range)","pitman arm puller (if removing box)","power-steering fluid"],"skills_required":["suspension/steering diagnosis","torquing large fasteners to spec"],"build_tiers":["stock","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["2014-2018","2019-2024","2025+"],"tags":["steering","steering-box","track-bar","wander","death-wobble","safety"],"parts":[{"name":"Track bar (heavy-duty, with new bushings/ball-stud)","vendor":"Synergy / Carli / Mopar","price_estimate_usd":250},{"name":"Steering gear box (remanufactured or upgraded)","vendor":"Mopar / Redhead","price_estimate_usd":350},{"name":"Steering box brace / stabilizer (sector-shaft support)","vendor":"Synergy / Blue Top","price_estimate_usd":150},{"name":"Power-steering fluid (Mopar spec)","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":12}],"citations":[{"title":"Synergy Manufacturing \u2014 Ram HD track bar and steering upgrades","url":"https://www.synergymfg.com/"},{"title":"RamForum \u2014 2500 steering wander / track bar / gearbox diagnosis","url":"https://www.ramforum.com/"}],"alternatives":["pw-tie-rod-drag-link","pw-front-brakes"],"summary":"Steering wander, a clunk over bumps, or the dreaded front-end shimmy on a solid-axle Power Wagon almost always starts at the **track bar**, not the steering box \u2014 diagnose in that order. The track bar locates the front axle side-to-side, and its bushings and ball-stud wear with off-road use, letting the axle move under the truck. The steering gear box itself can develop sector-shaft play at higher mileage. This is safety-critical: a worn track bar is a primary trigger for the shimmy that solid-axle trucks are prone to. Inspect before every trail trip.","description":"The Power Wagon's solid front axle gives it the articulation and durability that make it the truck it is, but a solid axle steers through a track bar, drag link, and recirculating-ball gear box that all wear. Loose steering is not something to live with \u2014 on a heavy truck at speed it is a handling and safety problem. The good news is that the diagnosis follows a clear order and most fixes are bolt-on.\n\nWith the truck on the ground and a helper rocking the steering wheel back and forth, watch the front end: motion at the **track bar** ends (axle-side bracket or frame-side mount) before the wheels turn means a worn track bar bushing or ball-stud \u2014 the most common cause of wander and shimmy. Next watch the **drag link and tie rod** ends for play. Only after those check out do you look at the **steering box**: with the wheels straight, feel for slop in the pitman arm / sector shaft as the wheel is rocked \u2014 excess sector-shaft play points to a worn or loose gear box. Also confirm the steering-box mounting bolts are tight; a loose box mimics internal wear and can crack the frame mount over time.\n\nA worn track bar is replaced as a unit (the OEM bushings are not separately serviceable on most). Heavy-duty aftermarket bars from the off-road suspension makers use a rebuildable ball-stud and greasable bushings that hold up far better under trail use. Replace with the suspension at ride height, torque the frame-side and axle-side fasteners to the service-manual values, and re-check after a few hundred miles \u2014 track bar fasteners can settle. A correctly fitted, tight track bar is the single biggest improvement to a wandering Power Wagon.\n\nIf the box has genuine sector-shaft play, fit a remanufactured or upgraded gear box. On lifted or hard-wheeled trucks a **steering-box brace** (sector-shaft support) ties the bottom of the sector shaft to the frame, cutting flex and protecting the box and frame mount \u2014 a worthwhile add for any built Power Wagon. Bleed and top the power-steering fluid after any box work, and verify there is no leak at the sector-shaft seal. Treat any return of shimmy as a stop-and-inspect event, not something to drive through.\n\nAny front lift steepens the track bar's operating angle, and that has two consequences worth checking on a leveled or lifted Power Wagon. First, the axle shifts toward the driver side at the new ride height \u2014 a properly set adjustable track bar re-centers it (measure hub-to-frame on both sides). Second, a steeper bar converts more of every bump into lateral load at the bushings and ball-stud, so the same parts wear faster than they did at stock height. If the truck got a [budget leveling kit](/db/power-wagon/pw-budget-lift/) or a [mid-height lift](/db/power-wagon/pw-mid-lift/) and the steering has never felt as tight since, inspect the track bar first and consider the heavy-duty adjustable bar the corrective, not the upgrade. The [Articulink front-end explainer](/db/power-wagon/articulink-front-end-explained/) covers how the link-coil front end locates the axle and why the track bar takes the brunt.\n\nDiagnosis is free and takes fifteen minutes with a helper \u2014 do it before buying anything. A heavy-duty track bar runs $250\u2013500 in parts and about an hour of labor (one frame-side bolt, one axle-side bolt, torque at ride height); a shop will charge 1\u20131.5 hours. A remanufactured or upgraded steering box is $350\u2013700 in parts plus 2\u20133 hours of labor including the pitman-arm pull and fluid bleed; add an alignment and steering-wheel re-center after either job. The sector-shaft brace is $150 and under an hour with hand tools. Budget the full-front-end inspection into every one of these jobs \u2014 the [tie rod and drag link guide](/db/power-wagon/pw-tie-rod-drag-link/) covers the joints that commonly fail alongside the track bar, and replacing one worn joint while leaving its neighbors is how a truck ends up back on the lift in six months.","pros":["A tight track bar cures most wander and shimmy on a solid-axle truck","The diagnosis follows a clear order \u2014 no parts-cannon needed","A steering brace protects the box and frame mount on built trucks"],"cons":["A worn track bar is a real safety item, not a comfort complaint","Steering-box replacement needs a pitman-arm puller and high-torque tools","Shimmy can have stacked causes; verify every joint, not the first one found"]},{"id":"pw-tie-rod-drag-link","title":"Tie Rod and Drag Link Replacement \u2014 Power Wagon","vehicle":"power-wagon","category":"steering","subcategory":"linkage","type":"procedure","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":1.5,"high":4},"cost_usd":{"low":150,"high":600},"tools_required":["floor jack + rated stands","tie-rod / pickle-fork separator or ball-joint press","metric + large wrenches","torque wrench","tape measure (for toe)","penetrating oil"],"skills_required":["steering linkage work","setting toe well enough to drive to an alignment"],"build_tiers":["stock","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2014-2018","2019-2024","2025+"],"tags":["steering","tie-rod","drag-link","alignment","wander","safety"],"parts":[{"name":"Tie rod assembly (HD forged, with new ends)","vendor":"Synergy / Carli / Mopar","price_estimate_usd":250},{"name":"Drag link assembly (HD, adjustable)","vendor":"Synergy / Carli / Mopar","price_estimate_usd":200},{"name":"Steering linkage cotter pins / castle nuts hardware","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":10},{"name":"Grease + grease gun (for serviceable joints)","vendor":"generic","price_estimate_usd":15}],"citations":[{"title":"Synergy Manufacturing \u2014 Ram HD heavy-duty steering linkage","url":"https://www.synergymfg.com/"},{"title":"RamForum \u2014 2500 tie rod / drag link wear and replacement","url":"https://www.ramforum.com/"}],"alternatives":["pw-steering-gear","pw-front-brakes"],"summary":"Worn tie rod and drag link ends cause loose, wandering steering and uneven tire wear, and on a heavy solid-axle truck they are a safety item \u2014 a failed end means a loss of steering. The factory linkage is adequate for stock use; trucks that wheel hard or run larger tires bend or wear the stock parts and benefit from forged heavy-duty replacements. Budget $150-$600 depending on parts grade, and always finish with a professional alignment. You can set toe well enough to drive, but the truck needs a proper alignment to wear tires evenly.","description":"The Power Wagon steers its solid front axle through a tie rod (connecting the two wheels) and a drag link (connecting the steering box to the passenger-side knuckle, in the typical HD layout). Both use tapered ball-stud ends that wear with miles and trail abuse. Replacing worn ends restores tight, predictable steering and stops the inner-and-outer edge tire wear that loose linkage causes.\n\nLoose, vague on-center steering, a clunk when you transition direction, and feathered or edge-worn front tires all point at the linkage. With the front lifted and the wheels off the ground, grab each front tire at 9-and-3 and push-pull: play there with the steering locked indicates a tie rod end. Grab at 12-and-6 to separate that from a wheel bearing or ball joint. Have a helper rock the steering wheel while you watch the drag link ends for any lash. Any torn boot on a ball-stud end means grit has gotten in and the joint is on borrowed time.\n\nSoak the castle-nut threads and the tapered studs in penetrating oil. Remove the cotter pin and castle nut, then separate the taper \u2014 a tie-rod separator or ball-joint press is the clean way; a pickle fork works but tears the boot, so only fork a part you are replacing. Transfer the measurements: count the threads or measure the old assembly length so the new tie rod goes back at roughly the same length, preserving toe well enough to drive. Torque the studs to the service-manual values and install **new** cotter pins \u2014 a steering fastener without its cotter pin is a failure waiting to happen. Heavy-duty forged tie rods and drag links from the off-road makers resist bending on larger tires and use rebuildable, greasable ends.\n\nAfter replacement, set toe close with a tape measure (front-of-tire vs rear-of-tire distance) so the truck tracks straight and is safe to drive, then get a professional alignment to set toe, and check caster, precisely \u2014 correct caster is what gives a solid-axle truck its on-center stability and resistance to shimmy. Re-torque and re-inspect after the first few hundred miles. Treat any steering looseness or a torn boot as a near-term safety repair, not a someday item.\n\nOn a heavy solid-axle truck, worn steering linkage is one of the ingredients in **death wobble** \u2014 the violent, self-feeding front-end shimmy a bump can trigger at speed. Worn tie-rod or drag-link ends add the free play that lets the front tires shake, and once it starts the only safe response is to **slow down firmly and pull over**; it usually calms below about 45 mph. Linkage is rarely the sole cause \u2014 a loose **track bar**, worn ball joints, insufficient caster, or an out-of-balance tire usually share the blame \u2014 so treat a shimmy as a whole-front-end inspection, not a single-part swap. Check the [steering gear/box](/db/power-wagon/pw-steering-gear/) for lash and review how the front geometry moves through travel in the [Articulink front-end explainer](/db/power-wagon/articulink-front-end-explained/) as part of the same look, and verify tire balance before you condemn the linkage.\n\nFor a stock-tire truck that simply wore out its ends, **Mopar or Moog replacement** parts restore it for **$150-250** and are perfectly adequate. Once you run larger, heavier tires or wheel hard, the factory tie rod can **bend** in a hard hit \u2014 this is where forged HD linkage from **Synergy, Carli, or Apex** ($250-350 for the tie rod, similar for the drag link) earns its keep: thicker forged bar, larger tapered studs, and **rebuildable, greasable ends** you service instead of replace. Match this work to the rest of the front end \u2014 if the [front brakes](/db/power-wagon/pw-front-brakes/) are due or you're chasing a wander that traces back to tire condition in the [tire fitment guide](/db/power-wagon/power-wagon-tire-fitment/), doing them together saves a second alignment. Whatever grade you buy, torque every stud to spec and fit **new cotter pins** \u2014 the single most important non-negotiable on any steering fastener.","pros":["Restores tight, predictable steering and stops edge tire wear","HD forged linkage resists bending on larger tires and hard trail use","Greasable rebuildable ends last far longer than sealed factory parts"],"cons":["A failed steering end is a genuine safety hazard \u2014 do not defer it","Separating tapers cleanly needs the right tool to avoid wrecking good boots","A home toe-set gets you driving, but a real alignment is non-negotiable"]},{"id":"articulink-front-end-explained","title":"Articulink Front Suspension: What It Does, What Wears Out, and When to Inspect It","vehicle":"power-wagon","category":"suspension","subcategory":"front_suspension","type":"diagnostic","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":0.5,"high":2.0},"cost_usd":{"low":0,"high":1800},"tools_required":["pry bar","floor jack","jack stands","flashlight","dial indicator or tape measure","grease gun","scan tool"],"skills_required":["suspension inspection","basic understanding of control arm geometry"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2014-2018","2019-present"],"tags":["articulink","suspension","front-axle","wear-points","inspection"],"parts":[{"name":"Articulink joint rebuild kit","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":280},{"name":"Replacement control arm assembly with high-misalignment joint","vendor":"Carli Suspension","price_estimate_usd":480},{"name":"Joint boot / dust seal","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":35}],"citations":[{"title":"Carli Suspension \u2014 Ram HD / Power Wagon front end components","url":"https://carlisuspension.com/power-wagon/"},{"title":"Ram Power Wagon suspension service information","url":"https://www.mopar.com/en-us/care/service-records.html"},{"title":"Ram Trucks \u2014 2500 Power Wagon specifications","url":"https://www.ramtrucks.com/ram-2500/specs.power-wagon.html"}],"alternatives":["pw-tie-rod-drag-link","sway-bar-disconnect-service"],"summary":"Articulink is Ram's name for the high-movement pivot joints at the axle end of the Power Wagon's front control arms, introduced with the 2014 truck. They allow the front axle to move through far more travel than a bushing-only setup, and they are a wear item. **Any perceptible lateral play at the axle end of a control arm means service now, not later** \u2014 worn joints degrade steering precision, accelerate tire wear, and on a lifted truck they are a primary contributor to death wobble.","description":"The Power Wagon's front end is a **link/coil solid axle**: an AAM 9.25\" axle on coil springs, located by upper and lower control arms and a track bar. On a conventional truck, every one of those arms pivots on rubber or polyurethane bushings at both ends. Bushings flex to permit axle movement, but flex has a hard limit \u2014 past a certain angle the rubber stops deforming and the axle stops moving, which is what pins an inside front wheel in the air on a step-over.\n\nArticulink replaces the **axle-end** pivot with a **ball-and-socket-style joint that rotates on multiple axes** rather than relying on material deformation. The frame-end bushings stay conventional. The result is that the axle can twist and articulate through a much wider range before anything binds.\n\nRam markets this as a substantial articulation gain over the pre-2014 Power Wagon; treat any specific percentage figure as a marketing number rather than a measured one. What is not in dispute is the trail behavior \u2014 the inside front tire stays loaded longer on side-hills and step-overs, which is exactly where a heavy truck loses traction.\n\nThe **electronic sway bar disconnect** is the other half of the system. Below 18 mph in 4WD the front bar releases, and with the bar out of the picture the Articulink joints carry the full articulation load. That interaction is why a truck with a stuck-connected sway bar never shows the articulation it paid for \u2014 see the [sway bar disconnect service guide](/db/power-wagon/sway-bar-disconnect-service/) before blaming the joints.\n\n| Symptom | Most likely cause | Urgency |\n|---|---|---|\n| Vague, wandering steering on highway; constant small corrections | Lateral play in Articulink joints | Service now |\n| Clunk or pop from the front over small bumps, worse at low speed | Worn joint or a dry, unlubricated pivot | Service now |\n| Grease visible on the arm or a torn rubber boot | Failed boot, contaminated joint | Replace boot immediately; joint is on borrowed time |\n| Inner-edge or feathered front tire wear | Joint play corrupting toe under load | Service, then align |\n| Violent front-end oscillation after a bump (death wobble) | Joint play compounding with track bar, drag link, or damper wear | **Stop driving. Inspect the whole front end.** |\n\n**The joints themselves** are the primary wear item. New, they have essentially zero lateral play. They wear from the inside out after water intrusion or repeated high-load articulation without regreasing, and the resulting slop shows up first as imprecise steering.\n\n**The boots** are the leading indicator. Cracked or torn boots let grit in and grease out, and a contaminated joint fails in a fraction of its normal life. Hot-climate trucks crack boots faster; a boot is inexpensive and a joint is not.\n\n**The frame-end bushings** are conventional and wear on a conventional schedule \u2014 expect **80,000-120,000 miles** before measurable deflection.\n\nDo this on jack stands with the front wheels hanging free, so the joints are unloaded and any play is detectable.\n\n1. Lift the front of the truck and support the **frame** on jack stands. Confirm the truck is stable before getting under it.\n2. Grab each front tire at **9 and 3 o'clock** and push/pull sharply while a helper watches the axle end of the control arms. Movement at the tire that shows up as movement **in the joint** is joint play; movement that appears at the wheel bearing or ball joint is a different problem. Watch the part, not the tire.\n3. Repeat at **6 and 12 o'clock** to check vertical play and separate joint wear from ball joint wear.\n4. Visually inspect every **boot** with a flashlight \u2014 look for cracks, tears, and grease weeping out of the seal.\n5. Pry gently against the axle end of each control arm with a bar. **Any perceptible movement between the arm eye and the axle bracket is a failing joint.**\n6. On 2014-2018 trucks, look hardest at the **lower** joints \u2014 they carry more load than the uppers and generally wear first.\n7. While you are there, check the **track bar ends, drag link, and tie rod** ([tie rod and drag link guide](/db/power-wagon/pw-tie-rod-drag-link/)). Front-end play is rarely isolated to one component, and replacing one worn part while leaving three others is how people chase death wobble for a year.\n\nThere is no \"monitor it\" tier here. Play at these joints puts imprecise forces into the steering geometry, and the truck's mass amplifies every bit of it.\n\nRam does not publish a hard interval. A workable schedule from owner practice:\n\n- **Inspect at 40,000 miles**, then **every 20,000 miles** after\n- **Inspect every 10,000 miles** on a truck that sees regular trail use or water crossings\n- **Regrease at every inspection** if the boots are intact, using the grease specification in your service literature \u2014 see the [fluids and service guide](/db/power-wagon/pw-fluids-service/) for the rest of the chassis schedule\n- **Inspect immediately** after any deep water crossing, and after any impact hard enough to knock the alignment out\n\nIf you are lifting the truck, inspect these joints during the build while the front end is already apart \u2014 the [4\" mid-lift guide](/db/power-wagon/pw-mid-lift/) covers the geometry work, and a lift increases the static angle these joints operate at, so starting with worn ones is buying a problem.\n\n| Job | Typical cost |\n|---|---|\n| Boot replacement (per joint, DIY) | $35-60 |\n| Mopar joint rebuild kit (per joint, parts) | $250-320 |\n| Shop labor per joint | $120-180 |\n| **OEM joint replaced at a shop, per corner** | **$280-450** |\n| **Full four-joint refresh** | **$1,000-1,800** |\n| Aftermarket control arms with heavier joints (Carli class, per pair) | $480-600 |\n| Four-wheel alignment afterward (required) | $150-250 |\n\nThe decision point: if you are replacing more than two worn OEM joints on a truck that sees real trail use, price the **aftermarket arms with serviceable, higher-misalignment joints** against the OEM rebuild. The aftermarket route costs more up front and is rebuildable rather than disposable, which changes the math over the second and third service interval. For a mostly-street truck, the OEM parts are the sensible spend.","pros":["Real, measurable off-road articulation gain over conventional link/coil geometry","Bolt-in design is serviceable without specialty press tooling in most cases","OEM parts available through Mopar dealers, with an aftermarket upgrade path"],"cons":["Adds wear points a conventional truck does not have","Joint replacement is not cheap, and a full refresh runs four figures","Cracked boots are a maintenance item most owners never check until the joint is already contaminated","Worn joints are a contributing cause of death wobble on lifted trucks, which makes neglect a safety issue rather than a comfort one"]},{"id":"pw-budget-lift","title":"Budget Lift Kit for the Power Wagon \u2014 2\"-2.5\" Front Leveling on a Solid Axle","vehicle":"power-wagon","category":"suspension","type":"kit","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":3,"high":5},"cost_usd":{"low":130,"high":900},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","socket set","torque wrench","pry bar","jack stand or second jack to support the axle"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","wheel removal","solid-axle suspension familiarity"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2016-2024"],"tags":["lift","suspension","leveling","budget"],"parts":[{"name":"Rough Country 2.5\" Leveling Kit (coil spacers) for Ram 2500 4WD","vendor":"Rough Country","price_estimate_usd":130},{"name":"BDS 2\" Leveling Kit for Ram 2500 4WD","vendor":"BDS Suspension","price_estimate_usd":300},{"name":"Thuren Fabrication 2\" front leveling coils for Ram 2500","vendor":"Thuren Fabrication","price_estimate_usd":430},{"name":"Carli Suspension Leveling Kit for Ram 2500 (coils + shock package tiers)","vendor":"Carli Suspension","price_estimate_usd":700}],"citations":[{"title":"BDS Suspension \u2014 2\" Leveling Kit, Ram 2500 4WD","url":"https://bds-suspension.com/products/2-inch-leveling-kit-ram-2500-w-rear-air-ride-14-24-4wd"},{"title":"Carli Suspension \u2014 Ram 2500 leveling and suspension systems","url":"https://carlisuspension.com/"},{"title":"Thuren Fabrication \u2014 Ram HD coils and suspension","url":"https://thuren.net/"}],"alternatives":["pw-mid-lift","pw-lift-leveling-kit"],"summary":"A budget lift on the Power Wagon means front leveling coils or coil spacers on the solid front axle \u2014 $130 to $900, no cutting, no geometry kit required at this height. The rear five-link coil suspension stays stock: the factory nose-low rake is the whole problem, and 2\"\u20132.5\" up front cures it.","description":"The Power Wagon runs a coil-sprung solid front axle \u2014 an AAM 9.25 beam located by upper and lower control arms and a track bar, with separate coil springs and shocks. There are no struts and no CV half-shafts up front, which is exactly why a budget lift is mechanically honest on this truck: you are swapping or spacing a coil spring on an axle that does not care much about 2\" of extra height. The rear is a five-link coil setup, and it stays untouched \u2014 Ram builds the truck with the nose lower than the tail so it levels out under payload. Unloaded, that rake costs you visual stance and, more importantly, approach angle.\n\nWhat 2\"\u20132.5\" of front lift buys: a level truck, meaningfully better approach angle for trail entry, and clearance toward 35\" tires without significant trimming. What it does not buy: any of the geometry correction, longer-travel damping, or rear height that define a real suspension system \u2014 that territory starts at the [mid-lift tier](/db/power-wagon/pw-mid-lift/).\n\n**Coil spacers ($130\u2013180).** Rough Country's 2.5\" kit stacks a spacer above the factory front coil. Cheapest path to level, fully reversible, rides exactly like stock because it is stock \u2014 same spring, same shock. The factory shock is near its extension limit at 2.5\", so droop travel shrinks slightly.\n\n**Replacement leveling coils ($300\u2013450).** BDS's 2\" kit and Thuren's 2\" front coils replace the spring instead of spacing it. Thuren's coils are wound softer than factory for ride quality on washboard and are the standout at this price \u2014 this is where most owners who wheel the truck should land. Pair with fresh dampers if the factory shocks have miles on them; see the [shock guide](/db/power-wagon/pw-shocks/) for what actually fits a leveled truck.\n\n**Coils plus matched shocks ($600\u2013900+).** Carli's leveling packages bundle their coils with tuned 2.0 shocks. This is the top of \"budget\" and the bottom of their real systems \u2014 buy it if the plan stops here, skip it if a full mid-lift is coming later.\n\nThe track bar and drag link now sit at a slightly steeper angle. At 2\"\u20132.5\" this is within what the factory geometry tolerates, but it is why the axle shifts a few millimeters toward the driver side and why the alignment matters. Caster stays close enough to spec that no correction parts are needed at this height \u2014 that changes above 3\", which is one reason the [mid-lift](/db/power-wagon/pw-mid-lift/) costs what it does. If the front end develops a shimmy after the lift, stop and inspect the track bar and its fasteners first; the [Articulink front-end explainer](/db/power-wagon/articulink-front-end-explained/) covers how this axle is located and why the track bar carries the load. And before sizing tires around the new height, read the [weight-reality guide](/db/power-wagon/hd-3-4-ton-weight-reality/) \u2014 a 3/4-ton on 35s asks more of brakes and axles than the same tires on a half-ton.","steps":["Break front lug nuts loose, raise the front, and support the frame on rated stands ahead of the front spring perches.","Remove the front wheels. Support the axle with the floor jack.","Unbolt the front sway-bar end links and the lower shock bolts so the axle can droop.","Lower the axle slowly until the front coils unload. On this suspension the coils lift out at full droop \u2014 no spring compressor is involved. If a coil binds, loosening the axle-side track bar bolt buys the last bit of droop.","Install the spacer on top of the factory coil (spacer kit) or seat the new coil (coil kit), making sure the spring end indexes into the perch pocket.","Raise the axle back into position, then reinstall shocks and sway-bar end links. Torque control-arm and track bar fasteners at ride height, not at droop \u2014 bushings torqued at droop bind and wear out early.","Reinstall wheels, torque lug nuts to Ram 2500 spec, and lower the truck.","Get a front-end alignment and have the shop check steering-wheel center \u2014 the track bar will pull the axle slightly off-center at the new height."],"pros":["Corrects factory nose-low rake and improves approach angle","2\"\u20132.5\" front lift clears 35\" tires with minor or no trimming","Coil spacers and coils are both reversible; no spring compressor or cutting involved","No caster or track bar correction parts required at this lift height"],"cons":["Front-only by design \u2014 the rear five-link coil suspension stays at factory height","Factory shocks run near their extension limit at 2.5\"; droop travel shrinks slightly","No geometry or damping upgrade \u2014 this is stance and approach angle, not performance","Track bar pulls the axle slightly off-center at the new height; alignment required"]},{"id":"pw-lift-aev-dualsport","title":"AEV DualSport 4.5\" Lift \u2014 Power Wagon (Full Short-Arm, L6)","vehicle":"power-wagon","category":"suspension","subcategory":"lift_kit","type":"kit","difficulty":5,"time_hours":{"low":14,"high":28},"cost_usd":{"low":5800,"high":9200},"tools_required":["floor jack rated to 3500 lb","jack stands x4","spring compressor","torque wrench (300+ lb-ft)","press for control-arm bushings","alignment gear","extra hands"],"skills_required":["expert wrenching","suspension rebuild experience"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["2014-2018","2019-2024","2025+"],"tags":["lift","short-arm","advanced","premium","long-arm-alternative","tire-fitment"],"parts":[{"name":"AEV DualSport RT 4.5\" Lift Kit (coils, shocks, UCAs, brackets, hardware)","part_number":"AEV-DSRT-PW","vendor":"AEV","price_estimate_usd":6200},{"name":"Carli Pintop 4\" Full Lift (alternative \u2014 coils + Fox 2.5 + UCAs)","vendor":"Carli Suspension","price_estimate_usd":7400},{"name":"Front-end alignment after install + shakedown alignment at 500 miles","vendor":"local shop","price_estimate_usd":300}],"alternatives":["pw-lift-mid-3in","pw-lift-coilover-swap"],"citations":[{"title":"AEV DualSport RT \u2014 Ram 2500/Power Wagon suspension system","url":"https://www.aev-conversions.com/"},{"title":"Carli Suspension Pintop \u2014 Power Wagon lift overview","url":"https://carlisuspension.com/"},{"title":"American Axle (AAM) 9.25\" front axle \u2014 geometry reference","url":"https://www.aam.com/"}],"summary":"4.5-5\" of real lift on a Power Wagon, the proper way: new coils, new long-stroke Fox or AEV shocks, replacement upper control arms (factory UCAs hit the coil at this lift), correction brackets, fully aligned. AEV DualSport is the reference kit. Carli Pintop is the boutique alternative. $6-9k done \u2014 this is the kit that lets you run 37s and wheel hard.","description":"The Power Wagon's factory upper control arm is the binding constraint at lifts above 3\". Lift the truck higher than ~3.5\" and the factory UCA actually contacts the coil spring at full droop. You need:\n\n1. **New coils** sized for the lift height + loaded weight\n2. **Replacement upper control arms** with revised geometry that clears the coils and restores ball joint working angle\n3. **Long-stroke shocks** (Fox 2.5 or AEV-spec) with reservoirs\n4. **Correction brackets** for the lower control arm mounting (if not integrated into the UCAs)\n5. **Sway bar drop brackets** (factory sway bar geometry goes wrong)\n6. **Pitman + idler arm** considerations for steering geometry\n7. **Brake line extensions** (factory lines too short)\n\n**AEV DualSport RT 4.5\"** ($6,200 kit) \u2014 the reference kit. Includes everything above. Engineered as a system. Mile-for-mile the most popular real lift on Power Wagons. AEV is owned by AAM (the axle maker) so their geometry is dialed-in for the AAM 9.25\" front axle.\n\n**Carli Pintop 4\"** ($7,400 kit) \u2014 the boutique alternative. Slightly different damping philosophy (Fox 2.5 with custom valving), tunable for desert running. The choice if you want adjustable shock valving and don't mind the premium. Both AEV and Carli are excellent \u2014 there's no wrong answer between them.\n\n**What this lift does that a 3\" lift doesn't:**\n- Clears 37\" tires with minimal trim (only the inner fender liner needs trimming)\n- Adds 5-6\" of effective ground clearance (vs 3\" for the L4 kit)\n- Larger shock travel = better high-speed off-road performance\n- Restores proper UCA angle so ball joints last 50k+ miles\n\n**What it costs beyond the kit itself:**\n- Installation: 14-28 hours DIY; $1,500-2,500 at a shop\n- Alignment: $150 immediate + $150 shakedown at 500 miles\n- Re-gear to 4.56 (if running 37s): $1,200-1,800 (see [[pw-regear-456]])\n- 37\" tires: $2,400-3,200 set\n- Wheels or spacers: $300-2,000\n\n**Total realistic build cost for \"4.5\" lift + 37s + re-gear\":** $12-16k. Plan accordingly.\n\nThis is the lift for a Power Wagon you actually wheel hard or do extended overland trips with a loaded rig. For occasional weekend trail use on 33-35s, the 3\" kit ([[pw-lift-mid-3in]]) is plenty.\n\nThis is a 14-28 hour expert-level job that disassembles the entire front suspension. The order matters, and a few steps are where DIYers get hurt or get it wrong.\n\n1. Support the frame on jack stands and the front axle on a separate floor jack \u2014 the coil is captive and carries spring load until the lower control arm is dropped under control.\n2. Remove the front coils and factory shocks, then the factory upper control arms. At this lift the factory UCAs are the binding part; they're coming out for the kit's replacement arms regardless.\n3. Press in the new UCA bushings and install the replacement arms, setting the included correction brackets so the lower-arm and ball-joint angles land in spec. This is the step that determines whether your ball joints last 50k miles or 15k.\n4. Install the new coils and long-stroke reservoir shocks, then the sway-bar drop brackets and extended brake lines. Don't reuse the factory brake lines \u2014 they're too short and will tear at full droop.\n5. Set ride height, then drive it straight to an alignment rack. Run it for 500 miles and align it again \u2014 a fresh lift settles, and the shakedown alignment is what saves your new tires.\n\nIf you're running 37s with this lift, re-gearing to 4.56 is part of the build, not an afterthought \u2014 see [[pw-regear-456]]. Owners who want height without this much surgery should look at the [[pw-lift-coilover-swap]] or 3\" [[pw-lift-mid-3in]] path instead.\n\nThe factory upper control arm contacting the coil at full droop is not a maybe \u2014 it's the whole reason this kit replaces the UCAs. If you try to reach 4.5\" on factory arms or a spacer kit, you'll bind the suspension, wreck ball joints early, and lose articulation. Buy the engineered system or stay at 3\".\n\nBudget for the whole iceberg, not only the kit. The $6,200 box is half the story: alignment twice, 37\" tires, a re-gear, wheels or spacers, and likely shop labor push a real \"4.5-inch + 37s\" build to $12-16k. Going in expecting the kit price and discovering the rest mid-project is how trucks end up sitting on jack stands for months. And after install, re-torque the control-arm and track-bar hardware after the first few hundred miles \u2014 a settling lift loosens fasteners that were dead-on at assembly.","pros":["Real 4.5-5\" of clearance with proper geometry","Clears 37s with minimal fender work","Engineered-as-a-system kits \u2014 no mixing-and-matching","Long-term reliability data from thousands of Power Wagon installs"],"cons":["$6-9k for the kit alone, $12-16k for the full build with tires + re-gear","14-28 hour DIY install \u2014 most owners pay a shop","Mandatory shakedown alignment at 500 miles (suspension settles)","Sway bar drop brackets affect on-road body control noticeably","Brake lines need replacement (extended factory lines or aftermarket Russell stainless)"]},{"id":"pw-lift-coilover-swap","title":"Long-Travel Coilover Conversion \u2014 Power Wagon (L7 / Race-Prep)","vehicle":"power-wagon","category":"suspension","subcategory":"long_arm","type":"swap","difficulty":5,"time_hours":{"low":30,"high":80},"cost_usd":{"low":9500,"high":24000},"tools_required":["welder (MIG/TIG)","frame jig","shop press","alignment gear","torque wrench (300+ lb-ft)","fabrication mindset"],"skills_required":["expert fabrication","professional shop or shop-equivalent home garage"],"build_tiers":["rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["2014-2018","2019-2024","2025+"],"tags":["long-arm","coilover","race","premium","fabrication","weld-required"],"parts":[{"name":"Long-arm coilover conversion (long control arms, coilover mounts, frame brackets, bump-stops)","vendor":"Carli / Thuren / Kore","price_estimate_usd":12000},{"name":"Fabricated 4-link coilover conversion (alternative geometry \u2014 custom 4-link + coilover mounts)","vendor":"Kore / custom fab shop","price_estimate_usd":14000},{"name":"King 3.0 coilovers w/ remote reservoir + compression adjuster (front)","vendor":"King Shocks","price_estimate_usd":4800},{"name":"Professional install (frame welding, alignment, bump-stop tuning)","vendor":"specialty shop","price_estimate_usd":4000}],"citations":[{"title":"Carli Suspension \u2014 Ram HD (2500) solid-axle coilover systems","url":"https://carlisuspension.com/"},{"title":"Thuren Fabrication \u2014 Ram 2500 solid-axle suspension and coilover conversion","url":"https://thuren.com/"},{"title":"King Shocks \u2014 3.0 coilover application and tuning","url":"https://kingshocks.com/"}],"alternatives":["pw-lift-aev-dualsport"],"summary":"The end-state Power Wagon lift: a coilover conversion that replaces the factory coil-and-shock front suspension on the solid axle. A long-arm or fabricated 4-link kit, King 3.0 coilovers, frame brackets welded in, 12-14\" of front travel. $15-24k done. This is race-prep \u2014 not for normal trail use, not for daily driving (mostly).","description":"Long-travel coilover conversions replace the Power Wagon's entire factory front suspension with race-prep hardware:\n\n- New long control arms (or a fabricated 4-link) locating the solid axle\n- Coilovers (typically King 3.0 or Fox 3.0) in place of the factory shock + coil\n- Welded-in frame brackets for the coilover upper mounts\n- Custom bumpstops, often hydraulic\n- 12-14\" of front wheel travel (vs. ~8\" stock, ~10\" with AEV DualSport)\n\n**Who buys this?**\n- Desert racers \u2014 KOH, Mint 400, Baja 1000 prep\n- Owners who do high-speed desert running 50+ days/year\n- Tinkerers who want to build the most capable Power Wagon possible without constraint\n\n**Who shouldn't buy this?**\n- Daily drivers \u2014 the ride quality on-road is firm to harsh, depending on tuning\n- Casual trail users \u2014 overkill for typical trail terrain\n- Anyone within 10 years of selling the truck \u2014 resale value of long-travel conversions is poor\n\n**Cost reality:**\n- Kit: $12,000-14,000\n- Coilovers (if not bundled): $4,500-5,500\n- Install at a specialty shop: $3,500-4,500\n- Alignment + shakedown: $300\n- Re-gear, tires, wheels, fenders: another $5,000-8,000\n\n**Total realistic build:** $25,000-35,000 above the truck itself.\n\n**Long-arm vs. 4-link** \u2014 both work on the solid axle. A long-arm coilover conversion (Carli, Thuren, Kore) keeps the axle located with longer control arms, is the more common and better-documented Power Wagon path, and bolts up more predictably. A fabricated 4-link is fully custom, lets a shop tune anti-squat and roll center, and is shop-built to the truck. Note that the \"long-travel\" Camburg and Total Chaos kits you see advertised are **IFS-truck** parts (Tacoma, 4Runner, half-ton) and do not fit the Power Wagon's solid front axle.\n\n**On-road behavior:** With proper tuning (King 3.0 valving + bumpstop setup), a long-travel Power Wagon can be tolerable on highway, but the steering can feel different after the caster and track-bar geometry change, and the ride is firmer than a Carli or AEV setup. It's a race truck that you drive on the road, not a road truck with race capability.\n\n**Honest framing:** if you have to ask whether you need long-travel, you don't. The AEV DualSport 4.5\" ([[pw-lift-aev-dualsport]]) covers 95% of off-road use cases at a third of the cost. This entry exists for completeness; consider it the upper bound of what's possible.\n\nThis is not a bolt-in kit, and treating it like one is how people end up with a truck that handles worse than stock. The work, in order:\n\n1. **Strip the factory front suspension** \u2014 coil springs, shocks, control arms, sway bar, and often the factory bump-stop pockets come out entirely.\n2. **Weld in the frame brackets** \u2014 the coilover upper mounts tie into the frame with welded gussets. This step demands a jig (or a fixture built from the kit's templates) so left and right are mirror-identical; a few millimeters of bracket misplacement changes alignment and shock angle on that corner.\n3. **Hang the new arms and coilovers** \u2014 the long control arms (or fabricated 4-link) and the King or Fox 3.0 coilovers, then set ride height by spring preload.\n4. **Tune the bump-stops** \u2014 hydraulic or progressive stops are matched to the available up-travel so the tire never contacts sheet metal at full compression. Getting this wrong cuts travel or punches the tire into the fender.\n5. **Align and shake down** \u2014 a specialty alignment, then a deliberate low-speed then mid-speed shakedown to re-check every fastener and the coilover valving before any high-speed running.\n\nBecause the geometry is custom, a re-gear and a fender/tire plan ([[pw-lift-aev-dualsport]] covers the milder path) are part of the same project, not afterthoughts.\n\nThe failure modes here are expensive. Welding frame brackets at home without a jig is the most common one \u2014 a corner that sits a few degrees off throws caster and camber out of range that no alignment can recover, and the fix is cutting the bracket back off. Steering feel gets slower with the longer arms, which is normal; harsh on-road ride usually means the bump-stops or coilover valving were never tuned for street use, not that the kit is wrong. And the resale math is unforgiving: a buyer who wants a stock-ish Power Wagon will not pay for a welded-in race front end, so build this for yourself, not for the next owner.","pros":["12-14\" of front travel \u2014 change-of-class off-road capability","Race-grade hardware throughout","The most capable Power Wagon possible at the factory drivetrain"],"cons":["$15,000-24,000 for the front-end conversion","Total build with tires + re-gear: $25-35k above factory","Requires welding + alignment specialty shop \u2014 not a DIY job","Resale value of long-travel conversions is poor","On-road behavior changes meaningfully","Not necessary for typical trail use"]},{"id":"pw-lift-leveling-kit","title":"Leveling Kit \u2014 Power Wagon (Booster Lite L1)","vehicle":"power-wagon","category":"suspension","subcategory":"lift_kit","type":"kit","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":4},"cost_usd":{"low":120,"high":280},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","metric sockets","torque wrench","PB Blaster"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2014-2018","2019-2024","2025+"],"tags":["booster-lite","leveling","lift","budget","beginner"],"parts":[{"name":"Rough Country 2\" Front Leveling Kit (Strut Spacers)","part_number":"RC-373","vendor":"Rough Country","price_estimate_usd":90},{"name":"Bilstein B6 5100 Shock with adjustable upper ride height (front, pair)","vendor":"Bilstein","price_estimate_usd":240}],"citations":[{"title":"Rough Country 2\" leveling kit \u2014 Ram 2500 application","url":"https://www.roughcountry.com/"},{"title":"Bilstein 5100 adjustable ride-height strut overview","url":"https://www.bilstein.com/us/en/products/5100-series/"}],"alternatives":["pw-lift-mid-3in","pw-budget-lift"],"summary":"The cheapest possible Power Wagon \"lift\": a pair of 2\" coilover spacers up front to level the truck's factory rake. Adds nothing functional \u2014 same shocks, same coil, same geometry. removes the factory ~1.5\" nose-down stance and lets you clear 33s with a slightly fuller look.","description":"The Power Wagon comes from the factory with ~1.5\" of forward rake \u2014 the front sits lower than the rear to compensate for the front winch + heavy plow-prep front clip. Visually it looks nose-low.\n\nA leveling kit corrects that look with a coilover spacer. Two paths:\n\n**Strut spacer kit** ($90) \u2014 A 2\" polyurethane or aluminum spacer that mounts on top of the factory coilover, pushing the body up away from the strut. Bolts on in 2 hours with hand tools. Keeps the factory shock and spring. Cheapest possible \"lift.\"\n\n**Bilstein B6 5100 adjustable** ($240 pair) \u2014 Replaces the factory front shock with a Bilstein 5100 that has an adjustable upper-spring perch. Set the perch to the \"1.85\"\" position and you get a level stance with better-than-factory damping. The right answer if you're also going to upgrade shocks (see [[pw-shocks]]) \u2014 kills two birds.\n\n**This is NOT a real lift.** It does nothing for:\n- Tire clearance beyond the factory 33s \u2014 you still get the same fender clearance\n- Off-road geometry \u2014 no extra ground clearance, no extra articulation\n- Component longevity \u2014 spacer-only kits stack preload on the factory coil\n\nIt IS useful for:\n- Cosmetic level stance\n- Clearance for a slightly taller AT tire (33.5-34\", e.g. Toyo Open Country AT III LT295/70R17)\n- The cheapest possible step before committing to a real lift\n\nIf you want actual capability, skip this and go to a 3\" coilover-replacement kit ([[pw-lift-mid-3in]]) or higher. If you want the level look and aren't planning to wheel hard, the Rough Country spacer is $90 well spent.\n\nThis is a front-only job \u2014 the rear keeps its factory ride height. Budget 2-4 hours for a first-timer with hand tools.\n\n1. **Break the lug nuts loose** while the wheels are on the ground (M14x1.5 lugs, 140 lb-ft). Then jack the front of the truck at the frame rail and set both sides on jack stands rated to at least 3,500 lb each. The Power Wagon front end is heavy \u2014 do not work off the jack alone.\n2. **Pull the front wheels** and let the lower control arm hang. There is no spring tension to fight on a strut-spacer install because you are not opening the coilover \u2014 the spacer mounts on top of the assembly.\n3. **Unbolt the three upper strut nuts** at the shock tower (typically 18mm). Support the coilover from below with a floor jack so it doesn't drop when the last nut comes off.\n4. **Drop the coilover slightly**, set the spacer on top of the factory upper mount, and re-stud or re-stack per the kit instructions (Rough Country RC-373 reuses the factory studs with the supplied spacer plate).\n5. **Torque the upper nuts** to the kit spec (usually 30-40 lb-ft on the spacer hardware \u2014 follow the printed sheet, not this number, since it varies by kit), reinstall the wheels, torque lugs to 140 lb-ft, and lower the truck.\n6. **Re-torque lugs after 50-100 miles** and get a front alignment \u2014 even 2\" of level changes toe enough to wear the inside edge of new tires.\n\nThe Bilstein 5100 path follows the same teardown but you swap the entire front shock and set the adjustable perch to the 1.85\" groove before reinstalling. Because you're removing a loaded coil-over, use a spring compressor or have the assembly re-sprung at a shop \u2014 see [[pw-shocks]] for the shock-swap detail.\n\n- **Get the alignment.** A leveling kit pulls camber and toe out of spec. Skipping the alignment is the single most common way owners chew through a fresh set of 33s in 10k miles.\n- **Don't stack a spacer on tired coils and call it a lift.** If your factory coils are sagging at 100k+ miles, a spacer only hides the sag \u2014 the spring is still soft and you'll bottom on bumps. At that point spend the money on real coils ([[pw-lift-mid-3in]]).\n- **Confirm winch and sensor clearance.** The 2\" of level can change how the factory front camera and parking sensors read. Most owners see no issue at 2\", but verify before you trust the backup display off-road.","pros":["Cheapest possible cosmetic correction","2-hour driveway install","Reversible \u2014 pull the spacers, you're back to stock","Keeps factory winch + bumper geometry intact (which a real lift can affect)"],"cons":["Provides zero off-road benefit","Spacer-only stacks preload on already-tired factory coils (shortens spring life)","Doesn't clear bigger than ~34\" tire","A waste of money if you're going to do a real lift in the next 12 months"]},{"id":"pw-lift-mid-3in","title":"3\" Mid Lift \u2014 Power Wagon (Coils + Shocks + Correction Brackets, L4)","vehicle":"power-wagon","category":"suspension","subcategory":"lift_kit","type":"kit","difficulty":4,"time_hours":{"low":8,"high":16},"cost_usd":{"low":1400,"high":2600},"tools_required":["floor jack rated to 3500 lb","jack stands x4","metric sockets through 22mm","torque wrench (250 lb-ft)"],"skills_required":["solid wrenching","spring removal","alignment after install"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2014-2018","2019-2024","2025+"],"tags":["lift","geometry-correction","correction-bracket","midgrade","tire-fitment"],"parts":[{"name":"Carli Suspension Backcountry 2.5\" Coil Spring Set + Bilstein 5160 Reservoir Shocks","part_number":"CS-PW-BC","vendor":"Carli Suspension","price_estimate_usd":1900},{"name":"Adjustable front track bar + caster correction (adjustable/longer control arms or drop bracket) \u2014 required at 3\"+","vendor":"Carli / Synergy / Thuren","price_estimate_usd":450},{"name":"Front-end alignment after install (shop)","vendor":"local alignment shop","price_estimate_usd":150}],"citations":[{"title":"Carli Suspension Backcountry 2.5\" system \u2014 Ram 2500/Power Wagon","url":"https://carlisuspension.com/"},{"title":"Synergy Manufacturing \u2014 Ram HD (2500) adjustable track bar and control arms","url":"https://www.synergymfg.com/"}],"alternatives":["pw-lift-leveling-kit","pw-lift-aev-dualsport","pw-shocks"],"summary":"A real 3\" lift on a Power Wagon costs $2,000-2,600 and adds the things a leveling kit doesn't: better coils tuned for added load, longer-stroke shocks, and the geometry correction \u2014 an adjustable track bar and caster correction \u2014 that keeps the alignment right once you lift a coil-sprung solid axle past 2\". The Carli Backcountry + Bilstein 5160 + adjustable-track-bar combo is the proven 3\" reference setup.","description":"The Power Wagon's coil-sprung solid front axle is geometry-sensitive \u2014 lift it more than 2\" and the axle shifts sideways on its track bar while caster rolls back, giving a truck that darts, wanders, and won't hold an alignment. A real 3\" lift includes:\n\n1. **New coils tuned for the 3\" height + your loaded weight** (Carli Backcountry coils support ~600-800 lb of added front-end load including bumper, winch upgrade, etc.)\n2. **New shocks with longer stroke + reservoir** for fade resistance (Bilstein 5160 reservoir at this tier; Fox 2.5 at the L5+ tier)\n3. **An adjustable track bar** to re-center the axle under the frame, plus **caster correction** (adjustable/longer control arms or a drop bracket) to reset the axle's caster\n4. **Alignment after install** (mandatory \u2014 even with brackets, settings drift)\n\n**Carli Backcountry 2.5\"** is the most popular mid-tier kit. Comes with progressive-rate coils that handle daily-driver ride quality well but firm up under load. Pair with Bilstein 5160 reservoir shocks (Carli sells the combo).\n\n**Geometry correction** restores the front end. Required at 3\"+. An adjustable track bar re-centers the axle and caster correction resets the control-arm angle \u2014 skip it and the truck wanders and chews the inside tire edges.\n\n**Why not Rough Country 3\" \"kit\"?** RC sells a $700 \"lift kit\" that's a coilover spacer + add-a-leaf. It hits 3\" of lift but doesn't include geometry correction, doesn't include real shocks, and stresses the factory components. It's a Booster Boost (L2) in disguise \u2014 fine for daily/light trail, NOT a real 3\" lift.\n\n**Tire clearance at 3\":** Fits 35x12.50R17 on factory wheels with 1.25\" spacers (see [[pw-wheel-spacers]]) and no rub at full articulation. 37s require more (4-5\" lift, see [[pw-lift-aev-dualsport]]).\n\n**Alignment specs after install:**\n- Caster: 4.5-5.0 degrees positive (factory is ~4.0; caster correction restores it)\n- Toe: 1/16\" toe-in\n- Camber: -0.3 to +0.3 (factory range)\n\nInstall is 8-16 hours for a competent DIY mechanic; about $800 of shop labor if you don't have spring tools.\n\nThis is a real wrenching job \u2014 front coilovers come out under spring load and the correction brackets bolt into the frame. The front coil springs unload once the axle droops, so no spring compressor is needed up front \u2014 but this is still heavy, awkward under-vehicle work.\n\n1. **Support the truck on four stands** rated to 3,500 lb each, wheels off, and let the front axle hang fully to unload the coil springs.\n2. **Remove the front shocks, then the front coil springs.** On a coil-sprung solid axle the coil and the shock are separate parts \u2014 with the axle hanging the coils are unloaded and lift straight out of the spring buckets, so there is no loaded coilover to compress. Keep the axle supported and fully drooped before pulling a coil so it stays unloaded. Set the new Carli Backcountry coils into the factory buckets.\n3. **Install the adjustable track bar and caster correction** (adjustable control arms or a drop bracket) per the instructions. This is the step that makes 3\" of lift hold an alignment \u2014 set the track bar so the axle is centered under the frame, then torque the frame hardware to the maker's spec.\n4. **Install the new 5160 reservoir shocks**, route the reservoir lines clear of the tire and exhaust, and torque the shock mounts to the kit spec.\n5. **Reinstall wheels, torque lugs to 140 lb-ft**, lower the truck, and let it settle by rolling it a few feet before final ride-height check.\n6. **Drive straight to an alignment shop.** A 3\" lift without alignment eats ball joints and inside tire edges within 15-20k miles. Hand the shop the caster/toe/camber targets above.\n\n| Line item | Part | Typical cost |\n|---|---|---|\n| Coils + shocks | Carli Backcountry 2.5\" + Bilstein 5160 reservoir (CS-PW-BC) | ~$1,900 |\n| Geometry correction | Adjustable track bar + caster correction | ~$450 |\n| Alignment | Local shop, post-install | ~$150 |\n| Optional labor | Shop install if you lack spring tools | ~$800 |\n\nDIY total lands around $2,000-2,600; add the ~$800 labor only if you're not pulling the coilovers yourself. Spacers for 35s on factory wheels are a separate ~$280-360 ([[pw-wheel-spacers]]). If 37s are the real goal, this kit is the wrong tier \u2014 step to a 4-5\" system and plan the 4.56 regear ([[pw-regear-456]]) at the same time.","pros":["Real 3\" lift with real geometry correction","Pairs with 35s on factory wheels + spacers","Coils tuned for loaded weight (winch + bumper) \u2014 ride doesn't sag","Carli Backcountry has strong long-term reliability data"],"cons":["$2,000-2,600 done \u2014 3x the price of a Rough Country spacer-only kit","Requires geometry correction (track bar + caster) \u2014 no shortcut","Alignment after install is mandatory (+$150)","Coil and shock R&R plus track-bar work \u2014 heavy under-vehicle job, not hand-tools-only"]},{"id":"pw-mid-lift","title":"Mid-Tier Lift for the Power Wagon \u2014 4\"-6\" Full Kit ($1,500\u2013$4,000)","vehicle":"power-wagon","category":"suspension","type":"kit","difficulty":4,"time_hours":{"low":10,"high":16},"cost_usd":{"low":1500,"high":4000},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","torque wrench","socket set","breaker bar","coil spring compressor","pry bar","angle finder","penetrating oil","alignment shop appointment"],"skills_required":["solid-axle suspension service","track bar and control arm geometry","torque spec adherence"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2014-2018","2019-2024"],"tags":["lift","suspension","coil-spring","full-kit","track-bar"],"parts":[{"name":"4\" coil-spring lift system, Ram 2500 Power Wagon specific","vendor":"BDS Suspension","price_estimate_usd":2300},{"name":"4.5\" SST lift system, Ram 2500","vendor":"ReadyLift","price_estimate_usd":1800},{"name":"Adjustable front track bar","vendor":"Carli / Synergy / Thuren","price_estimate_usd":350},{"name":"Extended stainless brake lines, front and rear","vendor":"Crown / Goodridge","price_estimate_usd":160},{"name":"Front bump stop extensions","vendor":"various","price_estimate_usd":70}],"citations":[{"title":"BDS Suspension \u2014 Ram 2500 lift kits and install literature","url":"https://www.bds-suspension.com/ram-2500-power-wagon-lift-kits"},{"title":"ReadyLift \u2014 Ram 2500 SST series","url":"https://www.readylift.com/ram-2500"},{"title":"Ram Trucks \u2014 2500 Power Wagon specifications","url":"https://www.ramtrucks.com/ram-2500/specs.power-wagon.html"}],"alternatives":["pw-lift-mid-3in","pw-budget-lift","pw-shocks-premium"],"summary":"A 4\" lift on the Power Wagon opens up 37\" tires, meaningfully improves departure angle, and puts you in a different league for approach geometry on technical trails. The thing that determines whether the install goes well is not the springs \u2014 it is whether the kit corrects the **track bar, caster, and steering geometry** that a coil-sprung solid axle throws out of alignment the moment you raise it. Buy the kit for its geometry parts, not its lift number.","description":"Clear up a persistent piece of misinformation first, because it sends people to the wrong parts shelf: **the 2014+ Power Wagon is coil-sprung at both ends and has no air suspension and no leaf springs.**\n\n- **Front:** solid **AAM 9.25\"** axle on **coil springs**, located by a three-link with a **track bar**, damped by conventional shocks. There are no struts anywhere on this truck.\n- **Rear:** solid **AAM 11.5\"** axle on **coil springs**, located by a **five-link with its own track bar**.\n- **Rear air suspension is an option on other Ram 2500 trims \u2014 it is not offered on the Power Wagon.** If a kit's instructions talk about air bag travel range or rear leaf packs, it was written for a different truck.\n\nThat architecture is why lifting a Power Wagon is a geometry job. Raise a leaf-sprung axle and the springs still locate it. Raise a coil-sprung, track-bar-located axle and you have **shifted the axle sideways** relative to the frame, **rotated the caster** out of spec, **changed the drag link angle** into bump steer, and **moved the pinion** out of phase with the driveshaft. A kit that ships four springs and four shocks has addressed none of that.\n\nCheck the parts list against this before you buy. Anything missing you will be ordering later at retail.\n\n**Front**\n- Taller coil springs rated for the Power Wagon's nose weight (~4,600-5,000 lb on the front axle before you add a bumper)\n- **Adjustable track bar or a track bar relocation bracket** \u2014 without it the front axle sits roughly an inch off-center and the truck tracks visibly crooked\n- **Caster correction**: adjustable upper control arms, or cam bolts, targeting the factory window (typically **around 4-5 degrees positive**, verified against your model year's spec, and measured with the truck at ride height on the ground)\n- **Drop pitman arm or corrected drag link** to pull the steering linkage back parallel with the track bar \u2014 mismatched angles between the two is textbook bump steer, and on a 7,000 lb truck it feels like the front end is hunting\n- **Bump stop extensions** so the tire does not find the fender or the coil does not coil-bind before the bump stop does\n- Front shocks lengthened to suit ride height\n\n**Rear**\n- Taller coils or a spacer appropriate to the coil rate\n- **Adjustable rear track bar**, same reason as the front\n- Longer rear shocks\n- **Pinion angle check** \u2014 at 4\" the rear driveshaft operating angle grows enough to matter; if the kit does not include adjustable rear control arms, plan to measure and possibly shim\n\n**Both ends**\n- **Extended brake lines, front and rear.** This is the safety item people skip. A stock line at 4\" of lift can go taut at full droop and tear. Order stainless extended lines and route them clear of the coils.\n- **ABS harness and wiring slack** at each corner\n- **Sway bar link length** \u2014 and this is Power Wagon specific, below\n\n**The electronic sway bar disconnect.** The Power Wagon's front bar disconnects hydraulically below 18 mph in 4WD, and the actuator and its linkage have a fixed geometry. A generic Ram 2500 kit's sway bar drop bracket or extended end links **may not preserve the disconnect's engagement travel**, leaving you with a bar that will not re-latch or one that binds. Confirm the kit is validated against the disconnect system, and after install run a full connect/disconnect cycle on jack stands before you drive it. The [sway bar disconnect service guide](/db/power-wagon/sway-bar-disconnect-service/) covers what a failed re-latch looks like.\n\n**Articulink joints.** The extra pivot joints at the axle end of the front control arms are what give the Power Wagon its articulation advantage, and they are a wear item. Lifting increases the static angle they sit at. Inspect them during the install while the front end is apart \u2014 the [Articulink explainer](/db/power-wagon/articulink-front-end-explained/) has the check procedure, and doing it now costs nothing extra.\n\n**Gearing.** Factory ratio is **4.10 front and rear** on the 6.4L Hemi (410 hp / 429 lb-ft). At **35\"** the truck is acceptable on 4.10s. At **37\"** it is not \u2014 the effective ratio drops enough that the truck feels flat off the line, the 6-speed hunts on grades, and transmission temperatures climb while towing. Plan a **4.56** regear as part of the 37\" decision, not as a later fix; see [the regear guide](/db/power-wagon/pw-regear-456/) and [the mud-terrain tire guide](/db/power-wagon/pw-tires-mt/) for the full bill.\n\n**Load rating.** GVWR on a 2019+ Power Wagon is **8,510 lb** against a curb weight near **6,900 lb**. At 37\", buy tires by **load index**, not by size \u2014 a P-metric or light-duty LT casing in a 37 will be under-rated for this truck's front axle.\n\nBudget a **full weekend** and expect one seized fastener. Work one axle at a time so the truck stays located.\n\n1. **Document ride height first.** Measure fender-lip-to-hub-center at all four corners with the truck on level ground and write it down. This is your before-and-after reference and your sag check later.\n2. Park on level ground, chock, and support the **frame** on jack stands with a second jack under the axle you are working on.\n3. Remove wheels. Soak all suspension fasteners in penetrant and give it fifteen minutes.\n4. **Front:** disconnect the sway bar end links and the shocks. Support the axle, then **loosen the track bar at the frame** before unloading the coils.\n5. Lower the axle in controlled steps, watching brake lines and the ABS harness for tension. Swap the coils.\n6. Install the **track bar bracket or adjustable bar**, the **caster correction**, and the **drop pitman arm**. Leave everything finger-tight.\n7. Install extended brake lines and bleed the front circuit.\n8. **Rear:** support the axle, remove shocks, unload and swap the rear coils, install the adjustable rear track bar, then extended rear lines and bleed.\n9. Reinstall wheels, lower the truck to the ground, and **bounce it to settle the suspension** before final torque.\n10. **Torque every suspension fastener at ride height, not at full droop.** Bushings clamped at droop are pre-loaded wrong and will tear. Follow the kit's torque sheet.\n11. **Center the axles.** Measure frame-rail-to-tire on both sides at each axle and set the adjustable track bars until the axle is centered under the truck.\n12. **Cycle the sway bar disconnect** through connect and disconnect, and check for contact at full lock both directions.\n13. **Alignment.** A four-wheel alignment on an HD truck runs **$150-250**. Set caster to spec, confirm toe, and re-check after 500 miles.\n14. **Re-torque at 500 miles** and re-check ride height for settling.\n\n- **Death wobble.** A lifted Ram HD with a loose or mis-angled track bar, worn ball joints, or a tired steering damper is the classic recipe. If the truck develops a violent front-end oscillation after a bump, stop driving it and inspect the track bar ends, drag link, tie rod, and ball joints before anything else.\n- **Front sag over the first month.** Coil springs settle. Half an inch is normal; more than that means the spring rate is under-specified for your nose weight, especially if you have added a steel bumper and winch.\n- **Driveline vibration** appearing above 55 mph \u2014 measure pinion and driveshaft operating angles rather than guessing.\n- **Fuel economy.** Expect a **3-4 mpg** hit at 37\" with the added rotating mass and frontal area. That is not a kit defect.\n\n| Line item | Typical cost |\n|---|---|\n| 4\"-4.5\" Power Wagon-compatible lift system | $1,500-2,700 |\n| Adjustable front track bar (if not in kit) | $300-450 |\n| Caster correction arms (if not in kit) | $400-800 |\n| Extended brake lines, front + rear | $120-220 |\n| Bump stop extensions | $50-100 |\n| Four-wheel alignment | $150-250 |\n| Shop install (10-16 hr) | $1,200-2,200 |\n| **DIY all-in** | **$2,100-4,300** |\n| **Shop all-in** | **$3,300-6,500** |\n\nBefore buying, confirm two things in writing with the vendor: that the kit is **validated for the Power Wagon's electronic sway bar disconnect**, and that it includes or accommodates **track bar correction at both axles**. A vendor that cannot answer those has not tested on this truck. If 4\" is more than the build needs, the [3\" mid-lift](/db/power-wagon/pw-lift-mid-3in/) clears 35s with far less geometry work and is the better value for most owners.\n\nA 4\" lift on a Power Wagon is a coil-spring geometry project, not a spring swap \u2014 budget for track bars, caster correction, steering correction, and extended brake lines, and confirm the kit respects the factory sway bar disconnect. Pair it with 37s and a 4.56 regear if you actually crawl; stop at 3\" and 35s if you do not."},{"id":"pw-shocks-premium","title":"Premium Reservoir Shocks \u2014 Carli, Fox, King","vehicle":"power-wagon","category":"suspension","subcategory":"shocks","type":"part","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":5},"cost_usd":{"low":800,"high":4500},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands (3-ton minimum, rated for a 6,900 lb truck)","socket set","torque wrench","penetrating oil","pry bar","drill and step bit (some reservoir brackets)"],"skills_required":["wrenching","torquing suspension fasteners at ride height"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2005-2009","2010-2013","2014-2018","2019-2024"],"tags":["suspension","shocks","shock"],"parts":[{"name":"Fox 2.0 Performance Series smooth-body shocks (4 corners)","vendor":"Fox","price_estimate_usd":800},{"name":"Fox 2.5 Factory Series DSC remote-reservoir shocks (4 corners)","vendor":"Fox","price_estimate_usd":2100},{"name":"King 2.5 remote-reservoir shocks, rebuildable (4 corners)","vendor":"King Shocks","price_estimate_usd":2400},{"name":"Carli Backcountry / Pintop matched shock-and-coil system","vendor":"Carli Suspension","price_estimate_usd":3800},{"name":"Reservoir mounting brackets, clamps + hardware","vendor":"various","price_estimate_usd":150}],"citations":[{"title":"FN Tuned \u2014 Power Wagon suspension and shock upgrades (Carli/King)","url":"https://www.fntuned.com/blogs/apex/best-ram-2500-power-wagon-mods"},{"title":"Tread Magazine \u2014 building the ultimate 2021 Ram 2500 Power Wagon","url":"https://www.treadmagazine.com/vehicles/2500-power-wagon-the-big-pig/"},{"title":"Carli Suspension \u2014 Ram 2500 system selection and ride-height notes","url":"https://carlisuspension.com/collections/dodge-ram-2500-3500"}],"alternatives":["pw-shocks","pw-mid-lift"],"summary":"Reservoir shocks are the highest-return suspension upgrade on a loaded Power Wagon, and the cheapest way to get them wrong is to buy on brand instead of on valving. A $200-a-corner Fox 2.0 fixes the fade that ruins a long washboard run. A $500-a-corner King or a matched Carli system buys re-valvable damping tuned to your weight. What none of them buy is spring rate \u2014 if the truck sags under a bed load, the springs are the problem and a stiffer shock only makes it ride worse.","description":"Ram tunes the Power Wagon soft on purpose. It is coil-sprung at both ends \u2014 solid AAM 9.25 in front on coils located by control arms and a track bar, solid AAM 11.5 in rear on coils in a five-link with its own track bar \u2014 and there are no leaf springs and no struts anywhere on the truck. That coil setup is why it articulates well, and the soft damping is part of the same design goal.\n\nThe cost shows up when you add weight. A stock damper is a small oil column in a thin body; on a sustained washboard run it heats, the oil thins, the fluid aerates, and damping force drops off. That is fade, and you feel it as a truck that stops settling after a dip and starts pattering across the surface. Add a bumper, a winch, armor, a rack, and gear to a truck already near 6,900 lb curb and the stock shocks reach that point far sooner.\n\nA remote or piggyback reservoir addresses the cause directly: more oil, more surface area to shed heat, and a nitrogen charge separated from the working fluid so the oil resists aeration. Larger 2.0 or 2.5 in bodies add piston area, which is what lets a shock generate meaningful force on a heavy truck without going harsh at low speed.\n\n**Weight first.** Valving is a curve, not a quality level. A shock valved for an unloaded truck is underdamped once you hang 400 lb of bumper, winch, and rack on it; a shock valved for a heavy build rides stiff and busy when the truck is empty. Weigh the truck loaded the way you actually drive it \u2014 a CAT scale run costs about $15 and beats guessing.\n\n**A payload correction worth making.** The Power Wagon has the *lowest* payload in the Ram 2500 lineup, roughly **1,400-1,500 lb** depending on configuration, because the coil rear and the off-road hardware eat into it. Its tow rating, around **10,000 lb**, is likewise well under a conventional Ram 2500. Read the door-jamb sticker on your truck for the real numbers. The practical consequence: most slide-in truck campers put a Power Wagon over its rear GAWR before any suspension part is chosen, and no shock fixes an overloaded axle. If a camper is the goal, the honest answer is a different truck or a much lighter camper.\n\n**Then ride height.** A shock has a finite stroke. On a lifted truck a stock-length shock tops out in droop \u2014 the shock becomes the droop limiter, which destroys the articulation you lifted for and eventually tears a mount \u2014 or runs out of compression travel and slams into its own bump stop. Buy the shock length that matches your lift, and confirm the extended and collapsed lengths against your measured droop and bump numbers rather than trusting a \"fits 2-3 in lift\" listing.\n\n**Then springs.** On a coil truck, ride height and load capacity live in the coil. If the truck sits low with gear in it, order the matched coil with the shock. This is exactly why Carli sells systems rather than loose shocks.\n\n| Option | Rough price per corner | What you get | Best for |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Fox 2.0 Performance Series | $150-250 | Smooth-body, non-adjustable, big improvement over a faded stock damper | Mostly-stock truck, budget rebuild |\n| Fox 2.5 Factory Series with DSC | $450-600 | Remote reservoir, separate high- and low-speed compression adjusters | One truck that both tows and runs washboard |\n| King 2.5 remote reservoir | $500-700 | Rebuildable and re-valvable; send them out as your weight changes | Hard desert running, evolving build |\n| Carli matched system | $2,800-4,500 (set) | King-built hardware valved to the Ram HD chassis, coils included | Owner who wants it engineered, not assembled |\n\nThe DSC adjuster on the Fox 2.5 is the feature most owners underuse. High-speed compression controls the big square-edged hit; low-speed controls body roll and dive. Set low-speed firmer for a loaded highway run and back high-speed off for washboard, then re-check after a real trip.\n\nKing's argument is service life: a rebuildable shock gets new seals, fresh oil, and a re-shim for roughly **$150-250 per shock** every 30,000-50,000 hard miles, which over ten years is cheaper than replacing sealed units twice.\n\nShock replacement is ordinary wrenching, with two details that matter more on this truck than most.\n\n1. Park level, chock the rear wheels, and break the shock bolts loose before lifting anything.\n2. Lift by the frame and support the axle separately on stands rated for the weight \u2014 a 6,900 lb truck is not the place for a pair of 2-ton stands.\n3. Soak the upper mount studs in penetrant. Factory hardware on a salt-state truck seizes, and a snapped upper stud on the front turns an afternoon into a weekend.\n4. Swap **one corner at a time** so the axle stays located and you always have a reference for orientation.\n5. Mount remote reservoirs to clean frame or bracket locations, away from exhaust heat, and route hoses so they cannot rub on a hose clamp, the track bar, or the sway bar through full travel. Cycle the suspension by hand and look for contact before you drive.\n6. **Torque the shock bolts at ride height with the truck on its wheels,** not hanging on stands. Rubber and urethane bushings that are clamped at full droop are pre-loaded wrong for the entire life of the shock.\n7. Re-torque after the first 100 miles and check reservoir clamps after the first trail day.\n\nIf a lift is going on at the same time, the geometry work \u2014 adjustable track bars, caster correction, brake line length, and the electronic sway bar disconnect's travel envelope \u2014 belongs to the lift, not the shock. Do not let a shock install become an unplanned lift.\n\nA shock only controls a spring; it cannot fix a chassis that is out of balance elsewhere. If you have gone up in tire size and weight, read the [heavy-truck weight reality guide](/db/power-wagon/hd-3-4-ton-weight-reality/) first so you are not asking a damper to carry load the coils should. On a lifted truck, confirm shock length alongside your [mid-lift](/db/power-wagon/pw-mid-lift/) choice, or check whether a [leveling kit](/db/power-wagon/pw-lift-leveling-kit/) is all your tire target actually requires. Understand how the front geometry moves through travel via the [Articulink front-end explainer](/db/power-wagon/articulink-front-end-explained/), and if your lift changes bracket positions, verify the [electronic sway bar disconnect](/db/power-wagon/sway-bar-disconnect-service/) still re-latches. The [companion base shock guide](/db/power-wagon/pw-shocks/) covers the value tier if a full reservoir setup is more than your use warrants.\n\nOne safety note: if the truck develops a steering oscillation after a shock or lift change, that is death wobble and it is a geometry or worn-component fault \u2014 a track bar, ball joints, Articulink joints, or a loose steering box. Do not chase it with a stiffer steering stabilizer. A stabilizer masks the symptom and the underlying part keeps wearing.\n\nPremium reservoir shocks are the fix when a loaded Power Wagon outpaces its soft factory dampers on washboard and big hits. Weigh the truck, buy valving for that weight, buy length for your ride height, order the matched coil if it sags, route the reservoirs cleanly, and torque everything at ride height. Done that way it is the suspension change a heavy, built Power Wagon feels most."},{"id":"pw-shocks","title":"Aftermarket Shocks for Power Wagon (Fox 2.5 / King 2.5 / Bilstein 5160)","vehicle":"power-wagon","category":"suspension","subcategory":"shocks","type":"part","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":4,"high":8},"cost_usd":{"low":800,"high":3200},"tools_required":["floor jack rated to 3500 lb","jack stands","metric sockets (18mm, 21mm, 22mm)","torque wrench (250+ lb-ft capable)","PB Blaster"],"skills_required":["solid wrenching","spring removal experience","patience with rusty hardware"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2014-2018","2019-2024","2025+"],"tags":["shocks","suspension","premium","high-performance"],"parts":[{"name":"Bilstein 5160 Reservoir Shocks (front + rear, set of 4)","part_number":"B8-5160-PW","vendor":"Bilstein","price_estimate_usd":900},{"name":"Fox 2.5 Performance Series w/ Reservoir (front + rear)","vendor":"Fox","price_estimate_usd":1800},{"name":"King 2.5 OEM Performance Series w/ Reservoir + Compression Adjuster","vendor":"King Shocks","price_estimate_usd":2800},{"name":"Carli Performance 2.5 (matched to Carli lift kits)","vendor":"Carli Suspension","price_estimate_usd":2200}],"citations":[{"title":"Bilstein 5160 series \u2014 remote-reservoir monotube overview","url":"https://www.bilstein.com/us/en/products/5100-series/"},{"title":"Fox 2.5 Performance Series shocks \u2014 product line","url":"https://www.ridefox.com/"},{"title":"Carli Suspension \u2014 Ram 2500/Power Wagon shock tuning notes","url":"https://carlisuspension.com/"}],"alternatives":["pw-lift-aev-dualsport","pw-lift-aev-dualsport"],"summary":"The factory Bilsteins on the Power Wagon are good for stock height and light-duty trail. Step up to Bilstein 5160 reservoir shocks for $900 and you get noticeably better fade resistance at desert speeds. Step up to Fox 2.5 or King 2.5 reservoirs for $1,800-$2,800 and you change the truck \u2014 controlled, plush, no-fade over washboard at 50 mph.","description":"The factory Power Wagon Bilstein 5100-class shocks are tuned for stock 33\" tires and stock weight. They're fine for daily and mild trail use but they fade fast under desert running or sustained off-road speed. Three upgrade paths:\n\n**Bilstein 5160 reservoir shocks** ($900 for all four) \u2014 the value upgrade. Same 46mm body as the factory shock but with a remote reservoir for added oil capacity and fade resistance. Bolts in like a factory replacement. About 70% of the performance of a Fox 2.5 for 50% of the price. Best for moderate trail + occasional desert.\n\n**Fox 2.5 Performance Series with reservoir** ($1,800) \u2014 true 2.5\" body shocks, internal floating piston, remote reservoir. Big step up in damping control and ride quality, especially on washboard and rocky high-speed terrain. The mainstream \"I'm serious about this truck\" upgrade.\n\n**King 2.5 OEM Performance Series** ($2,800) \u2014 premium tier. Adjustable compression damping (24-click), serviceable internals, longer service intervals than Fox in dusty desert use. The pick if you wheel hard and want to tune your own damping curve.\n\n**Carli Performance 2.5** ($2,200) \u2014 bundled with Carli lift kits, tuned specifically for the Power Wagon's solid-axle geometry. The choice if you're running a Carli Pintop or Backcountry lift.\n\nNotes for the install: the Power Wagon's front coilover bolts are notorious for rust seizure on 2014-2018 trucks. Plan on penetrating oil 24 hours before, and have an impact wrench available. The 22mm upper nut on the front strut tower is the worst \u2014 many owners snap the stud trying to break it loose with a hand wrench.\n\nFor all four shock options, do front AND rear together. Mismatched damping (new fronts, tired rears) makes the truck feel weird and accelerates wear on the new shocks.\n\nAll four options are a bolt-in shock swap \u2014 no fabrication \u2014 but the Power Wagon's front coilover assembly makes this a real afternoon job, not a 30-minute one.\n\n1. Soak the front lower shock bolt and the 22mm upper strut nut in penetrating oil 24 hours ahead. On 2014-2018 trucks these are the seizure points; budget the extra day.\n2. Support the front lower control arm with the floor jack before unbolting anything \u2014 the coil is captive on the front strut, so the arm carries spring load. Lower it slowly after the lower bolt is out.\n3. Break the upper nut with an impact wrench, not a hand ratchet. Owners routinely snap the stud trying to muscle it loose by hand; the impact's hammering action is what frees a seized stud without shearing it.\n4. Transfer the spring to the new front strut on a compressor rated for the load, or have a shop do the spring swap \u2014 a captive 2,000+ lb coil is the one part of this job that earns professional respect.\n5. Rear shocks are far less stubborn: two bolts each, 21mm. Torque the lower to factory spec and re-check the uppers after a shakedown drive.\n6. Do front AND rear in the same session. Mixing new fronts with tired rears makes the truck feel disconnected and accelerates wear on the new units.\n\nFor a step up to a taller ride height, the shock change pairs with a lift kit \u2014 see the [[pw-lift-aev-dualsport]] and Carli paths, which spec their own valving.\n\nThe biggest mistake is ordering reservoir shocks without checking frame clearance on an already-lifted truck \u2014 Fox and King 2.5 remote reservoirs need a clear routing path, and a 3\"+ lift changes where the hose lands. Mock up the reservoir bracket before committing.\n\nThe second is valving mismatch. A non-Carli shock dropped onto a Carli or AEV spring without the matching tune will ride harsher or wallow depending on the pairing; follow the shock maker's preload and valving recommendation for your exact spring, or expect to chase ride quality afterward. And if a front coilover bolt does seize past the point of penetrating oil, stop before you snap it \u2014 heat from a small torch on the bracket (not the stud) usually wins where brute force loses.","pros":["Bilstein 5160 is a true bolt-in upgrade with a meaningful improvement","Fox 2.5 / King 2.5 change the character of the truck for desert and washboard","All four options are rebuildable (Fox/King especially \u2014 long-term value)","Required for any 3\"+ lift kit (factory shocks aren't long enough)"],"cons":["Rusty hardware on 4th-gen trucks is the install bottleneck","King 2.5 at $2,800 is premium pricing","All non-Carli options need Carli's tuning recommendations to match a specific lift; otherwise expect to adjust spring preload","Fox/King reservoirs need clearance on the frame \u2014 check fitment on lifted trucks before ordering"]},{"id":"sway-bar-disconnect-service","title":"Power Wagon Electronic Sway Bar Disconnect: Maintenance, Failure Modes, and Manual Override","vehicle":"power-wagon","category":"suspension","subcategory":"sway_bar","type":"diagnostic","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":1.0,"high":4.0},"cost_usd":{"low":30,"high":900},"tools_required":["socket set","torque wrench","floor jack","jack stands","multimeter","Ram-capable bidirectional scan tool","flashlight","shop rags"],"skills_required":["basic suspension service","basic electrical diagnosis","reading a factory service manual"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2005-2009","2010-2018","2019-present"],"tags":["sway-bar","disconnect","articulink","hydraulic-actuator","suspension"],"parts":[{"name":"Sway bar disconnect actuator seal kit (where serviceable by year)","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":65},{"name":"Sway bar disconnect actuator assembly","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":480},{"name":"Front sway bar end link","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":45},{"name":"Sway bar frame bushing set","vendor":"Mopar / Energy Suspension","price_estimate_usd":40}],"citations":[{"title":"Ram Power Wagon Smart Bar service \u2014 Ram factory service information","url":"https://www.mopar.com/en-us/care/service-records.html"},{"title":"Ram Trucks \u2014 2500 Power Wagon features and specifications","url":"https://www.ramtrucks.com/ram-2500/specs.power-wagon.html"}],"alternatives":["articulink-front-end-explained","pw-lift-coilover-swap"],"summary":"The Power Wagon's electronic front sway bar disconnect \u2014 Ram's \"Smart Bar\" \u2014 is one of the two features that genuinely separate this truck from lesser off-road packages, the other being the factory lockers. It works well when maintained and fails in a small number of predictable ways. **The failure that matters for safety is the bar stuck disconnected**: the truck loses meaningful roll resistance and gets vague at highway speed. Diagnose the direction of the failure first, because it determines whether you can drive home.","description":"The Smart Bar releases the front sway bar when you select the function in **4WD below 18 mph**, freeing the front suspension to articulate fully \u2014 with the bar connected, it limits droop on the unloaded side and pulls that tire off the ground. Above **18 mph** the system reconnects automatically, restoring highway roll stability without the driver having to remember.\n\nMechanically it is an **electro-hydraulic actuator** at the sway bar: an electric pump builds pressure, and that pressure moves a piston that withdraws the pin coupling the bar to the link. The control logic reads vehicle speed and 4WD status and will not disconnect outside its window, which is why \"it will not disconnect\" is often a driver-input problem rather than a hardware fault.\n\nTwo dependencies worth understanding:\n\n- **The Articulink joints carry the load once the bar is out.** A working disconnect that reveals a worn front end shows up as clunking and vague steering on trail; check the [Articulink inspection procedure](/db/power-wagon/articulink-front-end-explained/) before assuming the sway bar system is at fault.\n- **Lift kits can break the disconnect.** A generic Ram 2500 sway bar drop bracket or extended end link may put the actuator outside its working travel, leaving a bar that will not re-latch. If symptoms started right after a suspension install, that is the first suspect \u2014 see the [4\" mid-lift guide](/db/power-wagon/pw-mid-lift/) for what a Power Wagon-validated kit must include.\n\n**Fluid caution:** the actuator uses a specific hydraulic fluid specification. Whether the system on your model year has a serviceable reservoir at all varies \u2014 some are effectively sealed units where visible seepage means replacement rather than a top-up. Check your year's factory service information before adding anything. **Putting the wrong fluid into this system destroys the actuator seals**, converting a $65 repair into a $480 one.\n\n| What the truck does | Likely cause | Can you drive it? |\n|---|---|---|\n| Bar will not disconnect; indicator does not change | Pump fuse, pump failure, actuator seized connected, or conditions not met (not in 4WD, above 18 mph) | Yes \u2014 you lose articulation only |\n| Bar will not reconnect; indicator stays disconnected | Low pressure from a leak, or piston seized retracted | **No, not at highway speed** \u2014 roll resistance is significantly reduced |\n| Disconnects and reconnects, but slowly, or indicator flashes | Low fluid, partial pump failure, or a slow seal leak | Cautiously \u2014 fix soon, it is trending toward stuck |\n| Warning light with no obvious mechanical symptom | Sensor, wiring, or control fault | Yes \u2014 scan it before touching hardware |\n| Clunk from the front with the bar connected | Sway bar frame bushings or end links, not the actuator | Yes \u2014 a bushing job, not an actuator job |\n\n**Failure 1 \u2014 stuck connected.** You lose the articulation benefit; the truck is safe on road. Check the **pump fuse first**, then **listen for the pump** when you request a disconnect \u2014 it should audibly cycle for a second or two. Silence points at fuse, relay, wiring, or pump. Sound with no movement points at the actuator.\n\n**Failure 2 \u2014 stuck disconnected.** This is the one to take seriously. Look for **external fluid seepage at the actuator piston seal** with a flashlight, and inspect the lines and fittings. A leak that has depleted the system cannot generate the pressure to re-latch.\n\n**Failure 3 \u2014 slow or incomplete operation.** Usually a slow leak that has not fully depleted the system yet, or a pump losing output. It gets worse, not better.\n\n**Failure 4 \u2014 light only.** Scan it. The system has a dedicated fault set, and reading it takes a **Ram-capable bidirectional scan tool** \u2014 a generic code reader will report a generic chassis fault or nothing at all. The [OBD2 diagnostics guide](/db/power-wagon/pw-obd2-diagnostics/) covers what tool actually talks to this truck.\n\nIf the actuator fails on the trail with the bar disconnected, the bar can be **manually locked back into the connected position** using the coupling pin the actuator normally moves. The hardware changed between early and later builds, so **look up the override procedure for your model year in the factory service information before you need it** \u2014 print it and put it in the glovebox with your recovery gear. Trying to reason it out under a truck at dusk is how people give up and drive home unsafely.\n\nDriving with the bar permanently disconnected is a get-off-the-trail measure, not a plan. On a truck weighing near **6,900 lb** with a high center of gravity, the reduction in roll resistance is real. Keep speeds low, avoid quick lane changes, and get it repaired before regular highway use.\n\n- **Inspect the actuator and its lines at every other oil change** \u2014 a flashlight and thirty seconds. You are looking for weeping fluid and chafed lines. Fold it into the routine in the [fluids and service guide](/db/power-wagon/pw-fluids-service/).\n- **Replace a cracked boot or seal at the first sign of seepage.** The seal kit is inexpensive where your year supports it; the full actuator assembly is not.\n- **Grease the sway bar frame bushings and pivot points** and replace them when they crack. Worn bushings let the bar move where it should not, which side-loads the actuator \u2014 this is the cheapest thing on the list and the one most often skipped.\n- **Cycle the system deliberately once a month**, even in winter. A mechanism that never moves seizes faster than one that does.\n- **After any deep water crossing**, inspect for intrusion at the actuator seal.\n- **After any suspension work**, cycle the disconnect through a full connect/disconnect on jack stands before driving.\n\n1. **Confirm conditions were met** \u2014 4WD engaged, below 18 mph. Rule out driver input before pulling tools out.\n2. **Note the failure direction** \u2014 will not disconnect, or will not reconnect. The table above splits the work from here.\n3. **Check the pump fuse** and listen for pump operation during an engagement attempt.\n4. **Inspect for external seepage** at the actuator, lines, and fittings with the truck on jack stands.\n5. **Scan for stored codes** with a Ram-capable bidirectional tool; use it to command the actuator if the tool supports it, which separates control-side faults from hardware faults immediately.\n6. **Repair**: seeping actuator gets the seal kit where serviceable; a dead pump gets the pump; a seized actuator gets the assembly.\n7. **Test through a full connect and disconnect cycle**, then a road test above 18 mph to confirm auto-reconnect, before returning to trail use.\n\n| Job | Typical cost |\n|---|---|\n| Sway bar frame bushings + end links (DIY) | $60-130 |\n| Actuator seal kit (parts, where serviceable) | $50-90 |\n| Actuator assembly (part) | $420-560 |\n| Pump assembly (part) | $300-500 |\n| Shop diagnosis | $120-200 |\n| Shop labor, actuator replacement | $200-400 |\n| **Typical DIY seal repair** | **$50-90** |\n| **Typical shop actuator replacement** | **$650-950** |","pros":["Fully automatic once conditions are met \u2014 no getting out to pull pins","Manual override exists for field failures","Seal kit allows actuator service without the full assembly on years that support it","Pairs with the Articulink front end to deliver articulation a bar-equipped truck cannot match"],"cons":["Hydraulic complexity that manual disconnects on other trucks do not have","Specific fluid requirement \u2014 the wrong fluid destroys the seals","Stuck-disconnected is a genuine highway safety problem, not an inconvenience","Diagnosis wants a Ram-capable scan tool and some electrical comfort","Aftermarket lift kits not validated for this system can break the re-latch"]},{"id":"pw-obd2-diagnostics","title":"OBD-II Diagnostics and Common Codes \u2014 6.4L Hemi Power Wagon","vehicle":"power-wagon","category":"tools_diagnostic","subcategory":"obd2","type":"diagnostic","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0.25,"high":2.0},"cost_usd":{"low":20,"high":400},"tools_required":["OBD-II scan tool (bidirectional preferred)","smartphone + Bluetooth OBD dongle (budget option)","12V test light or multimeter","factory service information or wiring reference","3/8 in ratchet + spark plug socket (misfire follow-up)"],"skills_required":["reading and interpreting trouble codes","basic electrical sense"],"build_tiers":["stock","mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["2014-2018","2019-2024","2025+"],"tags":["diagnostics","obd2","check-engine","misfire","troubleshooting","security-gateway"],"parts":[],"citations":[{"title":"DodgeGarage \u2014 Hemi ignition / misfire diagnosis (Sparks and Recreation)","url":"https://www.dodgegarage.com/news/article/how-to/2019/03/sparks-and-recreation"},{"title":"RamForum \u2014 common 6.4L trouble-code threads (P0300 / EVAP)","url":"https://www.ramforum.com/"},{"title":"AutoAuth \u2014 FCA Security Gateway (SGW) access registration","url":"https://www.autoauth.com/"}],"alternatives":["pw-spark-plugs","pw-fuel-pump"],"summary":"Buy for what you actually do. A **$20-30 Bluetooth dongle** reads and clears powertrain codes and shows basic live data \u2014 enough for a stock daily driver. A **$150-250 bidirectional scanner** adds ABS, airbag, and body-module coverage plus active tests, which is what earns its money on a truck you take past cell service. Before you buy anything for a 2018-or-newer truck, understand the **Security Gateway** below, because it decides whether your tool can command anything or only read. The codes you will actually see are misfires (P0300 plus a cylinder number), catalyst efficiency (P0420/P0430), and EVAP leaks (P0455/P0456). Read the freeze frame, fix the root cause, re-scan \u2014 clearing a code without fixing the cause resets the clock, nothing else.","description":"Starting with the **2018 model year**, FCA began fitting a **Security Gateway Module (SGW)** to Ram trucks. It sits between the OBD-II port and the vehicle's internal buses. Reading codes and live data still works normally through it. What the SGW blocks is **writes**: bidirectional commands, active tests, module programming, key programming, and some ABS bleed routines from tools that have not authenticated.\n\nThree ways around it, in order of how sane they are:\n\n- **Register the tool with AutoAuth.** This is the manufacturer-sanctioned route; a subscription runs around **$50 for a multi-year term** \u2014 verify current pricing and whether your specific scan tool supports AutoAuth before you rely on it. Many mainstream aftermarket scanners do.\n- **Buy a tool with SGW access built in.** Several current Autel, Launch, and Topdon units advertise FCA SGW support. Confirm it for your exact model number, not the product line.\n- **An SGW bypass harness.** These unplug the gateway and jumper the buses. They work and they are common, but you are removing a security device from a truck you drive on public roads. That is a real tradeoff, not a free one.\n\nIf a tool you own suddenly cannot run an active test that worked on an older vehicle, the SGW is almost always why.\n\n**P0300, P0301-P0308** \u2014 random or cylinder-specific misfire. On the 6.4L the order of suspicion is worn spark plugs first (**16 of them, two per cylinder**), then a failing coil-on-plug, then injector or compression problems. The classic confirmation: swap the suspect coil with a neighboring cylinder's, clear the code, and drive. If the misfire follows the coil, you found it. If it stays with the cylinder, look at the plug, injector, then compression.\n\n**P0420 / P0430** \u2014 catalyst efficiency below threshold. Frequently an aging downstream O2 sensor rather than a dead converter. Test before you spend converter money; a scan tool that graphs upstream versus downstream O2 activity settles it in a few minutes.\n\n**P0455 / P0456 / P0457** \u2014 EVAP leak, large to small. Start with the fuel cap and its seal, then the purge and vent solenoids. P0457 explicitly points at the cap or a loose fuel-fill component.\n\n**P0344 / P0339** \u2014 cam or crank position sensor signal. Often intermittent, often heat-related, and a common cause of a no-restart after the truck sits hot.\n\n**U0100-family codes** \u2014 module communication loss. Before condemning a module, check battery voltage and the chassis grounds. A weak battery or a corroded ground causes more U-codes than failed modules do, and on an accessorized truck a poorly grounded aftermarket circuit is a frequent culprit.\n\nCodes referencing cylinder deactivation on the trucks that have it generally trace back to oil pressure, lifters, or solenoids rather than to the electronics.\n\n1. Plug into the OBD-II port under the driver's-side dash, **key on, engine off**.\n2. Pull **stored, pending, and permanent** codes \u2014 pending codes catch a fault that has set once but not yet twice, which is your early warning.\n3. Pull the **freeze-frame data** for each code. It captures RPM, engine load, coolant temp, vehicle speed, and fuel trims at the moment the fault set. That context is half the diagnosis and most people skip it.\n4. Note **long-term fuel trims** at idle and at cruise. Trims pushed well positive point at unmetered air or low fuel delivery; well negative points at a leaking injector or high fuel pressure.\n5. **Write everything down before clearing anything.** Clearing wipes the freeze frame with it.\n6. Check **readiness monitors** \u2014 if they are all incomplete, someone cleared codes recently, which matters if you are buying the truck.\n\nAddress the root cause, clear the codes, then drive the truck through several **drive cycles** so the readiness monitors re-run. A cold start, a period of steady cruise, and some stop-and-go over a few days is usually enough; EVAP monitors are the fussiest and often need a specific fuel level, roughly a quarter to three-quarters of a tank, plus a cold soak.\n\nA code that returns immediately means the fault is still live. A code that stays gone through a full cycle with monitors reporting complete is fixed. For misfire codes, move to the [spark plug and coil guide](/db/power-wagon/pw-spark-plugs/); for fuel delivery and pressure codes, the [fuel pump guide](/db/power-wagon/pw-fuel-pump/) covers the pressure test.\n\nCodes tell you what already broke. Live data warns you before it does. On a loaded climb or a long low-range crawl, keep these on screen:\n\n| PID | Watch for | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| Transmission temperature | Sustained above ~220\u00b0F; back off well before ~250\u00b0F | The 66RFE six-speed on 2014-2018 trucks and the eight-speed on later 6.4L trucks both cook fluid under low-range load \u2014 confirm which you have by year |\n| Coolant temperature | Creep above the normal operating band | Catches a lazy fan clutch or a fouled radiator early |\n| Short/long-term fuel trims | Drift positive at idle | Vacuum or intake leak |\n| Battery / charging voltage | Sag under winch or light load | Catches a failing alternator on a winch-heavy day |\n| Misfire counters per cylinder | One cylinder climbing | Lets you back off before it sets a hard code miles from pavement |\n\nCross-check coolant behavior against the baseline in the [cooling system guide](/db/power-wagon/pw-radiator-service/), and if you are running a winch and accessories, the [alternator guide](/db/power-wagon/pw-alternator/) covers what the charging system can actually support.\n\n| Tier | Cost | What it does | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| ELM327 Bluetooth dongle + app | $20-30 | Read/clear powertrain codes, basic live data | Avoid the cheapest clones \u2014 they drop connection and misread Chrysler-specific PIDs |\n| OBDLink MX+ / BlueDriver | $100-140 | Reliable link, enhanced FCA codes, good logging | The sweet spot for an owner who is not doing module work |\n| Autel MK808-class / Launch CRP-class | $150-300 | All modules, active tests, service resets | Confirm FCA **SGW support** for your exact model on 2018+ trucks |\n| Shop-grade bidirectional | $400+ | Full bidirectional, programming | Only worth it if you are working on several vehicles |\n\nBuy the tier above the dongle if you go off pavement. Watching trans temp climb once, in time to stop and let it cool, pays for the tool.","pros":["Turns a warning light into a specific, short list of likely causes","A cheap dongle covers most daily needs; bidirectional tools add real trail value","Freeze-frame and fuel-trim data expose problems a bare code hides","Live-data logging catches heat and charging problems before they strand you"],"cons":["Clearing codes without fixing the cause hides the problem rather than solving it","2018+ trucks require SGW-capable or AutoAuth-registered tools for any bidirectional work","Budget readers miss ABS and airbag modules and the live data you want off-road","Generic code definitions still need Hemi-specific knowledge to act on"]},{"id":"pw-beadlock-wheels","title":"Beadlock-Capable Wheels for Low-Pressure Traction","vehicle":"power-wagon","category":"wheels_tires","subcategory":"wheels","type":"part","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":5},"cost_usd":{"low":1400,"high":4500},"tools_required":["torque wrench (in-lb and ft-lb)","tire machine or a shop that will mount beadlocks","bead-seal supplies","wire brush","medium-strength thread locker","valve stem tool"],"skills_required":["wrenching","torque-sequence discipline"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["2005-2009","2010-2013","2014-2018","2019-2024"],"tags":["wheels","beadlock","wheels_tires"],"parts":[{"name":"True beadlock wheels, 17 in, 8x6.5 bolt pattern (set of 4)","vendor":"Method Race Wheels / Hutchinson","price_estimate_usd":2200},{"name":"Beadlock-capable wheels, 17 in (set of 5)","vendor":"KMC / Fuel","price_estimate_usd":1400},{"name":"Beadlock ring hardware + medium-strength thread locker","vendor":"various","price_estimate_usd":120},{"name":"Onboard air or portable compressor (required if you air down)","vendor":"ARB / VIAIR","price_estimate_usd":350}],"citations":[{"title":"FN Tuned \u2014 Power Wagon wheels and tires","url":"https://www.fntuned.com/blogs/apex/best-ram-2500-power-wagon-mods"},{"title":"Tread Magazine \u2014 2021 Power Wagon build (wheels & tires)","url":"https://www.treadmagazine.com/vehicles/2500-power-wagon-the-big-pig/"},{"title":"Method Race Wheels \u2014 beadlock ring torque and service instructions","url":"https://methodracewheels.com/pages/beadlock-instructions"}],"alternatives":["pw-wheels","pw-tires-mt","power-wagon-tire-fitment"],"summary":"Buy true beadlocks only if you actually air down hard for rock. On a truck this heavy the payoff is real \u2014 clamped beads let you run pressures a sealed wheel cannot hold \u2014 but you are also signing up for a recurring ring-bolt re-torque, a wheel load rating that must clear the Power Wagon's front axle weight, and a wheel that no state has DOT-approved. If you run dirt roads, washboard, and moderate trail, the standard wheel in the companion guide is the better truck.","description":"Airing down lengthens the contact patch and lets the tire drape over rock. It is the single largest free traction gain available, and it matters more on a Power Wagon than on a Jeep because there is more mass to plant. The problem is that the same mass loads the sidewall hard, and at the pressures that help most on rock \u2014 call it under 12 psi \u2014 the bead can walk off the drop center of a standard wheel under lateral load. When that happens you are changing a tire on a side hill, or riding a flat out.\n\nA true beadlock solves this mechanically: an outer ring bolts through the tire's outer bead and clamps it to the wheel flange. The bead cannot move because it is pinched, not held by air pressure.\n\n**True beadlock.** External clamping ring, typically 16-32 bolts per wheel. This is the real article. It is not DOT-approved, and no manufacturer claims it is; enforcement varies by state and is usually a non-issue in practice, but \"not DOT-approved\" is the honest status and it can matter for insurance after an at-fault crash. Check your state before you buy.\n\n**Beadlock-capable (simulated).** Looks like a beadlock, has a decorative outer ring on a sealed wheel, and is machined to accept a real clamping ring later. It does not clamp the bead as sold. This is the right answer for most Power Wagon owners: the look, DOT compliance, no re-torque schedule, and a genuine upgrade path if your driving changes.\n\n**Double beadlock.** Clamps inner and outer beads. Hutchinson's Rock Monster is the military-derived version. Real capability, real money, real weight.\n\nThis is the step that separates a safe beadlock purchase from a dangerous one. Half-ton and Jeep-market beadlocks are common, cheap, and rated nowhere near a 3/4-ton's axle loads.\n\nThe math is short. The 2014+ Power Wagon carries a front **GAWR of 6,000 lb**, so each front wheel must be rated for at least **3,000 lb**. Rear GAWR is higher, but the front is the binding constraint because of the engine and winch. Verify the truck's own numbers on the door-jamb sticker rather than trusting a spec page.\n\nThen check three things on any wheel you are considering:\n\n- **Load rating stamped or published at 3,000 lb or above per wheel.** If the manufacturer will not publish it, walk away.\n- **Bolt pattern 8x6.5 in (8x165.1 mm)** for the Ram 2500 \u2014 confirm against your year.\n- **Center bore** large enough to sit hub-centric on the Ram HD hub, or a proper hub-centric ring supplied with the wheel. Lug-centric mounting on a truck this heavy invites vibration and stud fatigue.\n\nBeadlocks are also heavy. A stock 17 in Power Wagon alloy runs in the low 30 lb range; a steel-ring true beadlock in the same size is commonly **40-55 lb**. Four wheels at +15 lb each is 60 lb of unsprung, rotating mass on top of whatever the tire already added \u2014 one more reason the shock and gearing conversations in the linked guides stop being optional.\n\n| Wheel | Type | Rough price each | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| KMC / Fuel beadlock-capable | Simulated | $250-350 | DOT-legal, no maintenance, accepts a real ring later |\n| Method Race Wheels 105 Beadlock | True single | $350-450 | The common HD pick; Method publishes load ratings and torque specs |\n| Hutchinson Rock Monster | Double beadlock | $600-900 | Military-derived, heaviest, genuinely HD-rated |\n\nBudget a full set at **$1,400-3,000** for capable wheels or a single-ring true beadlock, and **$2,600-4,500** for double beadlocks, including ring bolts and thread locker. Add **$350** for air if you do not already carry it \u2014 a beadlock with no compressor is an expensive wheel you will never use at low pressure.\n\nOne practical cost people forget: many tire shops will not mount a true beadlock, because the ring install is outside their liability and their machines are set up for sealed wheels. Expect to mount them yourself or find a specific off-road shop. Budget an afternoon and a torque wrench you trust.\n\n1. Clean the wheel's bead seat and the ring mating surface with a wire brush \u2014 paint chips and grit under the ring are the usual cause of a slow leak.\n2. Lay the tire over the wheel and seat the outer bead against the flange by hand. No lubricant on the clamped side; lube lets the bead creep under the ring.\n3. Set the ring on and start every bolt by hand before torquing any of them. A cross-threaded ring bolt in an aluminum wheel is a ruined wheel.\n4. Torque in a **star pattern in three passes** \u2014 roughly a third, then two thirds, then full spec. Most single-ring beadlocks land near **20 ft-lb**; use the manufacturer's number, not this one.\n5. Air up, soap the ring, and check for bubbles all the way around before the wheel goes on the truck.\n6. **Re-torque after the first 50-100 miles**, then on a schedule \u2014 before every trip is the discipline that actually holds. A backed-out ring bolt is how a clamped bead fails anyway.\n7. Inspect ring bolts for stretch or corrosion annually and replace the set rather than one bolt.\n\nTreat the re-torque as non-negotiable. This is the maintenance cost of the capability, and it is the reason a beadlock-capable wheel is the right answer for anyone who will not do it.\n\nBackspacing and offset decide whether a big tire clears the steering knuckle, the track bar, and the factory flares. Match them to your tire size using the [Power Wagon tire fitment guide](/db/power-wagon/power-wagon-tire-fitment/) rather than guessing, and cross-check the tire against the [mud-terrain tire guide](/db/power-wagon/pw-tires-mt/). If you were considering spacers to correct backspacing instead, read the [wheel spacer guide](/db/power-wagon/pw-wheel-spacers/) first \u2014 on a 3/4-ton the correct-offset wheel is the better engineering answer. The [standard wheel guide](/db/power-wagon/pw-wheels/) covers the non-beadlock options that suit most owners, and the [heavy-truck weight reality guide](/db/power-wagon/hd-3-4-ton-weight-reality/) explains why every pound you add at the wheel costs more here than it does on a light rig.\n\nPressures, for a truck around 6,900 lb: roughly **28-35 psi** on pavement per the door-jamb placard for your tire size, **15-20 psi** for washboard and dirt roads, **10-14 psi** for technical dirt and sand, and **single digits on rock** once the beads are clamped. Reinflate before pavement, every time \u2014 low pressure at highway speed builds sidewall heat and is how a tire comes apart.\n\nTrue beadlocks earn their money on rock, on a heavy truck, for an owner who will re-torque the rings. Confirm a per-wheel rating of at least 3,000 lb against the front GAWR, accept the weight and the legality caveat, and carry air. For everyone else \u2014 and that is most Power Wagons \u2014 a beadlock-capable wheel gives the look and the upgrade path without the schedule."},{"id":"power-wagon-tire-fitment","title":"What Tires Fit a Power Wagon \u2014 and What 'Fits' Really Means on a 3/4-Ton Truck","vehicle":"power-wagon","category":"wheels_tires","subcategory":"tire_fitment","type":"reference","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":1.0,"high":4.0},"cost_usd":{"low":1200,"high":3500},"tools_required":["torque wrench","floor jack","jack stands"],"skills_required":["basic wheel/tire mounting knowledge"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2005-2009","2010-2018","2019-present"],"tags":["tires","fitment","35s","37s","rubbing","wheel-offset","goodyear"],"parts":[{"name":"Goodyear Wrangler DuraTrac 285/70R17 (factory replacement)","vendor":"Goodyear","price_estimate_usd":280},{"name":"BFGoodrich All-Terrain T/A KO2 285/70R17","vendor":"BFGoodrich","price_estimate_usd":310},{"name":"Nitto Ridge Grappler 285/70R17","vendor":"Nitto","price_estimate_usd":320},{"name":"Toyo Open Country AT III 305/70R18 (35\" equivalent)","vendor":"Toyo Tires","price_estimate_usd":340}],"citations":[{"title":"Ram Power Wagon specifications \u2014 wheel and tire","url":"https://www.ramtrucks.com/power-wagon.html"},{"title":"Ram Truck Forum \u2014 tire fitment thread","url":"https://www.ramtruckforum.com"}],"alternatives":["pw-tires-at","pw-tires-mt"],"summary":"The Power Wagon leaves the factory on 285/70R17 Goodyear DuraTrac tires \u2014 equivalent to a 33-inch tire. True 35s fit with minimal or no modification. Anything larger than 35s requires real work, and the 7,000-pound curb weight limits how much tire capability you actually need.","description":"The factory 285/70R17 DuraTrac is a genuine all-terrain tire, not a street tire with aggressive looks. Goodyear's DuraTrac is a capable choice for the factory setup, and many owners never swap it. If you need more grip or a specific sidewall pattern, there's room to go bigger \u2014 but understand what you're working with before spending money.\n\n285/70R17 is approximately 32.7 inches in diameter, typically described as \"33 inch\" in round numbers. The factory wheel is a 17x8 on the 8x165.1 pattern with a deep positive offset near +45 mm. The wheel well has been designed around this size, so fitment is clean with no rubbing.\n\nThis is the first common upgrade. True 35-inch tires on the factory 17\" wheel require attention to offset \u2014 the factory wheel's offset positions the tire so a 305/70R17 or 315/70R17 will typically rub the front sway bar link (when the bar is connected) and the inner fender at full lock.\n\nTypical fixes for a 35-inch fitment without a lift:\n- Cycle the sway bar disconnect \u2014 the rubbing is largely at the link, not at the fender\n- Trim the front inner fender liner (plastic, not metal \u2014 reversible)\n- Use a slightly narrower 305/70R17 rather than a wider 315/70R17\n\nWith a 2-inch level or leveling spacer, 35s fit cleanly without trimming. This is the path most Power Wagon owners take \u2014 it's inexpensive and keeps Articulink geometry intact.\n\n**On 17\" wheels:** Most common. Plenty of tire selection at 285/70R17, 305/70R17, 315/70R17.\n**On 18\" wheels (2019+ trucks with the optional 18\" wheel package):** Sizing shifts to 305/70R18 territory. The diameter equivalent stays the same, the fitment conversation is similar.\n\n37-inch tires are possible on a Power Wagon. They are not plug-and-play. The challenges:\n\n**Weight:** The Power Wagon weighs approximately 7,000 lb at the curb. Rotating mass matters at that weight \u2014 37s are heavier tires, and the 2014-and-later truck's 4.10 axle ratio (standard behind the 6.4L HEMI; 2005-2013 trucks ran 4.56 behind the 5.7L) is already on the conservative side for 35s. With 37s and stock gears, expect noticeable performance loss and fuel economy drop.\n\n**Articulink geometry:** Most 3-inch and taller lift kits for the Power Wagon modify or remove Articulink. Without Articulink, the truck is still a solid-axle truck \u2014 capable, but you've bought something different from what you paid for. Carli Suspension makes a kit that preserves Articulink geometry at lift; it is the correct engineering choice and it is expensive ($3,500\u2013$5,500 for the suspension system alone).\n\n**Fender clearance:** 37s on a stock body require significant trimming or flares. The factory fender openings are sized for 33s with clearance. Getting 37s to clear at full articulation \u2014 including through Articulink range \u2014 requires either body work or accepting contact marks on the inner liner.\n\n**Regearing:** To properly support 37s on a 2014+ truck, a regear from 4.10 to 4.56 is the standard answer \u2014 see the [regear guide](/db/power-wagon/pw-regear-456/) for the ratio math. The Power Wagon runs an AAM 9.25-inch front and an AAM 11.5-inch rear (AAM 10.5-inch rear on 2005-2013 trucks), not Dana axles, and setting up an AAM carrier calls for specific procedures and careful bearing preload. This is not a casual weekend job.\n\nFor most Power Wagon use \u2014 trail runs, overlanding, occasional rock work \u2014 35s on a leveled truck are the right answer. They fit without compromising Articulink, they don't require regearing, and the truck's factory capability is designed around roughly that size. Jumping to 37s means spending $3,000\u2013$6,000+ in suspension and potentially regearing to match, for a truck that weighs 3.5 tons. That money buys a lot of other capability improvements first.\n\n1. Choose tire size: 305/70R17 or 315/70R17 for 17\" wheels\n2. Confirm wheel offset \u2014 stock offset works with 305/70R17; 315/70R17 may need a slight spacer or trim\n3. Check inner fender liner at full lock before final installation\n4. Torque lug nuts to 130 ft-lb (check current factory spec for your year) in a star pattern\n5. Recheck torque at 25 miles and 100 miles after installation","pros":["35s fit with minimal modification on a leveled truck","Factory DuraTrac is a legitimately capable tire \u2014 don't replace it without a reason","17\" wheel sizing has the broadest tire selection"],"cons":["37s require lifting, regearing, and accepting Articulink compromise or spending for a Carli system","Curb weight limits the traction payoff from going larger \u2014 the truck is not light","Regearing a 3/4-ton AAM axle is not a quick job"]},{"id":"pw-tires-at","title":"All-Terrain Tires for Power Wagon (BFG KO2 / Falken AT3W / Toyo AT3)","vehicle":"power-wagon","category":"wheels_tires","subcategory":"tires_at","type":"part","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":2},"cost_usd":{"low":1400,"high":2400},"tools_required":["torque wrench (140 lb-ft for the M14x1.5 lugs)","tire-pressure gauge"],"skills_required":["none \u2014 mount/balance at a shop"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2014-2018","2019-2024","2025+"],"tags":["tires","all-terrain","at","overland","tire-fitment","premium"],"parts":[{"name":"BFGoodrich All-Terrain T/A KO2 LT285/70R17 (33\")","part_number":"BFG-KO2-285-70-17","vendor":"BFGoodrich","price_estimate_usd":380},{"name":"Falken Wildpeak A/T3W LT285/70R17 (33\")","vendor":"Falken","price_estimate_usd":290},{"name":"Toyo Open Country A/T III LT295/70R17 (34.3\")","vendor":"Toyo","price_estimate_usd":340},{"name":"Nitto Recon Grappler A/T LT285/70R17","vendor":"Nitto","price_estimate_usd":350}],"citations":[{"title":"BFGoodrich All-Terrain T/A KO2 \u2014 LT-metric specs","url":"https://www.bfgoodrichtires.com/"},{"title":"Falken Wildpeak A/T3W \u2014 product specs","url":"https://www.falkentire.com/"},{"title":"Toyo Open Country A/T III \u2014 product specs","url":"https://www.toyotires.com/"}],"alternatives":["pw-tires-mt","pw-wheel-spacers"],"summary":"Factory 33s on the Power Wagon are LT265/70R17 (Goodyear Wrangler DuraTrac). When they wear out, the upgrade most owners actually want is a 33\"\u201334\" all-terrain \u2014 quieter on highway, better wet/snow than the DuraTrac, and no need to re-gear. KO2 is the reference, Falken AT3W is the value pick, Toyo AT3 is the premium choice.","description":"The Power Wagon comes from the factory with a Goodyear Wrangler DuraTrac in LT265/70R17 \u2014 a hybrid AT/MT that's surprisingly capable but loud on highway and short-lived (40k miles is typical). When you're shopping replacements, the four mainstream AT options that actually fit factory wheels and clear the fenders with no rub are: BFG KO2, Falken Wildpeak AT3W, Toyo Open Country AT3, and Nitto Recon Grappler.\n\n**BFG KO2 (LT285/70R17 = 32.7\")** \u2014 the AT reference standard. Aggressive tread for an AT, excellent snow rating (3PMSF), 50k-mile typical life on a Power Wagon. Loudest of the four on highway above 65 mph. About $380/tire = $1,520 for a set of four.\n\n**Falken Wildpeak AT3W (LT285/70R17 = 32.7\")** \u2014 same size, ~25% cheaper, slightly less aggressive shoulder lugs, comparable highway noise, 60k+ life. The value choice \u2014 this is what most Power Wagon owners on the forums end up with.\n\n**Toyo Open Country AT III (LT295/70R17 = 34.3\")** \u2014 biggest of the four, premium tier. The slightly bigger 34.3\" diameter is the largest you can fit on factory fenders + factory wheels without trimming. Quieter than KO2, similar life, 30% more expensive. The choice if you want max diameter without going to 35s (which requires fender trimming and 4.56 re-gear).\n\n**Nitto Recon Grappler A/T** \u2014 newer entry, slightly more aggressive shoulder than KO2, decent snow, similar price to KO2. Smaller user base on Power Wagons so less long-term data, but well-reviewed.\n\nFor a moderate-trail Power Wagon build, the Falken AT3W is the value sweet spot. For overland, the Toyo AT III's larger diameter is worth the premium. For rock-crawler builds, skip the AT and go straight to 37\" MTs (see [[pw-tires-mt]]).\n\nThe Power Wagon weighs ~6,900 lb and has a front GAWR around 4,500 lb, so tire load rating is not optional. Every size above must be **LT-metric, Load Range C or higher** \u2014 never run a P-metric (passenger) tire on this truck. The factory DuraTrac is Load Range C; matching or exceeding it keeps you inside the door-jamb load spec.\n\n- **Factory 33s (LT265/70R17, ~31.6\" measured):** drop-in, no changes.\n- **285/70R17 (~32.7\"):** clears the factory fenders at full lock and full stuff with no trimming on a stock-height or leveled truck. This is the safe one-up size.\n- **295/70R17 (~34.3\"):** the largest that fits factory wheels and fenders without trimming. Watch the front inner sidewall at full lock \u2014 on a leveled truck you may catch the lower control arm. A 1.25\" hub-centric spacer ([[pw-wheel-spacers]]) cures it.\n- **35s and up:** need a real lift ([[pw-lift-mid-3in]]) plus spacers, and you cross the re-gear line below.\n\nSet cold pressure by load, not by the 80 psi on the sidewall. For an unloaded-to-lightly-loaded Power Wagon on 285s, most owners run ~45-50 psi front / 40-45 psi rear and verify with a chalk-line test across the tread. Always torque to 140 lb-ft on the M14x1.5 lugs and re-torque after the first 50 miles.\n\nHonest answer: not for any AT size on this list. The Power Wagon's factory 4.10 axle ratio (with the 33\" factory tire) tolerates a one-up to 285/70R17 or even the 34.3\" Toyo without a meaningful drivability hit \u2014 you lose a sliver of low-end and the speedo reads ~3% slow. The 6.4L Hemi has the torque to pull a 34\" tire on 4.10s all day.\n\nYou cross into re-gear territory at **37s**, where the effective ratio drops enough to hurt towing and trans temps. If a 37\" build is the goal, plan the tire and the 4.56 regear together (see [[pw-regear-456]]) rather than running big tires on stock gears and cooking the transmission on grades. For everything on this AT list, keep your money and stay on the factory gears.","pros":["Direct factory-size swap or one-up \u2014 no fender trimming, no re-gear","Better wet/snow than the factory DuraTrac","50-60k mile typical life on Power Wagon weight","Quieter highway noise than the factory DuraTrac (true of all four)"],"cons":["KO2 is loud above 65 mph","Largest AT (Toyo 34.3\") still needs LT load range \u2014 heavier than factory","No tire is as light-weight friendly as a passenger-rated tire (Power Wagon is heavy, you need LT-rated)","Factory wheel offset (-25mm) limits tire width \u2014 wider than 285 needs spacers or new wheels"]},{"id":"pw-tires-mt","title":"Mud-Terrain Tires for Power Wagon (KM3 / Patagonia M/T / Open Country M/T)","vehicle":"power-wagon","category":"wheels_tires","subcategory":"tires_mt","type":"part","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":3},"cost_usd":{"low":1800,"high":3200},"tools_required":["torque wrench","tire-pressure gauge","fender trim tools if going 37","tape measure"],"skills_required":["fender trimming if going 37+"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["2014-2018","2019-2024","2025+"],"tags":["tires","mud-terrain","mt","rock-crawl","tire-fitment","premium"],"parts":[{"name":"BFGoodrich Mud-Terrain T/A KM3 LT315/70R17 (34.4\")","part_number":"BFG-KM3-315-70-17","vendor":"BFGoodrich","price_estimate_usd":420},{"name":"BFGoodrich KM3 LT37x12.50R17","vendor":"BFGoodrich","price_estimate_usd":530},{"name":"Milestar Patagonia M/T LT315/70R17","vendor":"Milestar","price_estimate_usd":310},{"name":"Toyo Open Country M/T LT37x12.50R17","vendor":"Toyo","price_estimate_usd":590},{"name":"Nitto Trail Grappler LT37x12.50R17","vendor":"Nitto","price_estimate_usd":560},{"name":"AEV ProCal module (speedometer / tire size recalibration)","vendor":"American Expedition Vehicles","price_estimate_usd":200}],"citations":[{"title":"BFGoodrich Mud-Terrain T/A KM3 \u2014 LT-metric and flotation specs","url":"https://www.bfgoodrichtires.com/"},{"title":"Toyo Open Country M/T \u2014 product specs","url":"https://www.toyotires.com/"},{"title":"Nitto Trail Grappler M/T \u2014 product specs","url":"https://www.nittotire.com/"},{"title":"Ram Trucks \u2014 2500 Power Wagon specifications","url":"https://www.ramtrucks.com/ram-2500/specs.power-wagon.html"}],"alternatives":["pw-tires-at","pw-wheels","pw-regear-456"],"summary":"For a Power Wagon you actually wheel hard, 35\" or 37\" mud-terrain tires are the right answer. **35s** fit a 3\" lift on factory wheels with spacers and leave the factory **4.10** gears usable. **37s** need **4-5\" of lift**, +25mm spacers or aftermarket wheels, fender trimming, and a **4.56 regear** \u2014 the factory 4.10s feel flat on 37s and run the transmission hot on grades. Pick the path before you buy the tires, because the tire is the cheapest part of the 37\" decision.","description":"The 2014 and later Power Wagon ships with **LT285/70R17 (~33\")** tires on **4.10 gears** behind a **6.4L Hemi** (410 hp / 429 lb-ft), at a curb weight near **6,900 lb** and a **GVWR of 8,510 lb** on 2019+ trucks. That combination is well matched from the factory for daily driving and light trail work.\n\nOne correction worth stating plainly, because it circulates in forum threads: **the 2014+ Power Wagon is 6.4L Hemi only** \u2014 the 5.7L belongs to the 2005-2013 trucks \u2014 and **4.30 is not an available ratio** in these axles. The AAM 9.25\" front and AAM 11.5\" rear share ratios from a short list; the meaningful regear targets are **4.56** and **4.88**.\n\nMost Power Wagon owners overbuy here. Run this filter before spending.\n\n**Choose 35s (LT315/70R17, ~34.4\") if** you mix highway miles with moderate-to-hard trail, tow occasionally, or want to keep factory gears and wheels. This size clears a **3\" lift** on factory wheels with **1.5\" spacers**, or on factory wheels alone if you accept a rub at full articulation. Regear is optional \u2014 the 6.4 pulls 34.4s without complaint in normal driving. BFG KM3 in LT315 is the reference tire; the Milestar Patagonia M/T is the budget pick at meaningfully less money and meaningfully less tread life.\n\n**Choose 37s (LT37x12.50R17) only if** rock crawling or deep technical terrain is the actual use case *and* you are committed to the full supporting build. On factory 4.10 gears, 37s make the truck feel gutless, the 6-speed hunts on grades, and transmission temperature climbs while towing. The regear is not a later upgrade \u2014 it is part of the tire.\n\n1. **4-5\" of lift** \u2014 see the [4\" mid-lift guide](/db/power-wagon/pw-mid-lift/) for the geometry work (track bars, caster, brake lines) that a coil-sprung Power Wagon requires, or the [AEV DualSport system](/db/power-wagon/pw-lift-aev-dualsport/) for a matched package.\n2. **Wheel offset or spacers** \u2014 either **+25mm spacers** on factory wheels ([wheel spacer guide](/db/power-wagon/pw-wheel-spacers/)) or aftermarket **17x9 wheels at -12mm to 0mm offset**. Confirm against the [Power Wagon tire fitment guide](/db/power-wagon/power-wagon-tire-fitment/) rather than a forum photo.\n3. **Fender trimming** \u2014 inner liner trim is mandatory; the outer pinch weld often needs attention too. There is no configuration that avoids this.\n4. **Regear to 4.56**, both axles \u2014 see [the regear guide](/db/power-wagon/pw-regear-456/). Budget **$1,200-1,800** done, and do both axles at once; mismatched ratios destroy the transfer case.\n5. **Speedometer recalibration.** Going 33\" to 37\" is roughly a **12% error** \u2014 an indicated 60 mph is about 67 mph actual, and the odometer under-counts by the same margin, which distorts every service interval. An **AEV ProCal** or equivalent tire-size programmer (**~$200**) corrects it. This also matters legally; a 12% speedometer error is not a defense.\n6. **Winch capacity re-check.** The factory Warn 12,000 lb winch was rated against a stock truck. Add 37s, a steel bumper, and gear, and the effective margin shrinks \u2014 see the [factory recovery points guide](/db/power-wagon/recovery-points-and-front-bumper/) for why single-line pull drops as the drum fills.\n\nThe Power Wagon's **front GAWR is 6,000 lb**, which means **each front tire must be rated for at least 3,000 lb**. Shop by **load index and load range**, not by size. A 37\" tire in a light-duty flotation casing exists, sells cheaply, and is under-rated for this truck.\n\n- **Load Range E** LT casings are the safe default on a 3/4-ton\n- **Load Range D** is acceptable in some 37\" sizes \u2014 verify the published single-tire rating meets 3,000 lb before buying\n- Avoid unbranded 37s entirely. A stock Power Wagon at ~6,900 lb chews cheap sidewalls, and a sidewall failure on a heavy truck at highway speed is not a flat tire, it is an incident\n\nSet cold pressure conservatively and by load, not by the door placard number (which was written for the 33\" stock tire). Torque lugs to **140 lb-ft** and re-check after 50 miles \u2014 new wheels and spacers both settle.\n\n**BFGoodrich KM3** \u2014 the dominant Power Wagon choice in both 35 and 37. Proven sidewall construction, unusually good wet-road manners for a mud-terrain, and the tread survives rock. About **$420** in LT315/70R17 and **$530** in 37x12.50R17. This is the default recommendation.\n\n**Toyo Open Country M/T** \u2014 **~$590** in 37. Aggressive shoulder lugs, excellent in mud and soft terrain, noticeably louder on highway than the KM3 and generally shorter-lived on pavement.\n\n**Nitto Trail Grappler M/T** \u2014 **~$560** in 37. The quietest of the three on highway for a genuine M/T, with slightly less bite in deep mud. The right pick if the truck sees more pavement than the other two owners' trucks do.\n\n**Milestar Patagonia M/T** \u2014 **~$310** in LT315/70R17. Real value, real capability, and real tradeoffs: shorter tread life and less refined road manners. Sensible on a truck that gets new tires often anyway.\n\n**Weight matters more than the spec sheet suggests.** A stock 285/70R17 DuraTrac is roughly **57 lb**. A 315/70R17 KM3 is around **68 lb**; a 37x12.50R17 KM3 is around **82-88 lb**. Adding ~30 lb of unsprung rotating mass per corner is why the regear and better shocks stop being optional \u2014 see the [premium reservoir shock guide](/db/power-wagon/pw-shocks-premium/).\n\n| Line item | Typical cost |\n|---|---|\n| Five 37\" KM3 (LT37x12.50R17, including spare) | ~$2,650 |\n| Mount, balance, road-force | ~$200 |\n| 4-5\" lift system | $2,500-4,000 |\n| 4.56 regear, both axles | $1,200-1,800 |\n| Wheels or +25mm spacers | $360-2,000 |\n| Speedometer recalibration module | ~$200 |\n| Fender trim | shop time only |\n| Four-wheel alignment | $150-250 |\n| **All-in** | **$7,260-11,100** |\n\nThat is why the bottom-line advice holds: **37s are a $7-11k commitment** once the supporting mods are counted, and they only earn it on a truck that genuinely crawls. A **35\" KM3 setup on a 3\" lift** ([3\" mid-lift](/db/power-wagon/pw-lift-mid-3in/)) delivers most of the off-road capability for roughly a third of the money.","pros":["35s clear a 3\" lift with no regear and no trimming beyond a tap of the inner liner","37s on a properly regeared Power Wagon make it one of the most capable near-stock trucks on the market","KM3 sidewall construction is proven against Power Wagon weight","Step-change in rock traction over an all-terrain"],"cons":["37s make the regear mandatory ($1,200-1,800 done)","37s make fender trimming mandatory \u2014 no configuration avoids it","37s cost roughly 3-4 mpg","37s push the factory winch's effective margin toward marginal","Mud-terrains are loud on highway, without exception","12% speedometer and odometer error until recalibrated"]},{"id":"pw-wheel-spacers","title":"Wheel Spacers for Power Wagon (BORA 1.25\" / Spidertrax 1.5\" Hub-Centric)","vehicle":"power-wagon","category":"wheels_tires","subcategory":"wheels","type":"part","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":3},"cost_usd":{"low":280,"high":560},"tools_required":["torque wrench","anti-seize","extension","wire brush","jack stands rated to 3+ tons"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2014-2018","2019-2024","2025+"],"tags":["spacer","wheels","wheel-spacers","budget","hub-centric"],"parts":[{"name":"BORA 1.25\" Hub-Centric Spacer 8x6.5 (set of 4)","part_number":"BORA-125-8x65","vendor":"BORA Wheel Spacers","price_estimate_usd":360},{"name":"Spidertrax 1.5\" Hub-Centric 8x6.5 (set of 4)","vendor":"Spidertrax","price_estimate_usd":480},{"name":"Motorsport-Tech 1.25\" Hub-Centric (set of 4)","vendor":"Motorsport-Tech","price_estimate_usd":320}],"citations":[{"title":"BORA Wheel Spacers \u2014 hub-centric 8x6.5 application","url":"https://www.borawheelspacers.com/"},{"title":"Spidertrax \u2014 hub-centric wheel spacer specs","url":"https://www.spidertrax.com/"},{"title":"Ram 2500 wheel specs \u2014 bolt pattern, hub bore, factory offset","url":"https://www.wheel-size.com/size/ram/2500/2022/"}],"alternatives":["pw-wheels","power-wagon-tire-fitment","pw-beadlock-wheels"],"summary":"The cheapest way to clear 35s on a lifted Power Wagon while keeping the factory wheels: a 1.25\" or 1.5\" hub-centric spacer per corner, $280-$560 for a set of four, about an hour to install. Hub-centric is not a preference on a 7,000-pound truck \u2014 lug-centric spacers work loose and crack. Everything below assumes hub-centric.","description":"The Power Wagon's factory wheel is a 17x8 on the 8x165.1 (8x6.5) bolt pattern, with a hub bore of about 121.3 mm and a deep positive offset in the neighborhood of +45 mm. That high positive offset is why spacers come up at all: it tucks the wheel well inboard toward the suspension, which is fine around the factory 285/70R17 but gets tight fast as the tire gets wider. Go to a 35 and the inner sidewall starts finding the front sway bar end link and the inner fender liner at full lock.\n\nTwo ways to fix it:\n\n1. Wheel spacers \u2014 $280-$560 for a set of four, roughly an hour of work, keeps the factory wheels (which are genuinely good castings sized around a real 33-inch tire). This guide.\n2. Aftermarket wheels with a wider face and less positive offset \u2014 $1,200-$2,000. See the [wheels guide](/db/power-wagon/pw-wheels/).\n\nFor most owners running 35s on a 3\"-class lift, spacers are the right answer. You keep the factory look, you save over $1,000, and the install is inside a beginner's skill range.\n\nA spacer does not change your wheel. It changes the effective offset by exactly its thickness, and understanding that makes the sizing decision obvious rather than a guess.\n\nEffective offset = factory offset \u2212 spacer thickness. A 1.25-inch spacer is 31.75 mm, so a factory wheel near +45 mm behaves like roughly +13 mm once spaced. A 1.5-inch spacer is 38.1 mm, taking the same wheel to roughly +7 mm. In practical terms each corner moves outboard by the spacer thickness, so a 1.25-inch spacer at all four corners widens overall track by about 2.5 inches.\n\nThat extra track is doing two useful things. It moves the inner sidewall away from the sway bar link and inner liner, which is what buys the tire clearance. And it slightly widens the stance, which marginally improves lateral stability on a tall truck. It also does two less-useful things: it pushes the tire closer to (or past) the fender flare, which means spray, and it increases the scrub radius, which puts a little more effort into the steering and a little more load into the wheel bearing.\n\nMatch the spacer to the tire and lift rather than buying the biggest number:\n\n- 1.25\" (BORA): 33-34\" tires on a leveling kit, or 35s on a 3\"-class lift with factory wheels. This is the common Power Wagon size and the one to default to.\n- 1.5\" (Spidertrax): 37s on factory wheels with a 4-5\" lift, or anyone who wants the tire flush with the flare. At this width you are within a few hundred dollars of aftermarket wheels with the correct offset built in \u2014 price both before committing.\n- Do not stack thin spacers to reach a thickness. One correctly-specified hub-centric spacer per corner.\n\nCheck the [tire fitment guide](/db/power-wagon/power-wagon-tire-fitment/) for which tire size you are actually solving for before ordering a thickness.\n\nLug-centric spacers \u2014 where the lug studs locate the spacer \u2014 are common and cheap, often under $100 a set. On a 7,000-pound truck they are a bad bet. They permit micro-movement under load, the movement works the lug threads, and the failure mode is a cracked spacer or a spacer backing off at highway speed.\n\nA hub-centric spacer has a precision-machined center bore that seats on the hub itself; the lugs only clamp. BORA and Spidertrax are the two reference brands here, both machining the bore to the Ram 2500's 121.3 mm hub register and pressing in their own studs. Verify the bore spec on the listing before buying \u2014 a spacer advertised for \"Dodge 8-lug\" with a generic oversized bore is lug-centric in practice no matter what the title says.\n\nBudget about an hour for all four corners with hand tools.\n\n1. Break the lug nuts loose on the ground, then jack at the frame and set the truck on stands rated for at least 3,500 lb per corner. Never work under a 7,000-lb truck on a bottle jack alone.\n2. Pull the wheel and wire-brush the hub face clean. Rust or grit between hub and spacer defeats the hub-centric fit and lets the assembly walk.\n3. Anti-seize the factory studs lightly, slide the spacer flush onto the hub \u2014 it should seat on the machined center bore, not float on the studs \u2014 and start the spacer's inner nuts by hand.\n4. Torque the spacer's inner nuts to the spacer manufacturer's specification in a star pattern. Confirm the spacer sits dead flush with no gap anywhere around the circumference.\n5. Mount the wheel on the spacer's pressed-in studs, hand-start every nut, then torque in a star pattern to the lug torque value in your owner's manual for your model year.\n6. Re-torque both the spacer nuts and the wheel nuts after 100 miles, and again after the first off-road trip. This is the step that prevents the failure people blame on spacers generally.\n\nSpacers get a bad reputation that is 90% attributable to cheap lug-centric parts and skipped re-torques. The remaining real considerations:\n\nThread engagement. The factory studs now carry only the spacer, and the wheel rides on the spacer's studs. That is correct and safe with a quality part, but it means stud quality is now a purchased item rather than a factory one. Confirm the spacer uses forged studs at the correct thread pitch for your truck.\n\nBearing load. Moving the tire outboard lengthens the moment arm on the wheel bearing. At 1.25-1.5 inches on a bearing sized for a 3/4-ton truck, this is a small effect, not a scary one \u2014 but it is not zero, and it argues against going thicker than you need.\n\nFender coverage and law. Several states and most Canadian provinces require the tire to be covered by the fender or a flare. Pushing a 35 out 1.5 inches on a truck without flares can put you outside that, and it will throw road spray onto the door glass regardless.\n\nInspection habit. Add a quick check of the spacer nuts to whatever you already do before a trip. It takes two minutes and closes the only realistic failure path.","pros":["The cheapest path to 35s on factory wheels \u2014 a fraction of an aftermarket wheel set","About an hour of work with hand tools","Keeps the factory 17x8 wheels, which are well matched to a real off-road sidewall","Hub-centric BORA and Spidertrax parts have a long track record on heavy 3/4-tons"],"cons":["Adds roughly 5-8 lb of unsprung weight per corner","Hub-centric only \u2014 lug-centric parts have no place on this truck","Slightly heavier steering and marginally more wheel bearing load from the increased scrub radius","Re-torque after 100 miles is mandatory, and skipping it is the actual cause of most spacer failures"]},{"id":"pw-wheels","title":"Wheel Upgrade for the Power Wagon \u2014 17\", 18\" and 20\" Options","vehicle":"power-wagon","category":"wheels_tires","type":"part","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":1},"cost_usd":{"low":620,"high":1440},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","torque wrench","lug wrench or impact gun","wire brush"],"skills_required":["wheel removal and installation"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2016-2024"],"tags":["wheels","upgrade","8x6.5","load-rating"],"parts":[{"name":"Method Race Wheels 308 17x8.5 / 20x9 (8x165.1)","vendor":"Method Race Wheels","price_estimate_usd":185},{"name":"Fuel Maverick D538 20x9 (8x165.1)","vendor":"Fuel Off-Road","price_estimate_usd":175},{"name":"Black Rhino Armory 20x9.5 (8x165.1)","vendor":"Black Rhino Wheels","price_estimate_usd":165},{"name":"American Force Independence 20x14 (8x165.1)","vendor":"American Force","price_estimate_usd":350}],"citations":[{"title":"Method Race Wheels 308 NV Product Page","url":"https://www.methodracewheels.com/collections/8-lug-truck"},{"title":"Wheel Load Rating Guide for 3/4-Ton Trucks","url":"https://www.discounttire.com/learn/wheel-load-rating"}],"alternatives":["pw-budget-lift","pw-beadlock-wheels"],"summary":"The Power Wagon's 8x165.1 (8x6.5\") bolt pattern is shared with most 3/4-ton and 1-ton RAM trucks, giving you a wide selection \u2014 but load rating is the specification that matters most here, and many aftermarket wheels skip the engineering that a truck with an 8,510 lb GVWR demands.","description":"Load rating is the non-negotiable spec for the Power Wagon. GVWR of 8,510 lbs divided by four wheels = 2,127 lbs per wheel minimum. The industry standard recommendation for a margin of safety puts the target at 3,000\u20133,500 lbs per wheel minimum for a truck in this weight class, especially under payload or towing. Many wheels marketed for trucks carry load ratings of 2,500 lbs that are technically sufficient but leave no margin for dynamic loads (trail impacts, towing with tongue weight, etc.). Before finalizing any wheel purchase, look up the load rating in the manufacturer's spec sheet \u2014 if it isn't published, that's a reason to look elsewhere.\n\nOffset matters on the Power Wagon. The factory wheels run a +18mm offset; staying in the +10 to +25mm range keeps the wheel center close enough to factory geometry that you won't need [wheel spacers](/db/power-wagon/pw-wheel-spacers/) and won't strain the wheel bearings at the extended scrub radius of an aggressive negative-offset setup. Wide-stance wheels (deep negative offset, like 20x14) push the tread out past the fender, which requires flares or trimming \u2014 not the call for a trail-daily truck.\n\nThe factory Power Wagon wheel is a 17x8 \u2014 not an 18 or 20 like other Ram 2500 trims \u2014 because Ram sized it around the 33\" Goodyear Wrangler DuraTrac (LT285/70R17) and the sidewall that tire needs off-road. That logic still applies when you upgrade. Staying at 17\" with a 35\" tire (LT315/70R17 territory \u2014 see the [tire fitment guide](/db/power-wagon/power-wagon-tire-fitment/)) preserves the deepest sidewall, airs down the best, and has the widest selection of genuinely trail-focused tires. Method's 308 in 17x8.5 is the pick if the truck sees rocks.\n\n20x9 is the most popular street-side upgrade. With a 35\"\u201337\" tire it keeps an acceptable sidewall while opening up more all-terrain and hybrid tire selection, and it fills the wheel wells visually. Method 308 in 20x9 ($185/wheel) publishes clear load ratings and has a strong track record on 3/4-ton builds. Fuel Maverick D538 ($175) is a styling-forward choice with adequate ratings. Black Rhino Armory ($165) is the value end of the quality tier. American Force ($350) is for owners prioritizing appearance \u2014 billet construction, high polish, and the least sidewall of the bunch. 18\" splits the difference and is a legitimate middle path, though tire selection in LT sizes is thinner than at 17 or 20.\n\nWhat you give up moving from 17 to 20: roughly two inches of sidewall per side at the same overall tire diameter. That's two inches less air cushion between rim and rock, less bulge when aired down, and a harsher ride on washboard. For a truck that wheels hard enough to air down regularly, that trade runs the wrong direction \u2014 which is also the case for going further into [beadlock territory](/db/power-wagon/pw-beadlock-wheels/) at 17\".\n\nA cast 20\" wheel in this class runs 38\u201345 lbs bare versus roughly 30\u201334 lbs for the factory 17 \u2014 add a 37\" tire and you can put 15+ lbs of unsprung, rotating mass at each corner over stock. On a truck this heavy the shocks pay the bill: more unsprung mass means more heat in the dampers on washboard and faster fade on long corrugated roads. It also lengthens braking distance slightly and dulls throttle response, which stacks with the effects covered in the [tire guide](/db/power-wagon/pw-tires-mt/). Check the published wheel weight next to the load rating; among wheels with equal ratings, buy the lighter one.","steps":["Break lug nuts loose before jacking (Ram 2500 lug torque is high \u2014 a breaker bar works, or an impact gun).","Raise the truck and support on jack stands.","Remove factory wheels and inspect hub face and hub bore on the new wheels for correct fitment.","Clean the hub mounting surface with a wire brush to remove any corrosion \u2014 proper hub centering prevents vibration.","Mount new wheels and hand-thread all eight lug nuts before torquing any.","Torque lug nuts in a star pattern to the Ram 2500 spec for your year (check the owner's manual \u2014 spec varies by year and stud size).","Re-torque after the first 50\u2013100 miles of driving."],"pros":["8x165.1 bolt pattern has wide selection across quality off-road wheel brands","17\" options preserve the sidewall depth the truck was engineered around","Method and Fuel publish clear load ratings \u2014 verifiable before purchase","Wheel upgrade pairs naturally with a tire upsizing for a single combined fitment decision"],"cons":["Load rating verification is mandatory \u2014 not all marketed truck wheels are rated for the Power Wagon's GVWR","20\" wheels give up roughly two inches of sidewall versus 17\" at the same tire diameter","Heavier aftermarket wheels add unsprung mass that works the shocks harder on washboard","Hub-centric rings may be needed if aftermarket wheel bore diameter differs from factory hub \u2014 check before mounting"]}];